More precisely, there exists a critical coupling Kc > 0 such that1
(1) in the subcritical regime K < Kc, qu is the unique minimizer of free energy FK;
(2) in the critical regime K = Kc, qu remains a minimizer of FK (though a priori not necessarily
unique);
(3) in the supercritical regime K > Kc, there exists a nonuniform minimizer q ̸= qu of FK.
The phase transition is closely related to the stability of the uniform state. We therefore define the
linear stability threshold K# as the value at which the second variation of FK at qu loses positivity
