1.3. Comments on the proof. The key ingredient to the proof of Theorem 1.1 is a sharp func-
tional inequality relating the entropy to the ˙H−1/2 seminorm (see Proposition 2.2 below), which
allows to prove coercivity of the free energy FK. This inequality is a consequence—or more pre-
cisely, the dual form—of what is known in the harmonic analysis literature as the (constrained)
Lebedev–Milin inequality, presented in Proposition 1.5 below. The original5 inequality of Lebedev
and Milin [LM65] corresponds to the n = 0 case and the constrained n = 1 version was later shown
by Osgood et al. [OPS88]. Subsequently, Widom [Wid88] elegantly observed that the inequality
of [OPS88] is the first in a family of constrained inequalities imposing higher-order vanishing of the
Fourier coefficients, which are a straightforward consequence of the Szeg¨o limit theorem [GS84].
We refer to [CG23] and references therein for further discussion, as well as generalizations.
