1.5. Implications for the gradient flow. Since the McKean–Vlasov equation (1.3) is the 2-
Wasserstein gradient flow of FK, our static results for the free energy have direct consequences for
the long-time behavior of the dynamics. For the purposes of this discussion, we will continue to use
the normalization of Theorem 1.1, except when specializing to our motivating models. We further
assume that ∂θW ∈Lp for some p > 1, which ensures global well-posedness of solutions for initial
data in P(T) as well as instantaneous C∞smoothing, thereby avoiding any regularity issues.
By standard compactness arguments, it is easy to show that any solution qt of (1.3) converges
weakly to a critical point q∞of FK as t →∞. The key question is what can be said about the
selected equilibrium q∞(e.g., is it the global minimizer?) and the rate of convergence. Uniqueness
of critical points obviously answers the former question, which is guaranteed for K ≤1/2 by
Theorem 1.1. In the absence of uniqueness, the answer is more subtle and a priori depends on the
initial data.
