Despite its simplicity, this result offers a really important perspective on lattice gauge theory:
the combinatorial aspects of Wilson loop expectations do not depend on the action or the cou-
pling. This is the most important conceptual input of the paper, as it separates the dependence
on the plaquette action from the combinatorics of Wilson observables. All subsequent results
of the paper are manifestations of the combinatorial structure of the coefficients c
WΛ,L(α) at
fixed plaquette decoration α. Let us insist on the fact that, albeit stated for sublattices of Zd,
Theorem 1.1 holds for any two-dimensional CW-complex, e.g. a topological map, or the trian-
gulation of an arbitrary manifold of any dimension d ≥2 and any set of loops in this complex.
The arguments do not rely specifically on the fact that we work on a sublattice of Zd, but we
focus in this case because it is rather standard in lattice gauge theory.
