Theorem 1.4 may be compared with the worldsheet/background decompositions considered
in the spin-foam literature on confinement, notably by Cherrington and collaborators [CCK07].
There, Wilson loop observables are organized as sums over worldsheets interacting with vacuum
spin-foam backgrounds, typically with non-trivial degeneracy issues. In contrast, the present
defect-ratio formula is an exact identity at finite N: the observable is represented as the ratio of
two partition functions in the same local channel model, differing only through the finitely many
edge factors supported on the defect set D(L). This avoids any non-canonical decomposition of
charged configurations into background plus spanning surface, while retaining a fully local dual
description.
Together, Theorems 1.3 and 1.4 answer a question of Cao–Park–Sheffield [CPS25, §7, (16)]:
compared to the surface-sum expansion, the spin-foam representation captures the local proper-
ties of lattice Yang–Mills, which is not possible in the topological expansion. Such locality might
be naturally relevant for infinite-volume limits or scaling limits, and it would be interesting to
explore these directions.
1.5
Master loop equation
Master loop equations occupy a central place in lattice Yang–Mills theory because they
are the basic exact recursions satisfied by Wilson observables. In the Wilson-action setting,
such equations lie behind the strong-coupling and large-N analyses of Chatterjee and Jafarov
[Cha19a, Jaf16], and were recently rederived at finite N for SO(N), U(N) and SU(N) by Shen–
Smith–Zhu [SSZ24] through a Langevin-dynamic approach. These results show that the Wilson
action admits a remarkably rigid cut-and-join/deformation recursion directly at the level of
Wilson loop expectations. From that point of view, the classical finite-N master loop equation
is already one of the fundamental exact identities of the subject.
Theorem 1.1 reveals these equations as instances of a broader topological and algebraic
recursion. Rather than starting from a particular action and working directly with Wilson ex-
pectations, we first pass to the coefficient side of the state-sum expansion and prove a universal
integration-by-parts identity for the topological coefficients c
