This point of view is important for two reasons. First, it shows that the mechanism behind
the master loop equation is more universal than the classical Wilson-action formulas suggest:
what is truly fundamental is a hybrid position/Fourier recursion, in which the loop sector stays
geometric while the plaquette sector is transferred to representation space. Second, it opens
the door to non-Wilson specializations. For the Wilson action, the local recoupling operator
collapses after resummation to the usual deformation terms; for the heat kernel action, by
contrast, it remains visible as an explicit spectral transfer operator on the active plaquette
label. In this sense, Theorem 1.5 should be viewed as a common finite-N ancestor of the usual
lattice master loop equations and of the more spectral Makeenko–Migdal-type identities that
arise in two-dimensional Yang–Mills [SSZ25a].
1.6
Other classical groups and Large-N limit
Although this paper is deliberately focused on the unitary group U(N), the whole formalism
can be adapted to any compact classical group: SU(N), SO(N), Sp(N).
Indeed, the mixed
Schur–Weyl duality stated for U(N) also holds for SU(N) (it was observed and used by Magee
[Mag22, Mag25] in a similar context for instance), and there are other versions of Schur–Weyl
duality for orthogonal and symplectic groups. We will defer to Appendix A for a guideline on
how to adapt our formalism to these other groups.
Another part of the theory has been left untouched in the present paper, which is the large-N
regime. As the formalism imported from the two-dimensional setting in this paper has led to
the construction of the master field in the plane and in compact surfaces [L´ev17, DN20, DL25,
Dah26], it is natural to wonder whether the same holds in higher-dimensional lattices. We will
apply the finite-N results of the present universal formalism in a companion paper [Lem26a],
which will also rely on recent advances in asymptotic spectral theory of compact classical groups
[LM25a], obtained by the author together with Ma¨ıda.
1.7
Organization of the paper
First, we will develop in Section 2 the refined Weingarten construction of spanning surfaces
from integrals of products of rational characters of U(N), leading to the proof of Theorem 1.2.
Second, we will present in Section 3 the model of local channels based on the dual incidence
graph, and prove Theorems 1.3 and 1.4. Third, we will prove the master loop equation (The-
orem 1.5) in Section 4.
Finally, we will recover existing results about the Wilson action in
Section 5, and explain in Appendix A how to adapt our whole framework to other classical
