12
The relation between the standard and braided BV formalisms discussed at the beginning of this
section can now be clarified through the lens of A∞-algebras. An A∞-structure has no symmetries
and so is agnostic to the braiding of the monoidal category. Given an A∞-algebra in the category R,
braided graded antisymmetrization of its brackets results in a braided L∞-structure. On the other
hand, this same A∞-structure defines an A∞-algebra in the category V under the forgetful functor
R −→V which forgets the UF a-module structures, and standard graded antisymmetrization of its
brackets now leads to an ordinary L∞-structure. In fact, all elements of homological perturbation
theory apply to A∞-algebras, and (braided) quantum field theory can be developed equally well
in the language of A∞-structures by replacing the (braided) symmetric algebra Sym(V [2]) above
with the free unital tensor algebra T(V [2]) = L
n⩾0 (V [2])⊗n over V [2].
