When we restrict the pseudometric to the linear sub-
space corresponding to a single complex energy with-
out its complex conjugate partner, the result is zero
(diagonal blocks in Eq. (76)). Consequently, the be-
havior of a degeneracy at a complex energy under
perturbation is similar to the non-symmetric case of
Sec. III as it corresponds to the trivial zero pseudo-
metric [70]. Therefore, we will focus our attention
on the degeneracies at real energies.
Without loss of generality, we assume that other
energy levels have been projected out and focus
on the single eigenvalue.
We assume the pseudo-
metric to be restricted to the corresponding sub-
space and choose the basis in which it takes the
simple form (66). Since the actual value of the eigen-
value is not important, we set it to be zero and
consider traceless pseudo-Hermitian matrices.
For
the above-mentioned choice of the basis, we can as-
sociate the space of all traceless pseudo-Hermitian
matrices with the 𝑠𝑢(𝑝, 𝑞) Lie algebra:
𝜂𝑝,𝑞𝐻−𝐻†𝜂𝑝,𝑞= 0 ⇒𝜂𝑝,𝑞(𝑖𝐻) + (𝑖𝐻)†𝜂𝑝,𝑞= 0,
(77)
with (𝑖𝐻) ∈𝑠𝑢(𝑝, 𝑞).
From Theorem 1 it follows that every degeneracy
is uniquely characterized by the Jordan decomposi-
tion 𝐽𝑚1,𝑚2,...,𝑚𝑟and the set of signs (𝜀1, 𝜀2, . . . , 𝜀𝑟),
which together can be associated with a signed
Young diagram [71–73]. In comparison to a simple
Young diagram introduced in the previous section,
each row here also carries alternating pluses and mi-
nuses. The right-most sign corresponds to the sign 𝜀𝑖
in the decomposition of the pseudo-metric (71), and
the total numbers of pluses and minuses are consis-
tent with the signature of 𝜀𝑖𝑃𝑚𝑖. Since the diagram
is defined up to the permutation of the rows of the
same length, the unique type of degeneracy is deter-
mined by requiring the normalized ordering of the
signs: among the rows of equal length, those with
𝜀𝑖= +1 come first, followed by those with 𝜀𝑖= −1.
An example of such a signed Young diagram is
provided in Fig. 10.
