Since solving the linear programming problem is much more expensive than using
the IPFP algorithm proposed by Galichon and Salani´e (2022), it is also interesting
to compare their performances in our simulation runs. While we already know that
IPFP is much faster (and uses very little memory), it is only rigorously valid in
the “large market” limit when the number of individuals goes to infinity. Figure 5
shows that even with our population size of 1,000, IPFP performs remarkably well:
it yields equilibrium matching probabilities µxy that are very close to the mode of
the distribution of the Gurobi results for the finite-population case.
We also ran a set of simulations with only 200 individuals. The results are basically
similar as with 1,000 individuals, with more variation across runs as expected.
