3. History representation and approximation
For given local stress evolution σ : R →F, the hereditary law (1) defines a
convolution Volterra equation of the second kind in the local strain evolution
ϵ : R →E [75]. Necessary and sufficient stability conditions on the kernel
for the local problem, i. e., the problem of determining the strain evolution
corresponding to a given stress evolution, are summarized in [75, 67]. Such
stability conditions are found to be satisfied, for instance, by the Maxwell-
Wiechert model of Example 2.1.
A functional framework suitable for analysis and approximation may be
set forth as follows. We begin by noting that the elasticity tensor C can con-
veniently be enlisted to metrize strains and stresses, leading to the following
definitions.
Definition 3.1 (Local stress and strain spaces). We define the space M
of local strains as the linear space E metrized by C. We define the space
N of local stresses as the linear space F metrized by C−1. As Euclidean
spaces, N = M ∗and M = N∗and the Riesz mapping is given by Hooke’s
law σ(t) = Cϵ(t).
We adopt throughout a history representation [66, 67]. For a fixed mate-
rial point, the past local histories of strain and stress up to time t are the
functions
