confirmed later, and the phenomenologically introduced
magnetoelectric coupling term required to model optical
responses. Section III describes the real-time evolution
calculations performed using the iTEBD method. The
primary results of this work are presented in Section IV,
which showcases the linear and second-order nonlinear
optical conductivity spectra and demonstrates how these
spectra reflect the excitation structure of the quantum
spin system. In Sec. V, we present a brief summary and
discussions.
II.
MODEL
To study the light-matter coupling in a spin system,
we consider a one-dimensional (1D) quantum spin chain
model that incorporates a coupling with the external
electric field through the exchange striction mechanism
[19, 20]. The exchange striction arises from the electric
polarization that depends on the spin configuration, and
appears widely in multiferroic materials.
As a quantum spin chain, we consider the S = 1/2
XXZ model,
