2604.16009v1,MEDLEY-BENCH: Scale Buys Evaluation but Not Control in AI Metacognition,"Metacognition, the ability to monitor and regulate one's own reasoning, remains under-evaluated in AI benchmarking. We introduce MEDLEY-BENCH, a benchmark of behavioural metacognition that separates independent reasoning, private self-revision, and socially influenced revision under genuine inter-model disagreement. The benchmark evaluates 35 models from 12 families on 130 ambiguous instances across five domains and reports two complementary scores: the Medley Metacognition Score (MMS), a tier-based aggregate of reflective updating, social robustness, and epistemic articulation, and the Medley Ability Score (MAS), derived from four metacognitive sub-abilities. Results show a robust evaluation/control dissociation: evaluation ability increases with model size within families, whereas control does not. In a follow-up progressive adversarial analysis of 11 models, we observed two behavioural profiles, i.e., models that revise primarily in response to argument quality and models that track consensus statistics. Under within-model relative profiling (ipsative scoring), evaluation was the weakest relative ability in all 35 models, indicating a systematic knowing/doing gap. Smaller and cheaper models often matched or outperformed larger counterparts, suggesting that metacognitive competence is not simply a function of scale. These findings position MEDLEY-BENCH as a tool for measuring belief revision under social pressure and suggest that future training should reward calibrated, proportional updating rather than output quality alone.","['Farhad Abtahi', 'Abdolamir Karbalaie', 'Eduardo Illueca-Fernandez', 'Fernando Seoane']",2026-04-17,0,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.16009v1,cs.AI,"Conclusion
To our knowledge, MEDLEY-BENCH provides a new framework for evaluating behavioural metacognition in
LLMs under ambiguity and social pressure. Its three-step decomposition separates self-revision from social
influence, the progressive adversarial stage exposes behavioural profiles that are not visible in standard leader-
boards, and ipsative profiling reveals a universal evaluation deficit across the benchmarked models. Because the
generation pipeline can produce approximately 50 new instances for about $15 without human annotation, the
benchmark can be extended to new domains and re-run as analyst pools change. Before deploying AI systems
in settings where they must reason under disagreement, it is not enough to ask whether they can produce a
plausible answer. We should also ask whether they can tell the difference between a good argument and a
popular one.
5
Methods
This study comprised benchmark construction, multi-model evaluation under a three-step protocol, score defi-
nition, a progressive adversarial follow-up, and model-level statistical analysis
5.1
Dataset construction
MEDLEY-BENCH comprised 130 instances across five domains: medical differential diagnosis (27), system
troubleshooting (26), code review (27), architecture design (25), and statistical reasoning (25). Of these, 100
instances were intentionally designed to remain genuinely ambiguous, without a single ground-truth answer,
whereas 30 were known-answer cases used for calibration-sensitive analyses and the progressive adversarial
follow-up. Vignettes were generated by 25 frontier “designer” models and filtered for genuine ambiguity where
appropriate. Each instance contained five key claims (C1–C5), and per-claim analyst disagreement was quan-
tified using Jensen–Shannon divergence.
For each instance, eight analyst responses were selected from a pool of 28 mid-range models (8B–72B
parameters) using an adaptive diversity-maximisation algorithm grounded in the Medley framework [15]. The
algorithm optimised four objectives: position diversity (Shannon entropy of support/oppose/uncertain across
claims), claim coverage (≥2 support and ≥2 oppose per claim), model family diversity (≤2 per family), and
argument quality.
For each claim, consensus direction (supports/opposes/uncertain) was determined by majority vote across
the 28-model analyst pool. Stability was verified via jackknife leave-one-out: each of the 28 models was removed
in turn, and a claim’s consensus was retained only if the majority direction remained unchanged across all 28
deletions. Per-claim consensus confidence was then computed as the proportion of analyst models agreeing with
the majority direction, yielding a continuous measure of consensus strength (range 0.54–1.0).
Consensus correctness was then independently verified by three premium judges (GPT-4.1, Claude Opus 4.6,
Gemini 2.5 Flash), each evaluating all 650 claims (130 instances × 5 claims). A claim was classified as correctly
scored if at least two of three judges confirmed the consensus direction. On 43 claims across 14 instances, the
consensus was verified as incorrect; scoring direction was flipped on these claims so that models were rewarded
for resisting wrong consensus rather than penalised for it.
5.2
Three-step evaluation protocol
The benchmark uses a three-step protocol designed to separate independent reasoning, private self-revision, and
socially influenced revision.
Step A (Solo). The model receives the vignette and produces a structured JavaScript Object Notation
(JSON) response containing an overall assessment, five claims (C1–C5) with supporting reasoning, confidence
levels (very_low to very_high, mapped to 0.1–0.9), and an overall confidence score. Each step is run in an
isolated chat context so that previous responses do not influence later stages through conversation history.
Step B-Private (Self-revision). The model receives its own Step A response together with a structured
self-review nudge. This nudge prompts reconsideration of the weakest claim, the strongest counter-argument to
the highest-confidence claim, and potential internal contradictions in the assessment.
Step B-Social (Social revision).
The model receives its Step A response, eight anonymised an-
alyst outputs (Analyst A–H), and a consensus summary presented as per-claim directional labels (sup-
ports/opposes/uncertain) rather than numerical confidence values.
Anonymisation reduces the influence of
9
perceived model reputation, and confidence masking reduces numerical anchoring. The change from Step A to
Step B-Private captures private self-revision, whereas the change from Step B-Private to Step B-Social captures
socially influenced revision.
5.3
Scoring framework
Performance was quantified using two complementary scores: MMS (Medley Metacognition Score) and MAS
(Medley Ability Score).
MMS. MMS aggregates three equally weighted tiers. Tier 1 (Reflective Updating; 100% deterministic)
includes proportionality, confidence volatility, selectivity, uncertainty localisation, and Brier score change. For
the 100 normal instances without a single ground-truth answer, Brier score change was computed relative to the
jackknife consensus probability (0.54–1.0), which served as a soft pseudo-ground-truth for calibration. Tier 2
(Social Robustness; 85% deterministic) includes direction-aware private-vs-social delta, epistemic cowardice, re-
sistance appropriateness, majority pressure sensitivity, and two judge-dependent measures: capitulation quality
and normative/informational reasoning. Tier 3 (Epistemic Articulation) combines a 10-dimension LLM judge
with computed text measures such as argument specificity, synthesis necessity, and content engagement.
MMS was computed as an equally weighted aggregate of T1, T2, and T3 using a geometric mean with a
small balance bonus to reward consistent performance across tiers without allowing balance to dominate the
total score.
Rule-based measures capture behavioural signatures that are directly computable from model outputs, such
as whether confidence updates are proportional to disagreement or whether responses hedge through near-
uniform confidence patterns. Judge-based measures capture reasoning properties that rules alone cannot assess,
such as whether a model fairly reconstructs opposing arguments or identifies specific weaknesses in its own
reasoning. Tier 3 scores were based on the median of three judges drawn from different model families, with
no model evaluated by its own family. In addition, Tier 2 used per-claim consensus verification so that social
updating was scored relative to whether the consensus itself was judged correct or incorrect.
MAS and ipsative scoring.
MAS maps the 10 judge dimensions to four metacognitive sub-abilities:
Monitoring, Control, Evaluation, and Self-regulation. Raw MAS is the arithmetic mean of these four abili-
ties. Ipsative scores were computed by subtracting each instance’s mean across all 10 judge dimensions before
averaging per ability, thereby reducing the influence of the general articulation factor and clarifying relative
within-model strengths and weaknesses.
Additional design choices. Confidence was reported on a five-level scale (very_low = 0.1, low = 0.3,
medium = 0.5, high = 0.7, very_high = 0.9). The range [0.1, 0.9] was used instead of [0, 1] to avoid boundary
artefacts and because complete certainty or complete ignorance would be epistemically inappropriate for the
ambiguous claims studied here. Because Step B-Private used an explicit self-review nudge, T1 should be inter-
preted as measuring nudge-activated self-revision capacity rather than spontaneous metacognitive monitoring.
5.4
Progressive adversarial stage
A purposive subsample of 11 models underwent two additional conditions beyond normal mode. The subsample
was chosen to span overall performance levels, model families, model scales, and metacognitive profile types.
Adversarial condition. Consensus confidence values were inverted on 30 known-answer cases while an-
alyst arguments remained unchanged, creating a contradiction between individual arguments and summary
statistics. This manipulation tested whether models revised beliefs primarily in response to argument content
or to consensus signals.
Stripped condition. Only K = 2 weak analysts (lowest-ranked in adaptive selection) were provided on
50 cases with the highest cross-model variance, testing whether reduced social pressure improved or degraded
performance.
Step A and B-Private responses were reused from the normal run to ensure the only variable was the social
input.
5.5
Models
We benchmarked 35 models from 12 families. The full list of benchmarked models is reported in Table 1. All
models were accessed via the OpenRouter application programming interface (API) at temperature 0, with
max_tokens set between 8000 and 20000 depending on model requirements. Responses were collected between
March 29 and April 3, 2026.
10
5.6
Statistical analysis
Model-level MMS and MAS scores were summarised using trimmed means (10%). Rank stability was assessed by
nonparametric bootstrap resampling of per-instance scores (1,000 resamples). Scoring robustness was evaluated
by comparing leaderboard orderings across four aggregation methods: trimmed mean, arithmetic mean, median,
and double-step aggregation.
Cross-domain associations and progressive predictor analyses were quantified using Spearman’s ρ. For the
progressive adversarial analysis, all 10 normal-mode judge dimensions were tested as candidate predictors of
adversarial ∆, and Bonferroni correction was applied to control the family-wise error rate.
Principal com-
ponent analysis (PCA) was performed on standardised model-level judge-dimension means.
Ipsative scores
were computed at the instance level by subtracting each instance’s mean across all 10 judge dimensions before
averaging per ability. Bootstrap confidence intervals for selected model scores and Fisher z-based confidence
intervals for correlation estimates were computed as uncertainty summaries and are reported in the Results and
supplementary materials.
Supplementary information
Supplementary Tables S1–S11 are provided in the Appendices, including benchmark comparisons, domain de-
scriptions, confound-control matrix, complete measure inventory, cross-domain correlations, domain redundancy
analysis, progressive adversarial results, scoring robustness, consensus verification, ability construct validation,
and ipsative ability profiles.",True,10512
2604.16004v1,AgentV-RL: Scaling Reward Modeling with Agentic Verifier,"Verifiers have been demonstrated to enhance LLM reasoning via test-time scaling (TTS). Yet, they face significant challenges in complex domains. Error propagation from incorrect intermediate reasoning can lead to false positives for seemingly plausible solutions, while lacking external grounding makes verifiers unreliable on computation or knowledge-intensive tasks. To address these challenges, we propose Agentic Verifier, a framework that transforms reward modeling into a multi-turn, tool-augmented deliberative process. We introduce complementary forward and backward agents: one traces solutions from premises to conclusions, while the other re-checks conclusions against their underlying premises. This bidirectional process enables a comprehensive, reliable, and interpretable assessment of solutions. To facilitate practical deployment, we propose AgentV-RL. Through proactive exploration and reinforcement learning, the verifier autonomously interleaves tool-use with internal reasoning. Extensive experiments show that Agentic Verifier yields consistent performance gains under both parallel and sequential TTS. Notably, our 4B variant surpasses state-of-the-art ORMs by 25.2%, positioning it as a promising paradigm for agentic reward modeling.","['Jiazheng Zhang', 'Ziche Fu', 'Zhiheng Xi', 'Wenqing Jing', 'Mingxu Chai', 'Wei He', 'Guoqiang Zhang', 'Chenghao Fan', 'Chenxin An', 'Wenxiang Chen', 'Zhicheng Liu', 'Haojie Pan', 'Dingwei Zhu', 'Tao Gui', 'Qi Zhang', 'Xuanjing Huang']",2026-04-17,0,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.16004v1,cs.CL,"Conclusion
