Observe that a compound process (yt)t≥0 on G is a L´evy process, which is important for describing the generator of the
distributions µt of yt. L´evy processes on M correspond precisely with the projections by σ of K-bi-invariant L´evy processes
on G [13, Proposition 1.12]. This motivates the assumption that the steps (xn)n≥1 in Equation (3) are K-bi-invariant.
5
Compound processes on M have also been studied with respect to their asymptotic behaviour in distribution as t →∞in
[12]. As in the case of compact Lie groups, see [2, Proposition 5], the distribution of pt converges weakly to λM as t →∞
at an exponential rate, under mild assumptions on the initial distribution of (pt)t≥0. This tells us that information about the
distribution of the steps (xn)n≥1 dissipates as time passes and is relevant for questions on sampling of (yt)t≥0 or (pt)t≥0 in
the decompounding problem.
