III. FOURIER COEFFICIENT ESTIMATORS
A. Statistical Assumptions
A pressing question is in which sense information about (pt)t≥0 is to be assumed known. We will assume that a finite
number m ≥1 of independent observations of pt are available, for a fixed time t > 0. Note that independent sampling of this
sort does not necessarily assume that observations have been made from different independent trajectories. By the stationarity
and independence of the increments, it also covers the case when observations pt1, pt2, . . . , ptm have been made of a single
trajectory (pt)t≥0, as long as the timesteps tk −tk−1 for 2 ≤k ≤m remain constant, which may be seen as a more reasonable
assumption depending on the application. It would certainly be desired to develop ways of decompounding for more general
sampling assumptions, but this makes the problem significantly more difficult. While the L´evy–Khinchin formula provides a
relation between randomly sampled observations, it is ostensibly a situation where we are dealing with m observations of m
different distributions. Decompounding methods in this situation is admittedly a gap in research which would be interesting
to investigate, but for the purposes of this paper, we restrict attention to the following.
Assumption 1. Assume that there exists a sequence (pm)m≥1 of independent observations of the compound process pt observed
at a fixed time t > 0.
We acknowledge the unfortunate clash in notation between the observations (pm)m≥1 of pt for t fixed and the stochastic
process (pt)t≥0, but this should not pose an issue when read in context.
Secondly, assumptions on the distribution of the steps (xn)n≥1 are needed. We will assume that the distribution of xn is
sufficiently regular for it to admit a probability density fX ∈L2(G) with respect to the Haar measure λ. Necessary and
sufficient conditions for this to hold is a classically studied problem and can be read about in more detail for example in [11].
Since each xn is K-bi-invariant, it is easy to see that fX satisfies fX(xk) = fX(kx) = fX(x) for all k ∈K. With slight
abuse of notation, we therefore identify fX ∈L2
K(M) by setting fX(xK) = fX(x).
Assumption 2. The steps (xn)n≥1 are assumed to admit a K-bi-invariant probability density fX ∈L2(G).
Furthermore, it can be useful to assume that the steps xn are inverse invariant in distribution, i.e. xn
d= x−1
n . This is not a
great restriction since many commonly appearing distributions are inverse invariant, but computations become much simpler as
Proposition 2 below shows. We will return to the loosening of this assumption in Section III-C. For now however, we assume
the following.
Assumption 3. The steps (xn)n≥1 are assumed to be inverse invariant, whence their probability density fX satisfies
fX(x) = fX(x−1)
for all x ∈G.
As a consequence of Assumptions 1 and 2, the problem of decompounding can be seen as an inference problem in L2
K(M).
Because of this, our first steps towards an inference method is to develop estimators of the Fourier coefficients ⟨fX, ϕπ⟩for
π ∈bGK.
