B. Lower Bounds for K-invariant Density Estimation
To consider K-invariant density estimation we must firstly return to considerations of the geometrical structure of M = G/K.
The Lie algebra g of G can be written in terms of its Cartan decomposition g = h ⊕p, where h is the Lie algebra of K ⊂G
and where p is a Lie subalgebra of G which is isomorphic to the tangent space Tp0M of M at p0. The rank r of M is the
dimension of the maximal abelian subalgebra a ⊂p. In practical terms, 1 ≤r ≤d and r should be interpreted as the maximal
dimension of a totally geodesic flat submanifold of M. For example, Sd has rank r = 1 for all d, since we can at most embed
a great circle (of dimension 1) as a totally geodesic flat submanifold around any point. Likewise, Rd is trivially of rank d. We
reiterate once again that a formal treatment of these classical facts is found in [6].
For lower bounds on density estimation in L2
K(M), we will return to considering sets of the form
FT := {π ∈bGK : κπ ≤T},
and it is because of this that the rank r has an influence on density estimation. Indeed, in FT there is only a single element
π for each Casimir element, as opposed to the dπ basis elements that are included for each Casimir element in the set ΓT
constructed for Proposition 6. Hence, the Weyl counting law can not be applied directly as it could for ΓT . Instead, the growth
rate of this set will be
|FT | ∼T r/2.
Let us give a sketch of the main steps needed to prove why this is true: By the classical statement of the Cartan–Helgason-
theorem, see [22, Chapter 5], the spherical representations π ∈bGK can be parametrized by a subset of the dominant weights
of G that restrict to linear functionals γπ : a →R under certain invariance conditions, where again a ⊂p is a maximal Abelian
subspace. We aptly say that the resulting restrictions W := {γπ : a →R} are the restricted weights of M. It is a classical
result, see e.g. [11, Corollary 2.5.1], that a dominant weight γ corresponding to π ∈bG satisfies |γ|2 ∼κπ. In other words,
counting elements in FT amounts to counting the number of restricted weights that are contained in a ball of radius T 1/2 in
the algebraic dual a∗∼= Rr of a. Due also to the Cartan–Helgason-theorem, the set of restricted weights W = L ∩C is given
as the intersection by an r-dimensional lattice L ⊂a∗and a closed cone2 C with non-empty interior and full rank r. In other
words, the number of restricted weights contained in a ball of radius T 1/2 has the same leading order growth as lattice point
counting in Rr since the intersection by C is immaterial for the leading order growth of W. We conclude that
|FT | ∼|{γ ∈W : |γ| ≤T 1/2}| ∼|{γ ∈L ⊂a∗: |γ| ≤T 1/2}| ∼T r/2.
2In actuality, C is a positive Weyl-Chamber of the root system in a∗, but this would require a paragraph on its own to define and we thus leave it as
generically defined as possible.
14
