First order CDW transition and phonon
measurements
An absence of thermal hysteresis in resistivity and
magnetization across TCDW suggests the coupled CDW
and magnetic transitions in CsCr3Sb5 to be either second
order or weakly ﬁrst order [28]. Using the elastic chan-
nel of inelastic X-ray scattering, we probed the inten-
sity of the CDW peak Q = (2.25, 1.75, 0), which shows
saturation within a few Kelvin of its onset [Fig. 3(a)],
directly evidencing a ﬁrst order transition.
A negligi-
ble thermal hysteresis is observed between warming and
cooling, consistent with physical property measurements
[28]. A step-like transition with a Gaussian distribution
of transition temperatures gives an excellent ﬁt of the
measured CDW intensity, yielding TCDW ≈52 K and
a width of 0.8 K. A ﬁt to I ∝(TCDW −T )2β with a
Gaussian distribution of transitions temperatures leads
to β ≈0.03, where the small exponent also excludes the
transition being second order.
Temperature dependence of the normalized CDW peak
intensities are compared between CsCr3Sb5 and AV3Sb5
compounds in Fig. 3(b). The CDW transitions are ﬁrst
order in CsV3Sb5 and RbV3Sb5 , and becomes second
order in KV3Sb5 [38–40]. In terms of the discontinuity
in the CDW peak intensity, the discontinuity weakens
from CsV3Sb5 to RbV3Sb5, and disappears in KV3Sb5.
In CsCr3Sb5, the CDW transition is sharper (with larger
discontinuity) than the AV3Sb5 compounds, which indi-
cates the transition is more strongly ﬁrst-order, although
still weakly ﬁrst order such that a signiﬁcant thermal
hysteresis is not observed.
