served in the XRD images at 60 K [Figs. 1(e) and (g)],
which corresponds to (T −TCDW)/TCDW ≈0.15, in con-
trast to CsV3Sb5 that shows diﬀuse scattering associated
with the CDW up to at least (T −TCDW)/TCDW ≈0.21
[41]. Recent IXS measurements indicate the diﬀuse scat-
tering in AV3Sb5 result from soft phonons [42, 43], and
the absence of diﬀuse scattering in CsCr3Sb5 suggests
that compared to AV3Sb5, similar soft phonons are ei-
ther absent or less prominent in CsCr3Sb5, consistent
with its more strongly ﬁrst order CDW transition.
An absence of prominent soft phonons is consistent
with our IXS measurements, which only shows an acous-
tic phonon mode along (2 + q, 2 −q, 0) at 70 K, without
signatures of soft modes at qCDW [Fig. 3(c)]. Phonon
measurements were also performed in other Brillouin
zones, also without qCDW soft phonons, and the detected
qCDW acoustic phonons are essentially independent of
temperature [Fig. 3(d)]. Raw and additional IXS data
are presented in Supplementary Note 1 and Supplemen-
tary Figs. 1-3. A similar absence of soft phonons was also
reported in CsCr3Sb5 with short-range CDW [44].
A divergent E2g elastoresistance above TCDW has been
reported for CsCr3Sb5 [36], which suggests strong ne-
matic ﬂuctuations in the high-temperature phase. How-
ever, such nematic ﬂuctuations do not lead to anomalies
of the in-plane transverse acoustic phonons with the same
symmetry [Fig. 3(c) and (d)], as widely observed in the
parent compounds of iron-based superconductors [45–47].
This suggests a weak coupling between nematicity and
the lattice in CsCr3Sb5, which occurs in the iron-based
superconductors upon overdoping [48].
Antiferromagnetic dimers in an altermagnetic
ground state
Based on the results in Ref. [35], we performed a high-
throughput search for all possible collinear in-plane AFM
magnetic conﬁgurations compatible with a 4 × 1 CDW
supercell with space group Pbam, and then relaxed the
crystal structures and computed their associated ener-
gies. The resulting state with the lowest energy is shown
in Fig. 4(a), which shows Cr-Cr dimers separated by
Cr chains, in remarkable agreement with the experimen-
tally determined CDW structure in Fig. 2(a). The cal-
culated ground state CDW structure also exhibits Cs-Cs
dimers [Supplementary Fig. 4], consistent with experi-
mental ﬁndings [Fig. 2(b)].
Other 4 × 1 Pbam CDW
structures with higher energies, and their associated mag-
netic structures, are presented in Supplementary Note 2
and Supplementary Fig. 5.
As shown in Fig. 4, the dimers in the calculated CDW
ground state are AFM, while the Cr chains are FM. To
probe the magnetic interactions between Cr atoms, we
examined how ﬂipping certain spins aﬀects the energy of
the ground state magnetic conﬁguration. By ﬂipping all
the spins on Cr dimers [Fig. 4(c)], we ﬁnd that the energy
is only increased by 11.4 meV/f.u. For this excited state,
all the dimers remain AFM and the Cr chains remain
FM, and the relative spin orientations between the AFM
dimers are also maintained. Thus, the increased energy
