the dimers, evidenced by the signiﬁcant energy costs of
conﬁgurations with 2 or 4 FM dimers [Figs. 4(d) and
(e)], which suggest ≈50 meV/dimer when the dimer is
changed from AFM to FM. Furthermore, our calculations
indicate the inter-layer couplings are very weak, such that
AFM and FM inter-layer couplings cannot be diﬀerenti-
ated within the precision of ﬁrst-principles calculations.
The ground state magnetic conﬁguration for the 4 ×
1 CDW has zero net magnetization, and because its
two spin sub-lattices are related by a {Myz|(0, 1
2, 0)}
or a {Mxz|( 1
2, 0, 0)} glide mirror plane [dashed lines in
Fig. 4(a)] rather than translation or inversion, it is in fact
an altermagnetic state [49–52] in the two-dimensional
limit. In three dimensions, a FM interlayer coupling be-
tween spins will lead to an altermagnetic state in the
bulk, whereas an AFM coupling will lead to the cancella-
tion of spin polarization between adjacent layers, result-
ing instead in a hidden altermagnetic state [53]. Assum-
ing a FM interlayer coupling in the CDW state, Fig. 5(a)
shows the spin polarized band structure of CsCr3Sb5, di-
rectly demonstrating its altermagnetic nature. The cor-
responding Fermi surface is shown in Fig. 5(b), further
evidencing spin-polarized bands.
When the CDW structure is not constrained to the ex-
perimentally determined 4×1 cell, a 4×2 CDW structure
is calculated to have a lower energy [Fig. 5(c)] [35]. This
4 × 2 CDW is also altermagnetic with the same crystal
symmetry and consists of AFM Cr-Cr dimers [35], similar
to the experimentally determined 4 × 1 CDW [Fig. 2(a)].
This shows that while distinct phases compete to be the
ground state of CsCr3Sb5, the presence of AFM dimers
is a robust feature of these phases. At the level of den-
sity functional theory, the 4 × 1 CDW can be stabilized
as the ground state by introducing a small amount of Sn
substituting for Sb (7%), modeled via the virtual crystal
approximation [Fig. 5(d)]. This suggests that the 4 × 2
and 4×1 CDWs are close in energy, with the latter possi-
bly stabilized by deviations from an ideal stoichiometry.
Alternatively, strong electronic correlations in CsCr3Sb5
beyond density functional theory may tilt the balance in
favor of the 4 × 1 CDW, and render the 4 × 2 state less
competitive.
DISCUSSION AND CONCLUSION
Our ﬁndings reveal that the CDW structure of
CsCr3Sb5
is very diﬀerent from those in AV3Sb5,
ScV6Sn6, and LuNb6Sn6.
In AV3Sb5, nonmagnetic V
atoms on the kagome lattice form star of David or in-
verse star-of-David structures that result in a 2×2 CDW
in the ab-plane [16].
In ScV6Sn6 and LuNb6Sn6, the
3 × 3 × 3 CDWs are dominated by the motion of Sn-
Sc(Lu)-Sn trimers along c-axis, rather than in-plane dis-
tortions of the V or Nb kagome lattice [54, 55]. The in-
plane modulations of these CDW structures retain the
sixfold rotational symmetries of their high temperature
phases, which maybe broken by the stacking of distorted
planes along the c-axis. On the other hand, the CDW
structure of CsCr3Sb5 is inherently strongly anisotropic,
with Cr-Cr dimers separated by Cr chains that run along
the a-axis of the 4 × 1 cell [Fig. 2(a)], which in combina-
tion with three possible twin domains [Fig. 1(c)], natu-
rally account for its three-state Potts nematicity [36].
As the ideal kagome lattice with AFM nearest-
neighbor interactions is highly frustrated, CDW forma-
tion provides a natural way to relieve this mangetic frus-
tration and facilitate long-range magnetic order. There-
fore, the CDW of CsCr3Sb5 is likely driven by the spin
Jahn-Teller eﬀect, which saves magnetic energy at the
cost of elastic energy.
For comparison, the CDWs in
AV3Sb5 are driven by the nesting between van Hove
singularities close to the Fermi level [56–59] or electron-
phonon coupling [42, 43, 60–63], whereas the CDWs in
ScV6Sn6 and LuNb6Sn6 are driven by electron-phonon
coupling [64–66], potentially also involving nesting eﬀects
[67].
Under pressure, the intertwined CDW and magnetic
transitions of CsCr3Sb5 split and are continuously sup-
pressed [28], similar to magnetic and nematic orders in
7
the iron-based superconductors [3, 5, 6].
Our ﬁndings
show that the AFM coupling within Cr-Cr dimers is the
dominant energy scale in the CDW state of CsCr3Sb5,
with the associated ﬂuctuations likely persisting beyond
the the suppression of the CDW, and contribute to the
non-Fermi-liquid state from which a dome of SC emerges.
Resonant inelastic X-ray scattering detected two spin ex-
citation modes in CsCr3Sb5 [31], and the dispersionless
nature of these modes suggest they are associated with
local entities, for which the AFM dimers uncovered in
this work are natural candidates.
In such a scenario,
the persistence of such local excitations above TCDW [31]
suggests that dynamic variants of ordered dimers in the
CDW state survive even when the CDW is destabilized.
SC at the verge of dimer formation has been reported
in IrTe2 [68, 69], AuTe2 [70, 71], and CuIr2S4 [72]. How-
ever, in these cases the dimers are nonmagnetic, the
dimer state is suppressed via ﬁrst-order-like quantum
phase transitions, and the associated SC emerge from
Fermi liquid normal states.
CsCr3Sb5 is thus unique
compared to these systems, as its dimers are magnetic
with a strong AFM coupling, the intertwined CDW and
magnetic orders are continuously suppressed towards a
putative QCP, and the SC emerges from a non-Fermi-
liquid normal state.
While our DFT calculations suggest ordered AFM
dimers that lead to an overall altermagnetic state, it takes
very little energy to ﬂip both spins of a given dimer.
This raises the interesting possibility that the dimers are
in fact spin singlets (rather than AFM-ordered), which
could become mobile as the CDW is suppressed under
pressure. Such a scenario is akin to the long-sought goal
of obtaining a superconductor by doping a resonating va-
lence bond state or a valence bond solid [73–75], where
mobile spin singlets become Cooper pairs.
While this
possibility is tantalizing, we note the superconducting
coherence length of CsCr3Sb5 from upper critical ﬁeld
measurements is around 5 nm [28], much larger than the
dimers observed in the parent phase.
In summary, we showed that the CDW of the cor-
related kagome metal CsCr3Sb5 is characterized by a
Pbam structure consisting of AFM Cr-Cr dimers and FM
chains, explicitly breaking the sixfold rotation symmetry
of the high temperature phase. The CDW transition in
CsCr3Sb5 is found to be more strongly ﬁrst order than
its vanadium-based sister compounds, likely accounting
for the absence of diﬀuse scattering or prominent soft
phonons above TCDW. Constrained by the experimen-
tally determined structure, we obtained the magnetic
structure of CsCr3Sb5 via a systematic search of collinear
magnetic phases, and showed the dominant magnetic in-
teractions is AFM and within the Cr dimers. The CDW
and magnetic structures determined in this work form
the basis for further understanding CsCr3Sb5, in which
kagome physics, unconventional superconductivity, and
strong electronic correlations intersect.",True,23139
