ahmed.laghribi@univ-artois.fr (Ahmed Laghribi)
arXiv:2604.15928v1 [math.KT] 17 Apr 2026
• If αm = βm for all m ∈{1, . . . , n} \ {k}, then {α1, . . . , αn} + {β1, . . . , βn} = {γ1, . . . , γn} where
γm = αm for m , k and γk = αkβk.
• If αm = 1 −αk for some m, k ∈{1, . . . , n} with m , k, then {α1, α2, . . . , αn} = 0.
A special attention is given to Kn/2Kn(F). The class of {α1, α2, . . . , αn} in Kn/2Kn(F) is denoted
by {α1, α2, . . . , αn}2. More generally, the class of {α1, α2, . . . , αn} in Kn/2mKn(F) is denoted by
{α1, α2, . . . , αn}2m.
In [9], it was proven that when char(F) = 2, Kn(F)/2Kn(F) is isomorphic to InF/In+1F, by
mapping {α1, α2, . . . , αn}2 to ⟨⟨α1, . . . , αn⟩⟩. The analogous result in char(F) , 2 was proven in
[14].
The ring of symmetric bilinear forms modulo metabolic forms is known as the Witt ring, and
denoted WF. Its fundamental ideal consisting of even-dimensional forms is IF. Its nth power,
InF, is known to be generated by scalar multiples of n-fold Pfister forms ⟨⟨α1, . . . , αn⟩⟩, defined to
be ⟨1, −α1⟩⊗· · · ⊗⟨1, −αn⟩. For background, see [7].
3. Chain Lemma
Let τ ∈K2(F)/2K2(F) be a sum of two symbols, and let (V, E) be a labeled graph whose
vertices are all pairs (S 1, S 2) of symbols in K2(F)/2K2(F) satisfying S 1 + S 2 = τ.
Two vertices (S 1, S 2) and (T1, T2) are connected by an edge if there exist α, β, γ, δ ∈F× and
x ∈F such that: S 1 = {α, β}2, S 2 = {γ, δ}2, T1 = {α, (x2 + αγ)β}2 and T2 = {γ, (x2 + αγ)δ}2. If
x = 0 (resp. x , 0), then we say that (S 1, S 2) and (T1, T2) are connected by an edge of type I
(resp. of type II). Note that when x , 0, we can assume x = 1.
Lemma 3.1.
(1) If there exist α, β, γ, δ ∈F× and x, y ∈F not both zero such that S 1 = {α, β}2, S 2 = {γ, δ}2,
T1 = {α, (x2 + αγy2)β}2 and T2 = {γ, (x2 + αγy2)δ}2, then (S 1, S 2) and (T1, T2) are either
equal or connected by an edge of type I or II.
(2) If (S 1, S 2) is connected to (T1, T2) by an edge of type 2, then there exists a vertex (R1, R2)
connected to both (S 1, S 2) and (T1, T2) by edges of type I.
Proof.
(1) If y = 0, then (S 1, S 2) = (T1, T2). So suppose y , 0. If x = 0, then (S 1, S 2) is connected to
(T1, T2) by an edge of type I, and if x , 0, by an edge of type 2.
(2) Suppose that (S 1, S 2) is connected to (T1, T2) by an edge of type 2. Then, there exist
α, β, γ, δ ∈F× such that S 1 = {α, β}2, S 2 = {γ, δ}2, T1 = {α, β(1 + αγ)}2 and T2 =
{γ, δ(1 + αγ)}2. Now, take R1 = {αγ, β}2 and R2 = {γ, βδ}2. Then, clearly (S 1, S 2) is
connected to (R1, R2) by an edge of type I. Seeing that R1 = {αγ, β(1 + αγ)}2 clarifies why
(R1, R2) is also connected to (T1, T2) by an edge of type I.
□
Theorem 3.2. Every two vertices are connected by a path consisting of at most three edges, with
at most one of type II and the others of type I. If three edges are necessary, including one of type
II, then the one of type II is in between the two edges of type I. If we restrict to edges of type I only,
there is a path of length at most 4.
