Proof. Suppose {α, β}2 + {γ, δ}2 = {a, b}2 + {c, d}2. Then the symmetric bilinear form
φ = ⟨α, β, αβγ, αβδ, αβγδ, a, b, abc, abd, abcd⟩
is a 10-dimensional form in I3F. By [6, Proposition 6.5], φ is isotropic. Therefore, αx2 + βy2 +
αβ(z2γ + w2δ + v2γδ) = aq2 + br2 + ab(cs2 + dt2 + cdu2) for some q, r, s, t, u, v, w, x, y, z ∈F, not
all zero.
Now, {α, β}2 + {γ, δ}2 = {αx2 + βy2, αβ}2 + {z2γ + w2δ + v2γδ, γδ(z2γ + w2δ)}2
= {αx2 + βy2, αβ(z2γ + w2δ + v2γδ)}2 + {z2γ + w2δ + v2γδ, γδ(z2γ + w2δ)(αx2 + βy2)}2
= {αx2+βy2+αβ(z2γ+w2δ+v2γδ), αβ(z2γ+w2δ+v2γδ)(αx2+βy2)}2+{z2γ+w2δ+v2γδ, γδ(z2γ+
w2δ)(αx2 + βy2)}2.
Similarly, {a, b}2 + {c, d}2 = {aq2 + br2, ab}2 + {cs2 + dt2 + cdu2, cd(cs2 + dt2)}2
= {aq2 + br2, ab(cs2 + dt2 + cdu2)}2 + {cs2 + dt2 + cdu2, cd(cs2 + dt2 + cdu2)(aq2 + br2)}2
= {aq2 + br2 + ab(cs2 + dt2 + cdu2), ab(cs2 + dt2 + cdu2)(aq2 + br2)}2 + {cs2 + dt2 + cdu2, cd(cs2 +
dt2 + cdu2)(aq2 + br2)}2.
Therefore, ({α, β}2, {γ, δ}2) is connected to ({A, B}2, {C, D}2) by an edge of type I, and ({a, b}2, {c, d}2)
is connected to ({A, L}2, {M, N}2) by an edge of type I. It is enough to show that ({A, B}2, {C, D}2)
is connected to ({A, L}2, {M, N}2) by an edge of either type. Since {A, B}2 + {C, D}2 = {A, L}2 +
{M, N}2, we have {A, BL}2 = {C, D}2 + {M, N}2. Therefore, the symmetric bilinear form
⟨C, D,CD, M, N, MN⟩
is isotropic by [5, Section 5, Lemma 1]. Consequently, Ci2 + Dj2 + CDk2 = Mℓ2 + Nm2 +
MNn2, which means {C, D}2 = {Ci2 + Dj2 + CDk2,CD(Ci2 + Dj2)}2 and {M, N}2 = {Mℓ2 +
Nm2 + MNn2, MN(Mℓ2 + Nm2)}2. It means that we could assume C = M from the start. Now,
{A, B}2 + {C, D}2 = {A, L}2 + {C, N}2 implies {A, BL}2 = {C, DN}2. Hence, there exists P for which
{A, BL}2 = {A, P}2 = {C, P}2 = {C, DN}2. Since {AC, P}2 = 0, P = µ2 + ACν2 for some µ, ν ∈F.
From {A, BLP}2 = 0 we conclude that by a suitable choice of B, we can assume L = BP. Similarly,
from {C, DN}2 = 0, by a suitable choice of D, we can assume N = DP, and that completes the
proof.
□
Remark 3.3. Note that what we call here an edge of type I is analogous what is called in [11]
“strongly simply-equivalent"". In that paper, the author concluded that in characteristic not 2, every
two vertices in this graph are connected by a path of length at most three of edges of either type
and then concluded that a path of length at most 6 of edges of type I must exist ([11, Propositions
1 and 2]). However, following the same computations as we describe here, his Proposition 2 can
be easily improved to a path of length at most 4 instead of 6.
The following corollary is the characteristic 2 analogue of the main result of [12]. It is also a
special case of [8, Appendix], but it is nice to have an elementary explanation.
Corollary 3.4. The map from sums of two symbols in K2(F)/2K2(F) to K4(F)/2K4(F) given by
{a, b}2 + {c, d}2 7→{a, b, c, d}2 is well-defined.
Proof. It readily follows from Theorem 3.2. It is enough to stress that an edge of type I between
two vertices does not change the image of this sum under this map. Indeed, we have: {a, bc}2 +
{c, ad}2 7→{a, bc, c, ad}2 = {a, b, c, d}2.
□
