The case of n = 1 in this lemma serves as a partial analogue of Albert’s theorem on degree 4
exponent 2 central simple algebras (see [1, Page 174, Theorem 2]).
Theorem 4.2. A sum of two symbols τ = {a, b}2 + {c, d}2 ∈K2(F)/2K2(F) is equal to a single
symbol in K2(F)/4K2(F) if and only if {a, b, c, d} is trivial in K4(F)/2K4(F).
Proof. Suppose that τ is a symbol in K4(F)/2K4(F). This means that {a, b2} + {c, d2} = {α, β}4 for
suitable α, β ∈F×. Then, we get {α, β}2 = 0 by the sequence (1). The previous remark yields
τ = {x, (x2 + αy2)α}2 + {y, x2 + αy2}2,
for suitable x, y ∈F×. This means {a, b, c, d} = {x, (x2 + αy2)α, y, x2 + αy2} for some x, y ∈F.
Since [F2(x, (x2 + αy2)α, y, x2 + αy2) : F2] = [F2(α, x, y) : F2] ≤8, the symbol {a, b, c, d} must be
trivial in K4(F)/2K4(F).
In the opposite direction, suppose {a, b, c, d} is trivial in K4(F)/2K4(F). Then ⟨⟨a, b, c, d⟩⟩is
isotropic. If τ is equal to one symbol in K2(F)/2K2(F) then clearly it is equal to one symbol
in K2(F)/4K2(F), so assume it is not. In particular, the Albert form φ = ⟨a, b, ab, c, d, cd⟩is
anisotropic. Moreover, the isotropy of ⟨⟨a, b, c, d⟩⟩implies that its quasi-Pfister neighbor
⟨1, ac, ad, acd, a, ab, c, d, cd⟩
is isotropic. Therefore, q2 + a(cr2 + ds2 + cdt2) + au2 + abv2 + cx2 + dy2 + cdz2 = 0 for some
q, r, s, t, u, v, x, y, z ∈F not all zero. Since φ is anisotropic, we have λ := q2 + a(cr2 + ds2 + cdt2) is
not zero. Moreover, the scalars cr2 + ds2 + cdt2 and cx2 + dy2 + cdz2 are not simultaneously zero.
Take c′ a nonzero scalar among them. Therefore, we can choose d′ such that {c, d}2 = {c′, d′}2 and
λ + au2 + abv2 + c′ℓ2 + d′m2 + c′d′n2 = 0 for some ℓ, m, n ∈F. Dividing by λ, we obtain that the
form ψ = ⟨1, λa, λab, λc′, λd′, λc′d′⟩is isotropic.
Since ⟨1, ac′⟩represents λ, we have ⟨1, ac′⟩= λ⟨1, ac′⟩, which means ⟨λa, λc′⟩= ⟨a, c′⟩.
Therefore, ψ = ⟨1, a, λab, c′, λd′, λc′d′⟩. Since ψ is isotropic, there exists an element γ that is
represented both by ⟨1, a, λab⟩and by ⟨c′, λd′, λc′d′⟩. Therefore, there exist δ, β ∈F× such that
{c′, λd′}2 = {γ, δ}2 and either {a, λb}2 = {1 + γ, β}2 or {γ, β]2. The second option is impossible,
because that would mean that τ = {a, b}2 + {c, d}2 = {a, b}2 + {c′, d′}2 + {a, λb}2 + {c′, λd′}2 =
{γ, δβ}2 is equal to one symbol in K2(F)/2K2(F), and we assumed it is not. Hence, {γ, δ}2 =
{γ, δ2}4 = {γ, δ2(γ + 1)}4, whereas {1 + γ, β}2 = {(1 + γ)δ2, β}2 = {(1 + γ)δ2, β2}4. Consequently,
