2604.15920v1,Local qubit invariants on quantum computer,"We present two general methods to implement quantum circuits for the direct measuring of local unitary invariants on quantum computers. We work these out for important three-qubit invariants, and also demonstrate these on the IBM Quantum Platform for important entanglement measures of three qubits.","['Szilárd Szalay', 'Frédéric Holweck']",2026-04-17,0,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.15920v1,quant-ph,"LOCAL QUBIT INVARIANTS ON QUANTUM COMPUTER
SZILÁRD SZALAY
Department of Theoretical Solid State Physics, Wigner Research Centre for
Physics, Budapest, Hungary
FRÉDÉRIC HOLWECK
Laboratoire Interdisciplinaire Carnot de Bourgogne, UMR 6303 CNRS, University
of Technology of Belfort-Montbéliard, 90010 Belfort Cedex, France
Mathematics and Statistics Department, Auburn University, Auburn, AL, USA
Abstract. We present two general methods to implement quantum circuits for
the direct measuring of local unitary invariants on quantum computers. We work
these out for important three-qubit invariants, and also demonstrate these on the
IBM Quantum Platform for important entanglement measures of three qubits.
1. Introduction
Entanglement is the most notable form of nonclassical correlations characteris-
tic of quantum systems [1]. Since entanglement is invariant with respect to local
unitary transformations, local unitary (LU-) invariants give the finest description of
entanglement, in principle, everything about entanglement in a quantum state can
be told in terms of LU-invariants. A significant amount of effort was therefore made
to apply invariant theory to multipartite quantum systems [2–8], important exotic
results were obtained for small systems [9–17], while also the general description was
worked out [18–24].
Recently noisy intermediate scale quantum (NISQ) computers became accessible
online, and entangled quantum systems can be manipulated now from home, making
possible the demonstration of several exotic quantum phenomena [25–30]. In this
work we describe two general methods for the implementation of the direct measure-
ment of important nonnegative real-valued LU-invariants of n = 2 and 3 qubits on
E-mail addresses: szalay.szilard@wigner.hu, frederic.holweck@utbm.fr.
1
arXiv:2604.15920v1 [quant-ph] 17 Apr 2026
2
LOCAL QUBIT INVARIANTS ON QUANTUM COMPUTER
quantum computers, and we also demonstrate these using the IBM Quantum Plat-
form [31, 32]. These LU-invariants are positively homogeneous functions of degree
k = 4, 8, 12, and the appropriate roots of some of these are important entanglement
measures [11, 17, 33, 34]. The two methods use nk/4 and nk/2 qubits, respectively,
and both use k/2 copies of the state. More precisely, as cloning quantum states is
impossible, these use k/2 instances of the oracle Uψ producing the state and/or its
transpose or adjoint or conjugate. The second method leads to larger circuits, uti-
lizing a larger number of CNOT gates, and measurements on twice as many qubits,
leading to lower precision, or higher relative error on the outcomes than the first one.
The methods presented in this work translate the index contractions in the defini-
tions of the LU-invariants to the language of quantum circuits, then the probability
of a particular outcome of the measurement of the qubit registers gives a power of the
value of the LU-invariant directly. Our first method is related to the interferometric
method [35–37], where the value of the LU invariant can be read off from the visibility
of the interference pattern (by maximization/fitting). Other methods on the evalua-
tion of LU-invariants on quantum computers were given by ad-hoc methods based on
the tracking of the terms of the state vector coefficients in the polynomials by which
the LU-invariants are formulated. These were given for example for the concurrence
squared [38, 39] in two-qubit systems, or for the three-tangle squared [40, 41] in
three-qubit systems. The resulting circuits are related to our second method. Be-
sides these direct methods, there is another method, relying on LU-canonical forms,
which involves optimization [42], which means a sufficiently large number of runs
of the protocol for different parameters to achieve the optimized value. There is
also another way, LU-invariants can of course be evaluated simply by substituting
the amplitudes resulted from the full tomography of the state [43]. We emphasize
that the contraction methods presented here are applicable to arbitrary number of
subsystems of arbitrary dimensions, and are especially useful when the ad-hoc, opti-
mization based, and tomography based methods become infeasible with the increase
of the system size.
2. Local invariants and covariants
Polynomial invariants of quantum state vectors can be formulated by index con-
tractions. Here we show general methods, translating the index contractions directly
to the language of quantum circuits. U(2) and SL(2) invariants (scalars) and covari-
ants (tensors) of qubit systems can be formed by index contractions with
