a (14c), ω2 (14e) and τ 2 (14f),
as these are sufficient for the SLOCC classification, and the square roots of these
are important entanglement measures. Using the explicit forms in Appendix A.1,
straightforward calculations lead to the θ-dependent values of these invariants for
the one-parameter families of state vectors (26). These are shown in Table 2. Using
the SLOCC classification in Table 1, formulated by the vanishing of the invariants,
we have that |ψ1|23(θ)⟩is in class 1|2|3 (fully separable) for θ ∈πN, otherwise is in
class 1|23; |ψW(θ)⟩is in class 1|2|3 for θ ∈2πN, it is in class 1|23 for θ ∈2π(N+1/2),
otherwise it is in class W; and |ψGHZ(θ)⟩is in class 1|2|3 for θ ∈πN, otherwise it is
in class GHZ.
