PID controllers are often implemented based on the positional discrete representation
of eq. (5) and eq. (6). This representation is also sometimes called the direct form. In
each iteration, the control signal is computed from the two most recent setpoint and
process variable values, with previous value of the error e(t−∆t) and the previous value
of the error integral ui(t −∆t) as controller states.
2.3. Incremental form
The incremental form, also called the velocity form (Clarke, 1984), computes the control
signal increment since the last iteration, and uses the control signal itself to store the
controller state. The control law of the incremental form is
u(t) = u(t −∆t) + ∆u(t),
(7)
where ∆u(t) represents the increment of the following signal between the indexed time,
and an instance ∆t earlier. In general, a signal increment from one time step to the
next is denoted with ∆, for example,
∆u(t) = u(t) −u(t −∆t).
(8)
The control signal increment ∆u(t) can be split into the individual terms
∆u = ∆up(t) + ∆ui(t) + ∆ud(t),
(9)
