DAVID ASPER´O AND CURIAL GALLART
the uncountable. In fact, the space [λ]λ quotiented by the ideal of subsets of
λ of size < µ, for some cardinal µ < λ, is much harder to deal with. Indeed,
the known techniques coming from the area of cardinal characteristics do
not seem to work in this context, so our understanding of this space is much
poorer. In this paper we will focus on the space [ω1]ω1 quotiented by the
ideal of finite subsets of ω1. Let us first give a brief overview of the main
results in the study of the spaces [ω1]ω1 and ω1ω1 in this context.
Definition 1.1. If δ is a limit ordinal, a strong δ-almost disjoint family of
subsets of ω1 is a sequence ⟨Aα : α < δ⟩of subsets of ω1 such that for all
α < β < δ,
(1) |Aα| = ℵ1, and
(2) |Aα ∩Aβ| < ℵ0.
Baumgartner showed in [2] that one can consistently have arbitrarily long
strong almost disjoint families of subsets of ω1.
Theorem 1.2 (Baumgartner). Assume GCH and let δ be a limit ordinal.
There exists a cardinal-preserving forcing notion P that forces the existence
of a strong δ-almost disjoint family of subsets of ω1.
Baumgartner’s result motivated the introduction of stronger properties
for families living in the spaces [ω1]ω1 and ω1ω1.
Definition 1.3. A strong δ-chain of subsets of ω1 is a sequence ⟨Xα : α < δ⟩
of subsets of ω1 such that for all α < β < δ,
(1) |Xβ \ Xα| = ℵ1, and
(2) |Xα \ Xβ| < ℵ0.
In the same paper, Baumgartner showed that the existence of a strong
chain of subsets of ω1 implies the existence of a strong almost disjoint family
of subsets of ω1. More precisely, if ⟨Xα : α < δ⟩is a strong δ-chain of subsets
of ω1 and we define Aα := Xα+1 \ Xα, for every α < δ, then ⟨Aα : α < δ⟩is
a strong δ-almost disjoint family of subsets of ω1.
Definition 1.4. If δ is a limit ordinal, a strong δ-chain of functions from
ω1 to ω1 is a sequence ⟨fα : α < δ⟩of functions such that for all α < β < δ,
(1) fα ∈ω1ω1, and
(2) |{ξ ∈ω1 : fα(ξ) ≥fβ(ξ)}| < ℵ0.
The existence of a strong chain of functions from ω1 to ω1 implies the
existence of a strong chain of subsets of ω1, by identifying each subset of ω1
with its characteristic function.
Strong chains of length ω1 exist in ZFC. However, the existence of longer
strong chains of functions implies the failure of CH, and hence their existence
is independent of ZFC. Hajnal and Szentmikl´ossy asked in [18] (s. page 435),
the following two natural questions about the existence of long strong chains.
Question 1.5. Is it consistent that there exists a strong ω2-chain of subsets
of ω1?
LONG STRONG CHAINS OF SUBSETS OF ω1
3
And the harder version of the question.
Question 1.6. Is it consistent that there exists a strong ω2-chain of func-
tions from ω1 to ω1?
Both questions were answered affirmatively by Koszmider.
Theorem 1.7 (Koszmider, [12]). Assuming □ω1, there exists a c.c.c. forc-
ing poset that adds a strong ω2-chain of subsets of ω1.
Moreover, in the same paper he proves that Chang’s Conjecture implies
that there is no strong ω2-chain of subsets of ω1 (and hence no strong ω2-
chain of functions from ω1 to ω1 either). Furthermore, since Chang’s Conjec-
ture is preserved by c.c.c. forcings, the following theorem shows that being a
strong chain of subsets of ω1 is a strictly stronger notion than that of being
a strong almost disjoint family.
Theorem 1.8 (Koszmider, [12]). It is consistent, modulo large cardinals,
that Chang’s Conjecture holds together with the existence of a strong almost
disjoint family of subsets of ω1 and the nonexistence of strong chains of
subsets of ω1.
Koszmider answered the second question affirmatively in [13]. However,
his proof required completely new ideas; in fact, in the same paper he proved
that, assuming CH, there is no c.c.c. poset forcing the existence of a strong
ω2-chain of functions from ω1 to ω1. In particular, this implies that one can
consistently have long strong chains of subsets of ω1, while having no strong
chains of functions from ω1 to ω1.
Theorem 1.9 (Koszmider, [13]). It is consistent that there is a strong ω2-
chain of subsets of ω1 but there is no strong ω2-chain of functions from ω1
to ω1.
In order to answer the second question, Koszmider introduced a cardinal-
preserving forcing notion with side conditions organized along morasses.
These side conditions, which consist of finitely many elements of a simplified
(ω1, 1)-morass ([22]), resemble the symmetric systems of [1] or [15], and
ensure that the forcing poset is proper (for a stationary set of countable
elementary submodels) and has the ℵ2-chain condition, provided that CH
holds in the ground model.
Theorem 1.10 (Koszmider, [13]). Assuming CH, there exists a cardinal-
preserving forcing poset that adds a strong ω2-chain of functions from ω1 to
ω1.
We can naturally generalize the notions of strong chains of sets and func-
tions by considering sequences of elements of the spaces [λ]λ and λλ, respec-
tively, increasing modulo the ideal [λ]µ, for some µ < λ. However, Shelah
and Inamdar proved some impossibility results about the existence of strong
chains in higher spaces. First, Shelah [19] showed that for every infinite
4
DAVID ASPER´O AND CURIAL GALLART
cardinal λ, there cannot be sequences ⟨fα : α < λ+++⟩of functions from
λ++λ++ increasing modulo the ideal [λ++]<λ. Later, Inamdar [8] improved
Shelah’s result by showing that there cannot be sequences ⟨Xα : α < λ+++⟩
of subsets of λ++ increasing modulo the ideal [λ++]<λ. In particular, there
cannot be sequences of subsets of ω2 of length ω3 increasing modulo finite.
Despite the limitations imposed by the results of Shelah and Inamdar, the
following very general question about the spaces [ω1]ω1 and ω1ω1 remains
open.
Question 1.11. What are the possible lengths of strong chains of functions
from ω1 to ω1 and subsets of ω1?
In the wake of Neeman’s introduction of side conditions consisting of
finite ∈-chains of models of two types ([16], [17]), Veliˇckovi´c and Venturi
[21] obtained a much simpler proof of Koszmider’s result.
Their forcing
poset incorporates Neeman’s side conditions to ensure the preservation of
ω1 and ω2. However, the preservation of larger cardinals is, in general, not
guaranteed by this type of side conditions.2 Therefore, the problem of finding
strong chains in [ω1]ω1 and ω1ω1 of length ω3 seems to be out of reach of
Neeman’s method. Therefore, in addition to being a very important question
in combinatorial set theory, it has also been regarded as a test question for
finding the right notion of side conditions of models of three types.
In this paper, we will show that side conditions of models of two types
are enough to force a strong ω3-chain of subsets of ω1. In particular, we will
define a forcing poset that incorporates symmetric systems of models of two
types as side conditions. These side conditions, which were introduced by
the second author in [7], will ensure that the forcing is proper and ℵ1-proper,
and that it has the ℵ3-Knaster condition, under the right assumptions. The
following theorem is the main result of the paper.
Theorem 1.12. If GCH holds, then there is a cardinal-preserving forcing
notion P forcing the existence of a strong ω3-chain of subsets of ω1.
The proof of the above theorem is built on some of the ideas of Koszmider
([13]) and Veliˇckovi´c-Venturi ([21]), and we believe that a very mild modifi-
cation of the forcing witnessing the theorem should allow us to get the same
result for strong chains of functions from ω1 to ω1.
The paper is organized as follows.
In Section 2, we will establish the
notation and provide all the necessary background in (strongly) proper forc-
ing and elementary submodels. In section 3, we will introduce symmetric
systems of models of two types and give an overview of the main amalgama-
tion lemmas from [7]. We will not give a full account of the proofs of these
lemmas, but we will give a detailed sketch of the arguments. In Section 4,
we will define the forcing poset witnessing Theorem 1.12. We will start by
proving some density lemmas and basic properties of the forcing, and then
2Although it is possible to ensure the ℵ3-chain condition, in some cases, by considering
large enough transitive-type models ([17]).
LONG STRONG CHAINS OF SUBSETS OF ω1
5
we will prove the cardinal preservation lemmas, which are the most involved
results of the section. In particular, we will show that, starting from a model
of GCH, the forcing is proper, ℵ1-proper and has the ℵ3-Knaster condition.
2.
Preliminaries
Our notation will be standard and follows [14] and [9]. We refer the reader
to these two sources for any undefined notions. Unless otherwise specified,
lowercase Greek letters α, β, γ, δ, ε, ξ, η will be used to denote ordinals, while
κ, λ, µ, ν, θ will be used to denote infinite cardinals. We will denote by OR
the class of all ordinals. Let X be any set. If µ is a cardinal, we will denote
by [X]µ the set of all subsets of X of size µ. The sets [X]<µ and [X]≤µ are
defined in the obvious way. If f is a function and X ⊆dom(f), then f”(X)
denotes the set {f(x) : x ∈X}.
Recall that for every set X, a subset U ⊆P(X) is unbounded in P(X)
(or unbounded in X) if it is unbounded with respect to inclusion. A subset
C ⊆P(X) is club in P(X) (or club in X) if it is closed and unbounded with
respect to inclusion. A subset S ⊆P(X) is stationary in P(X) (or stationary
in X) if it has non-empty intersection with all clubs in P(X). More generally,
C ⊆P(X) is club in P(X) if there exists a function f : [X]<ω →X such
that for every x ∈P(X), x ∈C if and only if f”([x]<ω) ⊆x. Therefore, a
subset S ⊆P(X) is stationary in P(X) if for every function f : [X]<ω →X,
there is x ∈S which is closed under f, i.e., f”([x]<ω) ⊆x. The two notions
of club coincide when replacing P(X) with [X]ω.
Definition 2.1. Let κ be an uncountable cardinal. A poset P has the κ-
Knaster condition if for every A ⊆P of size κ there is a subset B ⊆A of
the same size consisting of pairwise compatible conditions.
Remark 2.2. Every poset P with the κ-Knaster condition has the κ-chain
condition (denoted κ-c.c.).
Lemma 2.3. If κ is an uncountable cardinal and P is a forcing notion with
the κ-c.c., then every cardinal λ ≥κ is preserved after forcing with P.
We review some well-known general facts about proper and strongly
proper forcing.
Definition 2.4. Let Q be an elementary submodel of a big enough H(θ),
let P be a forcing poset and let p be a condition in P.
(1) p is (Q, P)-generic if for every dense subset D ⊆P such that D ∈Q,
the set D ∩Q is predense below p.
(2) p is strongly (Q, P)-generic if for every dense subset D ⊆P ∩Q, the
set D is predense below p.
Remark 2.5. Note that if a condition p in a poset P is strongly (Q, P)-
generic, where Q is an elementary submodel of some H(θ) such that P ∈Q,
then p is (Q, P)-generic.
6
DAVID ASPER´O AND CURIAL GALLART
Remark 2.6. Let κ be an infinite cardinal and let P ⊆H(κ) be a forcing
notion. Let p ∈P and Q ⪯H(κ) such that p is strongly (Q, P)-generic. If
θ > κ is a large enough cardinal and Q∗⪯H(θ) is such that Q∗∩H(κ) = Q,
then p is also strongly (Q∗, P)-generic.
Definition 2.7. Let θ be an infinite cardinal, let K be a collection of ele-
mentary submodels of H(θ), and let P be a forcing notion.
(1) P is K-proper if for every Q ∈K such that P ∈Q and every p ∈P∩Q,
there is a (Q, P)-generic condition q ∈P extending p.
(2) P is strongly K-proper if for every Q ∈K such that P ∈Q and
every p ∈P ∩Q, there is a strongly (Q, P)-generic condition q ∈P
extending p.
If µ is an infinite cardinal and K is the collection of all elementary sub-
models Q ⪯H(θ) such that |Q| = µ and <µQ ⊆Q, then we call P µ-proper
or strongly µ-proper, respectively. Moreover, if µ = ℵ0, then we call P proper
or strongly proper, respectively.
Lemma 2.8 (Claim 3.4 in [16]). Let θ be an infinite cardinal and let K∗be
a collection of elementary submodels of H(θ). Suppose that P ∈H(θ) is a
K∗-proper forcing notion. Let λ be a cardinal and assume that
{Q∗∈K∗: α ⊆Q∗, |Q∗| < λ}
is stationary in H(θ) for each α < λ. Then the forcing P preserves λ.
Corollary 2.9 (Claim 3.5 in [16]). Let κ be an infinite cardinal and let K
be a collection of elementary submodels of H(κ). Suppose that P ⊆H(κ) is
a strongly K-proper forcing notion. Let λ be a cardinal and assume that
{Q ∈K : α ⊆Q, |Q| < λ}
is stationary in H(κ) for each α < λ. Then the forcing P preserves λ.
2.1.
Elementary submodels
Given a model Q, we will denote Q ∩ω1 by δQ and sup(Q ∩ω2) by εQ, and
we will call δQ the ω1-height of Q and εQ the ω2-height of Q.
Given two ∈-isomorphic models of the Axiom of Extensionality Q0 and
Q1, we write ΨQ0,Q1 to denote the unique isomorphism Ψ between the struc-
tures (Q0; ∈) and (Q1; ∈).
For the rest of the paper we will fix a cardinal κ > ω2 and a predicate
T ⊆H(κ). We will usually refer to the structure (H(κ); ∈, T) simply by
H(κ).
Let S be the collection of countable M ⪯(H(κ); ∈, T).
We will
tend to use capital letters M, N to refer to models in S, which we will
call countable elementary or small models. It is a well-known result that S
contains a club subset of [H(κ)]ω.
Definition 2.10. We will call a collection L of ℵ1-sized elementary sub-
models X ⪯(H(κ); ∈, T) appropriate for S, if for every X ∈L and every
M ∈S such that X ∈M, then X ∩M ∈X ∩S.
