7
We will use L to denote arbitrary collections of models of size ℵ1 appro-
priate for S, and the capital letters X, Y, Z to refer to the models in L, which
we will call uncountable or large models.
Fact 2.11. Assuming CH, the collection of all elementary submodels X ⪯
H(κ) such that |X| = ℵ1 and ωX ⊆X is appropriate for S and stationary
in H(κ).
To refer to models of arbitrary size, we will use Q, as well as other capital
letters further down the alphabet. If Q is an elementary submodel of H(κ),
we will usually refer to the structure (Q; ∈, T ∩Q) by (Q; ∈, T). Moreover,
we might indistinctly use Q to refer to the structure (Q; ∈, T) or its universe.
It will be clear from the context to which one we are referring to.
The following are some basic facts about elementary submodels, which
can be found in any standard reference for set-theoretic notions such as [14]
and [9]. We include them without proof, and will use them throughout the
paper, sometimes without mention. Additional information on elementary
submodels and their applications in set theory can be found in [6] and [10].
Theorem 2.12 (Tarski-Vaught test). Let M be a model and let A ⊆M.
Then, A is the domain of an elementary submodel N ⪯M iff for every
formula φ(y, x) and every tuple a of A such that M |= ∃yφ(y, a), there is
b ∈A such that M |= φ(b, a).
Proposition 2.13. If Q0, Q1 ⪯H(κ) are such that Q0 ⊆Q1, then Q0 ⪯Q1.
Proposition 2.14. Let Q ⪯H(κ), and let µ < κ be a cardinal such that
µ ⊆Q. For every A ∈Q, if |A| = µ, then A ⊆Q.
Proposition 2.15. Let Q ⪯H(κ). If A is definable over H(κ) with param-
eters in Q, then A ∈Q.
Proposition 2.16. Let θ > κ, Q∗⪯H(θ) and κ ∈Q∗. Then Q∗∩H(κ) is
an elementary submodel of H(κ).
Proposition 2.17. Let Q be an elementary submodel of H(κ) such that
|Q| = µ < µ+ < κ. Then Q ∩µ+ ∈µ+ is a limit ordinal.
Proposition 2.18. Let Q0, Q1 ⪯H(κ) such that |Q0| = |Q1| = µ < µ+ < κ
and µ ⊆Q0 ∩Q1 and let Ψ be an isomorphism between (Q0; ∈, T) and
(Q1; ∈, T). Suppose T codes a sequence (eα : α ∈µ+ \ {0}) such that each
eα is a surjection from µ onto α. Then Ψ is the identity on Q0 ∩µ+. In
particular, Q0 ∩µ+ = Q1 ∩µ+.
Proposition 2.19. Let Q0, Q1 and P be elementary submodels of H(κ).
Suppose that P ∈Q0 and P ⊆Q0, and that Ψ : (Q0; ∈, T) →(Q1; ∈, T) is
an isomorphism. Then Ψ(P) is an elementary submodel of (H(κ); ∈, T).
Proof. It is straightforward to see that Ψ ↾P is an isomorphism between
(P; ∈, T) and (Ψ(P); ∈, T). Assume now that φ(y, ¯x) is a first-order formula
in the language of set theory and let Ψ(¯a) be a tuple of elements of Ψ(P)
