The next result, which was already noticed by Neeman in [16], is very fun-
damental in the understanding of the structure of side conditions of models
of two types and their limitations.
Proposition 2.20. Let M ∈S and X ∈L∩M. Then, for every Q ∈S ∪L
such that εX∩M ≤εQ < εX, Q /∈M.
Proof. Suppose, towards a contradiction, that there is some Q ∈S ∪L such
that εX∩M ≤εQ < εX and Q ∈M. First, note that since X ∈L, then
εX = X ∩ω2 is an ordinal in ω2. Therefore, εQ = sup(Q ∩ω2) is an ordinal
in X∩ω2, and hence εQ ∈M∩(X∩ω2). So we can conclude that εQ < εX∩M,
which contradicts our assumption.
□
Definition 2.21. Given a model Q, we let
Q[ω1] := {f(α) : f ∈Q, f a function with dom(f) = ω1, α ∈ω1}.
We call Q[ω1] the ω1-hull of Q, or simply the hull of Q.
The notion of ω1-hull of a small model plays a central role in the definition
of the side conditions that we will introduce in the next section.
Proposition 2.22. Let Q ∈S∪L. Then Q[ω1] is the ⊆-smallest elementary
submodel of H(κ) that contains Q ∪ω1 as a subset.
Proof. First note that since ω1 ∈Q, the identity function id : ω1 →ω1 is
definable in Q, and thus ω1 ⊆Q[ω1]. Moreover, for each a ∈Q, the constant
function sending all α ∈ω1 to a is definable in Q, and so Q ⊆Q[ω1].
Therefore, Q ∪ω1 ⊆Q[ω1].
We use the Tarski-Vaught test to show that Q[ω1] is an elementary sub-
