3.
Two-type symmetric systems and amalgama-
tion lemmas
In this section, we will introduce (S, L)-symmetric systems and outline the
main amalgamation lemmas from [7]. We will not give a full account of the
proofs for such lemmas. We refer the interested reader to the aforementioned
paper.
The side conditions that we will present in this section are a natural
combination of Neeman’s chains of models of two types from [16] and As-
per´o-Mota-Todorˇcevi´c’s symmetric systems of elementary submodels from
[1] and [20]. The structural properties of these side conditions, which will
be reflected in the amalgamation lemmas, will ensure that the forcing wit-
nessing Theorem 1.12 is proper, ℵ1-proper and has the ℵ3-Knaster condition
(this crucially uses the assumption 2ℵ1 = ℵ2).
For any set M ⊆S ∪L, we let dom(M) denote the set of ω2-heights
{εQ : Q ∈M}. If ε ∈dom(M), let M(ε) be the set of all models Q ∈M
such that εQ = ε. Moreover, we let M(<ε) denote the set of models Q ∈M
such that εQ < ε. We define M(≤ε), M(>ε), and M(≥ε) similarly. Lastly,
we let M[ω1] denote the set {Q[ω1] : Q ∈M}.
Definition 3.1. Let M be a finite set of members of H(κ). We say that
M is an (S, L)-symmetric system if and only if the following holds:
(A) Every Q ∈M is an element of S ∪L.
(B) For any two Q0, Q1 ∈M, if εQ0 = εQ1, then Q0[ω1] ∼= Q1[ω1].
(C) If ε1 is the immediate successor of ε0 in dom(M) and Q0 ∈M(ε0),
then there is Q1 ∈M(ε1) such that Q0 ∈Q1.
(D) For all Q0, Q1, Q′
1 ∈M such that Q0 ∈Q1 and εQ1 = εQ′
1,
ΨQ1[ω1],Q′
1[ω1](Q0) ∈M.
(E) For every X ∈M ∩L and every M ∈M ∩S, if X ∈M, then
X ∩M ∈M.
Remark 3.2. If M is an (S, L)-symmetric system and ε0 < ε1 belong to
dom(M), then for every Q0 ∈M(ε0) we can find a model Q1 ∈M(ε1) such
that Q0 ∈Q1[ω1] by successive applications of clause (C).
We will refer to clause (C) and the conclusion of the last remark, indis-
tinctly, as the shoulder axiom for M. Moreover, when we talk about the
symmetry of an (S, L)-symmetric system, we usually refer to the closure
under isomorphisms given by the following strengthening of condition (D).
