The following are some basic facts about (S, L)-symmetric systems, which
are easy consequences of the preliminary material on elementary submodels
from Section 2. We omit their proofs, which can be found in [7].
Proposition 3.4 (Proposition 3.3 in [7]). Let M be an (S, L)-symmetric
system. The following hold for any two models Q0, Q1 ∈M:
(1) If Q0 ∈Q1 and |Q0| ≤|Q1|, then Q0 ⊆Q1.
(2) If Q1 is a small model, Q0 ∈Q1[ω1], and there is no large model
X ∈M such that εQ0 < εX < εQ1, then Q0 ∈Q1.
Proposition 3.5 (Proposition 3.5 in [7]). Let M be an (S, L)-symmetric
system and let Q0, Q1 be two models in M such that Q0 ∈Q1[ω1]. If there
is ε ∈dom(M) such that εQ0 < ε < εQ1, then there is some P ∈M(ε) such
that Q0 ∈P[ω1] and P ∈Q1[ω1].
Proposition 3.6 (Proposition 3.6 in [7]). Let M be an (S, L)-symmetric
system. Let Q0, Q1 be two elementary submodels of H(κ) such that ΨQ0,Q1
is the unique isomorphism between (Q0; ∈) and (Q1; ∈). If M ⊆Q0, then
ΨQ0,Q1(M) is an (S, L)-symmetric system.
Next, we will include some results, which are standard in the context of
forcing with side conditions, for amalgamating (S, L)-symmetric systems.
The preservation properties of the pure side condition forcing follow, by
standard arguments, from these amalgamation lemmas, but most impor-
tantly, as we shall see in the next section, the preservation theorems for the
forcing witnessing Theorem 1.12 rely very heavily on the following results.
We will omit the proofs and only give a sketch of the main ideas.
The first amalgamation lemma, which appears as Lemma 3.7 in [7], asserts
that any (S, L)-symmetric system can be extended by adding any model that
contains it.
Lemma 3.7. Let M be an (S, L)-symmetric system and let Q ∈S ∪L be
such that M ⊆Q. Then there is an (S, L)-symmetric system MQ such that
M ∪{Q} ⊆MQ
If Q is a large model, it is easy to check that MQ = M ∪{Q} is an
(S, L)-symmetric system. If Q is a small model, then MQ can be defined
to be the closure of M ∪{Q} under the relevant intersections. That is, the
(S, L)-symmetric system MQ witnessing the conclusion of the last lemma
is
MQ = M ∪{Q} ∪{X ∩Q : Q ∈M ∩L}.
The next lemma asserts that any (S, L)-symmetric system can be natu-
rally reflected inside any of its models.
