Lemma 3.8. Let M be an (S, L)-symmetric system and let Q be a member
of M ∪M[ω1]. Then M ∩Q is an (S, L)-symmetric system.
The proof of the second amalgamation lemma, which appears as Lemma
Lemma 3.10 in [7], is straightforward if Q ∈L or Q ∈M[ω1]. Indeed, in
that case M ∩Q coincides with the set of all models in M lying below Q,3
and the verification of clauses (A)-(E) from Definition 3.1 is an easy exercise.
However, if Q is a small model, there may be models in M lying below Q
that are not members of Q. For instance, according to Proposition 2.20,
every model P ∈M such that εX∩Q ≤εP < εX, for some X ∈M ∩Q ∩L,
fails to be a member of Q. In this case, the proof requires a previous analysis
of the structure of dom(M ∩Q), which is given in Proposition 3.9 of that
same paper. We will give more details about the structure of M ∩Q in the
outline of the proof of Lemma 3.11.
