The next amalgamation lemma will be crucially used to ensure that the
forcing from Section 4, witnessing our main theorem (Theorem 1.12), has
the ℵ3-Knaster condition, provided that 2ℵ1 = ℵ2 holds in the ground model.
In [7], it appears as Lemma 3.12, and the proof is standard in the context
of forcing with symmetric systems as side conditions.
Lemma 3.10. Let n < ω and suppose that M0, . . . , Mn are (S, L)-sym-
metric systems such that Mi ∼= Mj, for all i, j ≤n. Then S
i≤n Mi is an
(S, L)-symmetric system.
The next (and last) amalgamation lemma gives one of the most important
properties of the side conditions. In the context of the pure side condition
forcing, the preservation of the cardinals ℵ1 and ℵ2 is an immediate conse-
quence of the next result (see Theorems 3.23 and 3.24 from [7]). In the case
of the forcing from Section 4, the preservation of cardinals will require a bit
more work and the next lemma will play a central role in those arguments.
In [7], the next result is divided into Lemmas 3.19 and 3.20, which corre-
spond to the cases Q ∈L and Q ∈S, respectively.
Lemma 3.11. Let M be an (S, L)-symmetric system, and let Q ∈M. Let
W be another (S, L)-symmetric system such that M ∩Q ⊆W ⊆Q. Then
there is an (S, L)-symmetric system U such that M ∪W ⊆U.
3A model P is said to lie below Q if P ∈Q[ω1].
LONG STRONG CHAINS OF SUBSETS OF ω1
13
The idea behind the proof of the above lemma is that the (S, L)-symmetric
system U witnessing the compatibility of M and W is the result of closing
M∪W under both intersections and ω1-isomorphisms. However, this needs
to be done in a very surgical way if one wants to avoid adding too many
models, which makes it somewhat hard to check whether the resulting sys-
tem is indeed an (S, L)-symmetric system. Propositions 3.14-3.18 from the
aforementioned paper were isolated precisely to capture this surgical ap-
proach to the closure of M ∪W under intersections and ω1-isomorphisms.
In a very loose way, these propositions assert that it is enough to close a
small fragment of M ∪W under intersections so that the subsequent clo-
sure under ω1-isomorphisms results in an (S, L)-symmetric system. They
rely heavily on the fact that, as Propositions 2.28-2.31 show, in the con-
text of (S, L)-symmetric systems, the operations of taking intersections and
ω1-isomorphic copies commute. We will not state them explicitly, but their
content will be included in the outline of the proof of Lemma 3.11.
If Q is a large model, the conclusion of the last amalgamation lemma
follows from two important properties of (S, L)-symmetric systems, which
appear in [7] as Propositions 3.14 and 3.18.
The first one is that, since
M ∩Q contains every model in M lying below Q, as it was observed in the
outline of the proof of Lemma 3.8 above, M ∪W is already closed under
the relevant intersections. That is, and this is Proposition 3.14 in [7], it can
be shown that every model of the form X ∩M, where M ∈M ∩S and
X ∈W ∩L ∩M, is a member of W. The second property is that, if M ∪W
is closed under intersections in the above sense, then the closure of M ∪W
under the relevant ω1-isomorphisms is an (S, L)-symmetric system. More
precisely, and this is Proposition 3.18 from the same paper,
U := M(≥εQ) ∪{ΨQ[ω1],P[ω1](W) : W ∈W, P ∈M(εQ)}
is an (S, L)-symmetric system that extends M ∪W.
However, if Q is a small model, the argument requires a much deeper
analysis of the structure of M ∪W. It is no longer the case that M ∪W is
closed under the relevant intersections, and this is due to the fact that, as
already mentioned in the sketch of the proof of Lemma 3.8, M ∩Q may not
contain all the models in M lying below Q. However, this is not the only
obstacle in the construction of an (S, L)-symmetric system extending both
M and W. Let us first describe the structure of M∩Q and how the models
