Abstract—We introduce POSME (Proof of Sequential Memory
Execution), a cryptographic primitive that enforces sustained
sequential computation via latency-bound pointer chasing over a
mutable arena. Each step reads data-dependent addresses, writes
a block whose value and causal hash are mutually dependent
(symbiotic binding), and chains the result into a global transcript.
This yields three properties: (1) Ω(K) sequential memory-step
enforcement, (2) high TMTO resistance (10× at write density
ρ=4, with a formal S · T = Ω(K2) space-time lower bound),
and (3) a tight ASIC advantage bound by DRAM random-
access latency rather than bandwidth. Benchmarks across 17
CPU platforms and 4 GPU architectures demonstrate that hash
computation is under 3.5% of step cost and GPU hardware is 14–
19× slower than a consumer CPU. POSME requires no trusted
setup and provides a foundation for verifiable delay, authorship
attestation, and Sybil resistance.
I. INTRODUCTION
Many systems require proof that a party performed sus-
tained, sequential computation under concrete resource con-
straints [18], [28], [29]: ASIC-resistant mining, authorship
attestation, Sybil resistance, and verifiable delay. Existing
primitives each capture only part of this objective.
VDFs [1] certify sequential time but are insensitive to mem-
ory; a VDF ASIC gains arbitrary speedup via faster ALUs.
PoSW [2] proves graph traversal but over static, immutable
memory. Memory-hard functions such as Argon2id [3] raise
per-evaluation cost via memory pressure, but are single-shot
primitives with no chain proof system. Composing them (e.g.,
chaining Argon2id with Merkle sampling) yields independent
properties that do not reinforce each other. Table I summarizes
the gap.
