memory-hard
by [31], introduced memory-hardness; Argon2id [3] achieves
bandwidth-hardness with ∼2× single-pass TMTO penalty and
8–16× ASIC advantage [4]. RandomX [21] targets CPU-
egalitarianism via random code execution, achieving 2–5×
ASIC bound. POSME achieves higher TMTO resistance (10×
at ρ=4) and a tighter ∼2× ASIC bound via latency-hardness
rather than bandwidth- or compute-hardness.
PoS/PoST. Proofs of Space [9] and Space-Time [2] enforce
storage over static graphs. POSME extends to a mutable arena
with per-block temporal binding. CMC. Alwen et al. [5]
formalized cumulative memory complexity for static pebbling.
POSME’s causal DAG is dynamic, requiring the new frame-
work of §III-D.
II. CONSTRUCTION
A. Design Intuition
Before presenting the formal algorithms, we outline the
three core design principles that differentiate POSME from
existing sequential primitives:
1) Data-Dependent Pointer Chasing: The address of each
subsequent read depends on the value of the current read.
This enforces a strict sequential chain that cannot be
parallelized.1
2) Mutable Arena State: Unlike static graph-based proofs,
the arena evolves at every step. Recomputing an evicted
block requires replaying its write chain (O(ρ) hashes
per miss; Theorem 2).
3) Symbiotic Causal Binding: Every write binds the
data value and its temporal write-history (causal hash)
together. This ensures that an adversary cannot forge
a single block without also forging its entire causal
lineage.
B. Hypercube Arena and Multilinear Mapping
We map the arena to a Boolean hypercube of dimension
dhc, where N = 2dhc, addressing each block by a binary
coordinate vector v ∈{0, 1}dhc. The pointer chase becomes
a data-dependent walk across hypercube vertices. The arena
state at step t is expressible as a multilinear polynomial
Pt(x1, . . . , xdhc) over F2 (or an extension tower), and each
step’s writes are low-degree evaluations. This imposes no
overhead on the physical Prover but provides the foundation
for O(1) verification via binary-field folding (§II-J).
1The hardness of parallelizing pointer chasing has a long history in
communication complexity; Nisan and Wigderson [20] initiated this line;
Viola [13] gives a recent tight Ω(n/k + k) bound for k-step pointer chasing.
