F. Arena and Initialization
The arena consists of N = 2dhc blocks on a Boolean
hypercube, each storing (data, causal), each λ=256 bits,
initialized deterministically from seed s via Algorithm 1.
Vertices are enumerated in standard binary order; the skip-link
parent of vertex v is v ≫1 (right-shift by one bit), creating
a binary DAG requiring Ω(
√
N) space to evaluate any single
vertex.
G. Step Function
Each step t executes Algorithm 2. The coordinate vj+1
depends on the block at vj; the d reads are strictly sequential.
Symbiotic binding creates bidirectional dependency: new data
incorporates the old causal hash, and the new causal hash
incorporates the cursor. Addressing uses binary projection:
the first dhc bits of the hash output yield a coordinate vector
v ∈{0, 1}dhc, selecting a vertex on the Boolean hypercube.
H. Roles of the Causal Hash
The causal hash field h serves three distinct purposes in
POSME. We enumerate them here to prevent conflation in
the security analysis that follows.
1) Operational binding (Construction, §II). Each block’s
causal hash chains its write history into the block value,
creating a per-block temporal lineage. The symbiotic
bind (Algorithm 2, line 9) ensures data and causal
hash are mutually dependent: neither can be computed
without the other.
2) Soundness amplification (Theorem 1, §III). Because
the causal hash is folded into the cursor at every read
(Algorithm 2, line 5), any forged block produces a diver-
gent cursor, which propagates into a divergent transcript
Tt. This tightens the reduction to collision resistance:
the adversary must forge both data and causal lineage
simultaneously.
3) TMTO constant factor (Theorem 2, §III-C). When the
adversary discards a block and must recompute it on a
cache miss, the write chain must be traversed for both
the data field and the causal field, doubling the per-miss
recomputation cost (2ρ vs. ρ). This is a 2× constant, not
an asymptotic improvement.
These roles are complementary but logically independent:
operational binding is a construction choice, soundness am-
plification is a proof-theoretic consequence, and the TMTO
constant is an empirical cost multiplier.
I. Classical Verification (Merkle/Fiat-Shamir)
POSME supports two verification modes. The classi-
cal mode (Algorithms 3–4) uses Fiat-Shamir [22] sampled
Merkle [23] witnesses and requires only a hash function; it is
