The per-step DRAM cost is Tmem = (d+1) × 50 ns = 450 ns.
Since Tfold/Tmem ≈2.8, three fold threads (4 threads total
on AVX-512) achieve aggregate fold throughput of 423 ns,
keeping pace with the pointer-chase. On consumer AVX2
hardware (256-bit SIMD), Tfold ≈2,540 ns, requiring 6 fold
threads.
Critically, this parallelism is asymmetric: the adversary can-
not exploit it because the pointer-chase remains strictly single-
threaded, and GPUs are 14–19× slower at the sequential
bottleneck (§IV). The fold parallelism benefits only the honest
Prover.
