Extension to adaptive strategies. Theorems 3 and 4 as-
sume the adversary fixes a storage set before execution. We
now show that adaptive caching provides no advantage under
the ROM.
Theorem 5 (Adaptive Bound). At each step t, the adaptive
adversary’s storage set St is a function of {T0, . . . , Tt−1}
and prior ROM queries. All d read addresses {v0, . . . , vd−1}
satisfy Pr[vj ∈St] = α for every j ∈[0, d−1]. The adaptive
adversary’s miss rate is identical to the static case.
Proof sketch. The address v0 is computable from Tt−1 via a
single ROM query: the adversary knows which vertex to read,
but reading it requires v0 ∈St. Since |St| = αN and v0 is
a ROM output uniform over {0, 1}dhc, Pr[v0 ∈St] = α, no
better than any other read.
For j ≥1: the cursor cj = H(cj−1∥A[vj−1].d∥A[vj−1].h)
depends on A[vj−1], which is revealed only after the (j−1)-th
read completes. The address vj = H(“addr”∥cj∥j) is therefore
a ROM query whose output is independent of St (fixed before
this query). Thus Pr[vj ∈St] = α for all j.
Lookahead. Predicting read addresses for step t+1 requires
computing Tt, which requires executing step t, which is the
sequential work being bounded. The adversary has at most
one-step lookahead, and that step’s addresses are already
conditioned on values the adversary may not possess.
Theorem 5 shows that the static-storage bounds of Theorems 3
and 4 apply without modification to adaptive adversaries. No
factor loss is incurred.
E. Vertex Coverage and Mixing
The TMTO analysis (Theorems 2–5) assumes ROM-
uniform vertex access. We now validate this assumption both
mathematically and empirically.
Theorem 6 (Vertex Coverage). In the ROM with parameters
N, K, d, ρ = K/N:
(a) The write count Wv for each vertex v converges to
Poisson(ρ) as N →∞. The read count Rv converges
to Poisson(dρ).
(b) The maximum read count satisfies maxv Rv < (1+δ) dρ
with probability ≥1 −N · e−δ2dρ/3 for 0 < δ < 1.
(c) Successive read addresses vj, vj+1 within a step are
pairwise independent in the ROM (distinct oracle
queries with distinct inputs).
Proof sketch. (a) Write addresses are ROM-uniform over N
vertices, one per step. The classical balls-in-bins Poisson
limit gives Wv →Poisson(ρ). Read addresses are similarly
uniform; d per step gives Rv →Poisson(dρ).
(b) By the multiplicative Chernoff bound, Pr[Rv
>
(1+δ)µ] ≤e−δ2µ/3 where µ = dρ. Union bounding over N
vertices, Pr[maxv Rv > (1+δ)µ] ≤Ne−δ2µ/3. For d=8, ρ=4
(µ=32), N=224, δ=1: 224 · e−32/3 ≈3.7 × 10−6 · 224 ≈62.
The probability that any vertex exceeds 2µ = 64 reads is
< 62/N; no vertex exceeds 3µ with probability > 1 −2−40.
(c) vj+1 = first dhc bits of H(“addr”∥cj+1∥(j+1)) where
cj+1
= H(cj∥A[vj].d∥A[vj].h). This is a distinct ROM
query from vj’s (different cursor input), so the outputs are
independent.
