GPU cores are optimized for parallel throughput, not se-
quential pointer-chasing. A single CUDA thread at ∼1.5 GHz
cannot compete with a CPU core at ∼3.5 GHz for sequential
dependent loads. The GPU’s memory bandwidth advantage
is irrelevant because the pointer-chase is latency-bound, not
bandwidth-bound.
The ASIC advantage (1.3× measured for HBM3 vs DDR5
random access) requires a custom sequential core paired with
HBM, hardware that does not exist commercially. All pur-
chasable accelerators (GPUs) are strictly worse than consumer
CPUs.
C. Throughput and the Capacity-Bandwidth Bound
The preceding analysis addresses per-instance latency. A
sophisticated adversary may instead target throughput: running
many independent POSME instances in parallel to amortize
the per-instance GPU penalty. GPUs hide sequential latency
behind massive memory bandwidth (e.g., 3 TB/s on the H100).
However, arena isolation (§II) forces each instance to allocate
a unique M-byte arena, converting the attack from a band-
width problem into a capacity problem.
Capacity choke. An NVIDIA H100 [32] has 80 GiB of
HBM3 VRAM. With a 1 GiB arena per instance, the GPU
accommodates at most 80 concurrent executions. At the
physical HBM3 random-access floor of ∼20 ns per 64-byte
read, each instance consumes 64 B/20 ns = 3.2 GB/s. The
80 instances collectively use ∼256 GB/s, less than 9% of
the H100’s 3 TB/s bandwidth. The GPU is memory-capacity-
bound, not bandwidth-bound.
Economic comparison. At comparable capital outlay, com-
modity DDR5 platforms provide 10–20× more aggregate
arena capacity per dollar than HBM3-equipped accelerators,
with the exact ratio depending on market conditions. The
disparity grows with arena size: a 4 GiB arena reduces an
80 GiB GPU to 20 instances while a 16 GiB desktop still runs
3.
