The classical penalty interior-point algorithm (PIPA)
3.1
Merit and infeasibility measures
For (x, y, w, z) with y, w ≥0, define
ϕ(x, y, w, z) := ∥F(x, y, w, z)∥2 + y⊤w
and, for α > 0,
Pα(x, y, w, z) := f(x, y, w, z) + α ϕ(x, y, w, z).
Thus ϕ = 0 is equivalent to lower-level feasibility plus complementarity, while Pα balances
objective reduction against movement toward feasibility.
3.2
Interior-point geometry
PIPA keeps all iterates strictly positive in the complementarity variables:
yv > 0,
wv > 0.
It also imposes the centrality condition
