Because the residual equation is affine, the trial arc
xv(r) = xv + r dxv,
yv(r) = yv + r dyv,
wv(r) = wv + r dwv
satisfies the exact identity
r(xv(r), yv(r), wv(r)) = (1 −r) rv.
Thus the affine feasibility residual decays linearly with 1 −r.
4.3
Limited complementarity decrease
The main modification relative to classical PIPA is the additional condition
(wv(r))⊤yv(r) ≥(1 −r) (wv)⊤yv.
By this requirement, a higher priority to satisfying the linear constraint q + Nx + My −w = 0 is
given than to the complementary condition y⊤w = 0; especially, complementarity is expected not
to be satisfied before feasibility. This is an elegant repair of the imbalance that can arise in the
original PIPA logic.
4.4
Why the step-size analysis is explicit
Along the arc,
w(r) ◦y(r) = w ◦y + r(w ◦dy + y ◦dw) + r2(dw ◦dy),
and the perturbed complementarity equation implies
w ◦dy + y ◦dw = −w ◦y + σµe.
Hence
w(r) ◦y(r) = (1 −r) w ◦y + r σµe + r2(dw ◦dy).
Summing components gives
w(r)⊤y(r) = (1 −r) w⊤y + σr w⊤y + r2(dw)⊤dy.
Thus both the centrality condition and the limited-complementarity condition reduce to scalar
quadratic inequalities in r. This makes the line search unusually explicit.
7
4.5
Convergence message
The global theorem for this variant has the familiar penalty-method dichotomy:
(i) if the penalty sequence blows up, one extracts stationarity information along the penalty-update
subsequence;
(ii) if the penalty stays bounded and accepted step lengths stay away from zero, then regular
accumulation points are stationary.
Compared with classical PIPA, the conceptual novelty is not the QP itself, but the line-search
rule that keeps complementarity reduction synchronized with residual reduction.
