x∈X f(x, y(x)).
5.2
Local implicit solution map
At a feasible point (x, y), suppose there exists a local Lipschitz mapping
ˆy : V ∩X →U
such that ˆy(x) = y and ˆy(ξ) ∈S(ξ) for nearby ξ. Assume further that ˆy is directionally differentiable
at x. Then the reduced directional derivative is
φ′(x; dx) = ∇xf(x, y)⊤dx + ∇yf(x, y)⊤ˆy′(x; dx).
We thus have the first-order stationarity for the reduced problem.
Definition 5.1 (Stationarity via the implicit map). A feasible point (x, y) is stationary if
∇xf(x, y)⊤dx + ∇yf(x, y)⊤ˆy′(x; dx) ≥0
∀dx ∈T(x; X).
5.3
Direction-finding model
At iterate (xν, yν), with local implicit map yν(·), define the model subproblem
min
dx
