2dx⊤Qνdx
s.t.
xν + dx ∈X.
(7)
This is a regularized first-order model of the reduced objective. Even if Qν ≻0, the problem
need not be convex, because dx 7→(yν)′(xν; dx) may be nonlinear.
8
5.4
Line search along the equilibrium path
The update is not along a straight line in (x, y)-space. Instead one uses
r 7→
␀xν + r dxν, yν(xν + r dxν)
.
Armijo backtracking is performed on this equilibrium path.
This is the conceptual hallmark of the method: lower-level feasibility is preserved by construction,
because the path remains on the graph of the solution map.
5.5
Convergence logic
The convergence theorem typically requires:
(i) polyhedrality of X;
(ii) closedness of the solution map;
(iii) uniform positive definiteness and boundedness of Qν;
(iv) boundedness of a lower level set of f;
(v) strong Fréchet differentiability of the local implicit map at the cluster point.
Under these assumptions, any cluster point is stationary.
5.6
Main practical limitation
The reduced-space formulation is elegant, but computation can be difficult:
(i) the derivative y′(x; dx) may itself require solving an auxiliary VI or complementarity problem;
(ii) each line-search trial may require a fresh lower-level solve.
So the theory is conceptually clean, but the method is expensive unless the implicit map has a
tractable derivative.
6
Piecewise SQP (PSQP)
SQP works extremely well for smooth nonlinear programs, but direct SQP on MPECs is fragile near
degenerate complementarity points. The reason is that one linearization cannot faithfully represent
a feasible region that is locally a union of several smooth pieces.
PSQP resolves this by applying SQP on one selected local branch at a time.
6.1
MPEC in KKT form
Consider an MPEC written as
min
x,y,λ
f(x, y)
s.t.
Gx + Hy + a ≤0,
λ ≥0,
g(x, y) ≤0,
L(x, y, λ) = 0,
λ⊤g(x, y) = 0,
(8)
9
where
L(x, y, λ) = F(x, y) + ∇yg(x, y)⊤λ.
At a feasible point define
I0(z, λ) := {i : gi(z) = 0 = λi},
I+(z, λ) := {i : gi(z) = 0 < λi}.
The degenerate set I0 is exactly where multiple local pieces arise.
6.2
Piecewise NLPs
For a partition J1 ∪J2 = {1, . . . , ℓ} consistent with the current complementarity regime, define the
local NLP piece
min
