gi(zv) + ∇gi(zv)⊤dz ≤0,
i ∈J1,
(λv + dλ)i ≥0,
gi(zv) + ∇gi(zv)⊤dz = 0,
i ∈J2.
(10)
This is just an SQP step — but on one selected smooth piece.
6.4
Why local convergence works
The local convergence theorem has a finite-piece argument:
(i) for each admissible local piece, ordinary SQP theory applies;
(ii) only finitely many pieces can arise near a fixed solution;
(iii) hence the local contraction estimates can be uniformized across pieces.
The result is local Q-superlinear convergence, and often Q-quadratic convergence under stronger
smoothness assumptions.
6.5
What PSQP teaches conceptually
PSQP shows that one need not regularize complementarity away in order to justify Newton/SQP-type
local behavior. One may instead respect the piecewise geometry directly.
