7.2
Strengths and weaknesses
PIPA.
Strongly structured, interior-point flavored, and conceptually appealing; but the original
global convergence theory is too optimistic.
Monotone-LCP variant.
Cleaner balance between residual reduction and complementarity
reduction; best viewed as a targeted repair of classical PIPA for an important special case.
Implicit programming.
Theoretically elegant and faithful to the lower-level equilibrium map;
computationally hard because the implicit derivative and line-search evaluations may be expensive.
PSQP.
Excellent local interpretation and fast local rates under strong assumptions; but by itself
it is a local method and does not solve the globalization problem.
