Practice Questions
8.1
True or False
(i) In the primal-dual formulation of the stationary conditions for an MPEC, the piecewise linear
map LH∗being a global Lipschitzian homeomorphism is equivalent to the pair of matrices
(W0, W1) possessing the W property.
(ii) If the partitioned matrix Q = [A
B
C] has the mixed P property, then for any subset α,
the submatrix consisting of the columns Aα, the complement columns Bα, and all columns of
C must be positive definite.
(iii) The convergence analysis for the general Penalty Interior Point Algorithm (PIPA) relies on the
strict complementarity assumption (y∗+ w∗> 0) at the limit point, a requirement that cannot
currently be removed for MPECs, despite being inessential for standard complementarity
problems.
(iv) In the alternative PIPA designed for LCP-constrained mathematical programs, the “limited
complementarity decrease” condition is enforced to ensure that the complementarity condition
(yT w = 0) is satisfied strictly before the linear feasibility constraints.
(v) Under the Global B-differentiability and Implicit Function (GBIF) assumption, the direction-
finding subproblem in the implicit programming descent algorithm is guaranteed to be a
strictly convex quadratic program because the implicit function is Lipschitz continuous.
(vi) In the implicit programming descent algorithm, if the globally optimum objective value of the
direction-finding subproblem is strictly negative, the current iterate cannot be a stationary
point of the MPEC.
(vii) A direct application of the standard Sequential Quadratic Programming (SQP) method to
an MPEC formulated with KKT constraints often fails at degenerate vectors because the
Strict Mangasarian-Fromovitz Constraint Qualification (SMFCQ) generally requires strict
complementarity to hold.
(viii) The Piecewise Sequential Quadratic Programming (PSQP) algorithm overcomes the limitations
of standard SQP by enforcing a strict complementarity assumption at the limit point to ensure
superlinear convergence.
(ix) In the convergence proof for the implicit programming descent algorithm, the tangent cone
property for polyhedra guarantees that for any direction d in the tangent cone at a point x,
taking a sufficiently small step along d from any feasible point y sufficiently close to x will
remain within the polyhedron.
(x) The PSQP method for MPECs and the Kojima-Shindo method for piecewise smooth equations
are identical in their iterative subproblems; both rely on solving a system of linear equations
obtained from a single Newton iteration on an active piece.
