Abstract—Coverage closure is the most time-consuming phase
of hardware verification, and recent large language model
(LLM)-based coding agents offer a promising approach to
automated stimulus generation. However, prior LLM-based flows
do not systematically analyze which coverage holes remain
difficult to close or how inference-time computation is allo-
cated during agentic verification. As a result, the efficiency
limits and failure modes of LLM-based coverage closure remain
poorly understood, particularly for large designs. We present
an empirical study using a two-tier agentic framework compris-
ing a base Codex agent and an enhanced domain-specialized
LangGraph system. Our framework enables a taxonomy of
coverage holes: methodology-bound ceilings (integration tied-
off hardware, infeasible boundaries, dead code) and reasoning
frontiers (protocol sequencing, multi-module pipeline warm-up,
narrow timing conditions), exposing fundamental limits of purely
LLM-driven approaches. We further instrument the system to
track token usage across six categories, including system prompt,
design comprehension, stimulus generation, coverage feedback,
error recovery, and agentic overhead. We show that domain
specialization shifts token allocation toward coverage-directed
reasoning and improves efficiency. Across designs, the enhanced
system achieves comparable or higher coverage (95–99%) while
using 4–13× fewer tokens and converging to coverage targets 2–4×
faster than a general-purpose baseline. Our results characterize
the limits of LLM-based coverage closure, inform benchmark
design and human escalation strategies, and guide profile-driven
agent design for hardware verification.
I. INTRODUCTION
Hardware verification consumes an estimated 60–70% of
chip design effort [1], often dominating engineering cost and
schedule. Within verification, coverage closure remains one
of the most time-consuming and iterative stages. Verification
engineers manually interpret specifications, draft testplans,
generate stimulus, analyze coverage reports, and refine their
approach across dozens of iterations, which can take weeks to
months for complex designs.
Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have
enabled agentic coding systems that generate non-trivial pro-
grams and interact with toolchains. Prior work has explored
LLM-assisted RTL design generation [2]–[13], as well as
verification code generation like assertions [14]–[16]. More
recently, LLM-assisted verification flows have shown itera-
tive generation of stimulus using simulator feedback [17]–
[20]. However, two key questions remain underexplored. (1)
Which coverage holes remain fundamentally difficult to close
with LLM-generated stimulus? Automatically analyzing and
classifying coverage holes enables exclusion list generation,
Fig. 1: An overview of the proposed framework
informs benchmark design, guides human escalation decisions,
and provides actionable feedback to designers and verifi-
cation engineers (e.g., updating the coverage model or the
specification). (2) Where are inference-time tokens allocated
during agentic verification? Token allocation analysis, similar
to profiling, can guide efficient agent design, including multi-
agent pipelines, dynamic model selection, and structured tool
interfaces that reduce token usage. It also enables cost opti-
mization and supports developing efficiency metrics, such as
stimulus tokens per unit coverage gained.
To study these questions, we develop CovAgent, a two-tier
agentic framework for coverage closure. The base agent is
built on the general-purpose Codex tool from OpenAI [21] and
uses prompt-based interaction with verification tools via broad
shell access. The enhanced agent is a domain-specialized
system implemented in LangGraph [22], with structured tool
interfaces and coverage-focused feedback. Fig. 1 provides
an overview of CovAgent. It consumes design specifications,
design files, and a target coverage model. It generates testcases
with the goal of achieving 100% coverage, a coverage hole
classification report (and exclusion lists for human review),
and an inference-time token allocation report.
CovAgent enables three analyses: identifying and classi-
fying residual coverage holes where LLM-generated stim-
ulus struggles, characterizing inference-time token alloca-
tion across the coverage closure workflow, and measuring
whether domain-specialized workflow (structured feedback,
constrained tool interfaces, etc.) shifts token allocation toward
productive coverage-directed work compared to a general-
purpose flow. Our contributions are:
1) CovAgent: A two-tiered agentic framework, available at
[23], for coverage closure comprising a base agent and an
enhanced domain-specialized agent, enabling comparison
of general-purpose vs. domain-adapted agentic verification.
2) Coverage hole taxonomy: A six-category classification of
residual coverage holes that reveals fundamental limits of
LLM-based stimulus generation.
arXiv:2604.15657v1 [cs.AR] 17 Apr 2026
3) Inference-time token breakdown: A breakdown of token
allocation during agentic verification into six categories,
showing that design comprehension reasoning scales super-
linearly with design size and only 12–18% of accumulated
tokens produce testbench code.
4) Empirical comparison: Domain-specialized agent yields
4–13× token reduction and equal or higher coverage (95–
99%) than the general-purpose baseline on 19 designs.
5) Model sensitivity analysis: GPT-5-mini achieves near-
equivalent outcomes on small designs but suffers up to
11pp coverage degradation on complex designs, confirming
that the framework compensates for reduced model capa-
bility only below a complexity threshold.
II. RELATED WORK
A. LLMs for Hardware Design and Verification
LLMs have recently been used for RTL generation and
debugging [2]–[12]. They have also been applied to hardware
verification tasks, including assertion synthesis, testbench con-
struction, UVM environment synthesis, and coverage-guided
stimulus generation. UVLLM [13] identifies syntactic and
functional RTL errors using an agentic pipeline, while Chi-
RAAG [14] generates assertions via interactive prompting.
AssertLLM [15] uses ChatGPT and retrieval-augmented gener-
ation to produce SystemVerilog assertions from design specifi-
cations. AutoBench [16] generates hybrid testbenches using a
pipeline and an HDLBits-derived dataset. LLM4DV [17] gen-
erates test stimulus from functional coverpoints across three
designs, and Verilog Reader [18] focuses on code-coverage-
driven testcase generation. Hassan et al. [19] propose an
iterative verification framework where LLMs play a semantic
role. Finally, Ye et al. [20] propose an agentic UVM-based
framework with a nine-design benchmark suite.
While these efforts demonstrate feasibility, prior work
leaves three gaps: (1) lack of analysis of inference-time
token allocation during agentic verification—despite prior re-
search [24], [25] showing that token allocation significantly
affects task performance; (2) failure to distinguish whether
residual coverage holes arise from structural methodology
limits or LLM reasoning failures when 100% coverage is
not achieved; and (3) absence of evaluation on large designs
representative of industrial-scale unit-level verification.
B. Agentic Coding Systems
General-purpose coding agents such as Codex CLI [21] and
other ReAct-style [26] systems demonstrate tool-augmented
reasoning and iterative program repair, combining LLM rea-
soning with tool execution-based feedback.
However, such agents are not optimized for verification
workflows and often spend tokens on environmental explo-
ration, redundant parsing, or unstructured debugging. No prior
work has developed hardware verification frameworks using
Codex and LangGraph, nor systematically studied LLM token
allocation during verification, or evaluated how domain spe-
cialization shifts tokens toward coverage-directed reasoning.
III. THE COVAGENT FRAMEWORK
A. Architecture Overview
CovAgent is an agentic framework designed to systemati-
cally study, and ultimately improve, the process of coverage
closure in hardware verification. Rather than treating LLM-
based verification as a monolithic workflow, CovAgent pro-
vides two tiers: a base tier using general-purpose OpenAI
Codex CLI, and an enhanced tier using a domain-specialized
agent implemented in LangGraph. This two-tier design enables
controlled experimentation on how architectural constraints,
tool abstractions, and feedback structure influence both cov-
erage outcomes and inference-time efficiency. At its core,
CovAgent implements coverage closure as an iterative rea-
soning loop over six stages: (1) design comprehension from
specification and top-level interface, (2) testbench synthesis
constrained to stimulus injection, (3) compilation with cov-
erage instrumentation, (4) multi-seed simulation and coverage
aggregation, (5) structured analysis of residual coverage holes,
and (6) targeted refinement of stimulus to address those holes.
This loop mimics feedback-driven coverage closure.
As shown in Fig. 2, CovAgent adopts the ReAct (Reason-
ing + Acting) [26] paradigm. In contrast to generic ReAct-
style systems, CovAgent drives this loop using a carefully
crafted system prompt that embeds knowledge of verification
methodology. This prompt establishes the agent’s role as an
expert hardware verification engineer, provides the paths to
the specification, design files, and appends the top module
header. By enforcing strict top-level stimulus constraints (i.e.
disallowing hierarchical force/release and submodule instanti-
ation), the agent is compelled to explore realistically reachable
behaviors. The prompt specifies testbench requirements (e.g.,
signal declaration rules, constrained randomization), and also
provides coverage improvement strategies.
Upon termination, the latest coverage snapshot is saved and
the agent writes a structured report. The agent is instructed
to reason about why coverage points remain uncovered,
classifying them as unreachable from top-level ports, exclud-
able (dead code), potential bugs, or needs-more-effort. This
transforms coverage closure from a binary objective into a
diagnostic process, enabling systematic exclusion generation
and providing actionable signals for human escalation. It also
informs the design of effective benchmarks for evaluating
LLM-based verification systems. Furthermore, it can guide
decisions, providing actionable feedback to designers and
verification engineers (e.g., to update coverage models).
To support quantitative analysis, CovAgent incorporates
fine-grained token-level instrumentation. Every agent in-
teraction is logged and classified into six functional cate-
gories—system prompting, design comprehension, stimulus
generation, coverage feedback, error recovery, and agentic
overhead. As we later show, this profiling reveals that effi-
ciency gains arise not merely from better models, but from
restructuring how reasoning effort is spent. Profiling token
allocation can also help identify cost-optimization techniques,
e.g., dynamically switching from smaller/cheaper LLMs to
larger, more expensive LLMs. Such analysis can also be used
Fig. 2: CovAgent Workflow
to develop efficiency metrics for evaluating agentic verifica-
tion, e.g., stimulus tokens per unit coverage. Additionally, total
token usage can help design efficient agent architectures, e.g.,
developing multi-agentic pipelines using specialized agents or
using structured tool interfaces to reduce token waste.
B. Base Agent
The base agent represents a minimally constrained instan-
tiation of agentic verification, implemented using OpenAI’s
Codex CLI. It exposes the full verification workflow through
a comprehensive system prompt that includes command tem-
plates for compilation, simulation, and coverage report gener-
ation, in addition to information in the previous sub-section.
This agent maximizes flexibility by providing broad access
to a sandboxed execution environment. It can execute arbitrary
commands, write and run auxiliary scripts, and dynamically
adjust its workflow. It can batch-generate testbenches, build
custom coverage parsers, implement feature-based stimulus
generation strategies, or develop iterative debugging pipelines.
However, this flexibility comes at a cost. The agent must
implicitly infer the structure of the verification workflow;
a significant portion of time is spent on environmental ex-
ploration, parsing, and ad hoc orchestration. The absence
of structured feedback forces the agent to reconstruct high-
level actions (e.g., prioritize coverage holes) from low-level
artifacts such as raw reports. As a result, the base agent serves
as a reference point for unconstrained agentic verification,
capturing the upper bound of generality but also exposing its
inefficiencies.
C. Enhanced Agent
While the base tier allows the agent to discover the
workflow, the enhanced tier encodes domain knowledge into
the agent through purpose-built verification tools, structured
coverage feedback, and a LangGraph state graph.
Structured Tool Interfaces. CovAgent exposes seven
tools:
read file,
write file,
list directory
for
filesystem
access;
compile design
and
run simulation
for
simula-
tor interaction; parse coverage for coverage analysis; and
run verification cycle, a composite tool that chains write,
compile, simulate, and parse into a single atomic call. By
collapsing multiple steps into a single tool call, the composite
tool reduces unnecessary deliberation and minimizes token
overhead associated with intermediate reasoning.
Fig. 3: Enhanced Agent - High Level Architecture
Structured Coverage Feedback. Instead of requiring the
agent to interpret raw coverage artifacts, the system provides
curated, prioritized information about uncovered coverage
points. This transforms coverage analysis from a low-level
parsing task into a high-level decision problem, allowing the
agent to focus its reasoning on what to do next.
LangGraph State Graph. The enhanced agent implements
the ReAct loop as a state graph with six nodes: Initialize (loads
configuration, creates work directories, builds system prompt),
Agent (invokes LLM with tools), Tools (LangGraph’s built-
in ToolNode executes tool calls), Update State (parses tool
results, updates coverage and failure trackers), Prune Context
(removes old error logs) and Finalize (injects termination
message instructing the agent to write its report).
Fig. 2 shows the concrete workflow of this enhanced
agent’s architecture. During initialization, the Router delivers
the system prompt and the Agent is given access to the
specification and design files via read file, with Update State
recording the context consumed. The workflow then enters
an iterative loop: the Agent calls run verification cycle with
its generated stimulus, the Tool Node executes the composite
write, compile, simulate, and parse pipeline atomically, and
Update State merges the new coverage database and checks
termination conditions. If cumulative coverage remains below
100% and no termination condition is met, the Router injects
structured coverage feedback and returns control to the Agent
for a refined iteration. When coverage reaches 100%, or the
agent exhausts a limit, the Router triggers the analysis phase: a
finalize prompt instructs the Agent to write a report classifying
residual coverage, after which the workflow terminates.
IV. METHODOLOGY
All experiments use GPT-5.2 as the primary LLM. The base
and enhanced agents use the same model via the OpenAI API
to ensure that any observed differences arise from workflow
rather than from model capabilities. Temperature is 0.4 for
all runs. GPT-5-mini is used for a sensitivity study with the
enhanced agent to understand the impact of model capability.
Each testbench iteration uses 5 random seeds, with cover-
age merged per-iteration and cumulatively, reflecting standard
industry verification methodology. We evaluate performance
using multiple metrics. Final coverage (%): Cumulative cov-
erage after all iterations, merged across seeds and iterations.
Total token consumption: Sum of input, output, and reasoning
