We use a heterogeneous set of 19 hardware designs spanning
∼100 to ∼8, 500 lines, with module hierarchy depth up to 5.
These include CVDP benchmark designs with LOC > 100 and
larger designs from GitHub. The designs span timer/counter,
memory, FSM, arithmetic datapath, cryptography, communica-
tion, protocol controller, image proc, and network peripherals.
Note that these designs are representative for unit level
verification in the industry. Larger designs are usually verified
at sub-system level or top-level verification environments, and
only involve stimulus generation for connectivity testing. Thus,
there is limited requirement of randomized stimulus and full
coverage at these levels. While our work shows results for unit
level verification environments, our agent is applicable to sub-
system and top-level verification as well - only the coverage
model will need to change. This is identified as future work.
V. RESULTS
A. Coverage Curves
We first present the coverage achieved by our framework
for the various designs used in our study, and analyze it
as a function of cumulative tokens (context window). Fig. 4
presents aggregated coverage curves using the enhanced tier.
Fig. 5: Average token allocation across designs.
Convergence Pattern. Small designs converge to 100%
within ∼10K–30K tokens with a steep, near-vertical rise.
Large designs require ∼55K–175K tokens and plateau at
96–99%. All curves begin with a 0% coverage region cor-
responding to tokens spent before the first testbench compiles
and simulates. For small designs this is ∼5K–10K tokens; for
large designs ∼25K–75K tokens. This corresponds to DC and
initial stimulus generation, requiring significant upfront token
investment that increases with design size.
Diminishing Returns and Last-Mile Effort Across all
designs, we observe a consistent diminishing-returns pattern.
Most designs exhibit a steep initial coverage slope, rapidly
approaching a design-specific plateau (∼95%), after which
the marginal utility of additional tokens drops sharply. Ap-
proximately 50% of accumulated tokens are utilized after
this milestone, producing only incremental gains. This mirrors
human hardware verification flows where typically, the last 5%
of coverage requires the most effort.
Reasoning Effort and Design Complexity. Scaling trends
further reveal that reasoning effort grows superlinearly with
design complexity. Larger designs require additional reasoning
for design comprehension before effective stimulus generation
can occur. When normalized by LOC, reasoning tokens per
line increase dramatically with scale: small designs require
approximately 0.1–0.3 reasoning tokens per LOC, whereas
large designs require approximately 4–8 reasoning tokens per
LOC. This behavior highlights both the strengths and the
fundamental limits of scaling LLM-driven coverage closure.
B. Inference-Time Token Allocation
We instrument six categories to understand where tokens go:
System Prompt Input, Design Comprehension (DC) (input
+ reasoning + output), Stimulus Generation (SG) (reasoning
+ output), Coverage Feedback (input), Error Recovery, and
Agentic Overhead. Fig. 5 presents two pie charts: token
allocations averaged across (a) small and (b) large designs.
Effective Artifact Ratio. Only 12–18% of accumulated
tokens correspond to SG output - the actual testbench code.
The remaining tokens are spent on comprehension, reason-
ing, feedback, and infrastructure. Iterative, coverage-directed
