TABLE II: Classification of Coverage Holes
refinement is therefore token-intensive where most tokens
support the loop rather than produce code.
Allocation Shifts with Scale. For small designs, the System
Prompt (23%), DC input (27%), and SG (30%) dominate.
Coverage Feedback and Error Recovery remain negligible
(< 5%). The agent typically reads the design, generates a
testbench, and converges within one or two iterations.
For large designs, the profile shifts substantially. The System
Prompt share drops to 2.6% (amortized with increasing con-
text). SG reasoning increases (18% →28%), DC reasoning
(∼1% →8%), and Coverage Feedback (6%) appear. Error
Recovery rises to 8%, reflecting the increased compile and
simulation failures that accompany complex designs. The
workflow becomes an iterative refinement loop rather than
a single-pass generation. Notably, DC input remains ∼26%
across scales. The model reads a similar proportion of context
but the hidden reasoning cost within DC expands 8× in share.
Reasoning Redistribution and Plateau Effects. Examin-
ing reasoning tokens alone clarifies the scaling behavior. In
small designs, 89% of reasoning tokens are devoted to SG; the
model quickly understands the design and focuses its thinking
on code synthesis. In large designs, DC absorbs 21% of the
reasoning budget, forcing the model to split effort between
understanding the hierarchy and generating stimuli. For large
designs, 24% of DC tokens are reasoning, compared to 4% for
small designs. This indicates that comprehension—not stim-
ulus synthesis—is the bottleneck (across six large designs).
The SG reasoning-to-SG output (code) ratio remains relatively
stable (∼1.5×), suggesting that code-generation deliberation
scales modestly; it is comprehension effort that explodes.
C. Coverage Hole Taxonomy
For each run, CovAgent writes a post-run report analyzing
remaining uncovered coverage points. We classify every cov-
erage hole from the sub-100% runs (multiple seeds, 6 designs)
into six categories, tagged as methodology-bound (ceiling) or
reasoning-failure (frontier). Table II shows this taxonomy.
Reasoning failures dominate on complex designs. While
aggregate numbers show a roughly even split (38 ceiling vs.
35 frontier), the distribution is sharply skewed by complexity.
Chacha has only methodology-bound holes. The complex
designs (ethmac, SD-card, trng, CAN, AXI crossbar) account
for all 35 reasoning-failure holes, concentrated in protocol
sequencing (R1). Ethmac is particularly revealing: 80% of its
Fig. 6: Comparison between CovAgent’s base and enhanced tiers
residual coverage points are reasoning failures where the agent
needed to construct Wishbone burst models, MII frame gener-
ators, and MDIO PHY responders. CAN controller reinforces
this pattern: requiring bit-accurate CAN frame generation, bit
stuffing, and BTL-aligned timing. While the agent correctly
diagnoses these problems, it fails to implement the solution
in synthesizable SystemVerilog. This gap between diagnostic
and generative capabilities is a key finding of our framework.
Methodology-bound holes define the achievable ceiling.
These holes cannot be closed without environmental changes:
exposing tied-off registers, adding DFT hooks, or formal
reachability analysis. Their small count (< 1%) confirms
the methodology-bound ceiling exceeds 98% on designs with
residual coverage holes.
The coverage hole taxonomy maps directly to industry
coverage exclusion and waiver practices. The methodology-
bound categories (M1–M3) are standard exclusion targets; the
reasoning frontiers (R1–R3) identify where human escalation
(e.g., for spec updates) or BFM development would yield the
highest return. CovAgent generates coverage point exclusion
lists that can be reviewed by human engineers and incorporated
into the verification regression environment.
D. Impact of Domain Specialization
Fig. 6 compares the results obtained by using CovAgent’s
base tier and the enhanced domain-specialized tier. (GPT-5.2).
Coverage parity with order-of-magnitude token reduc-
tion. On 13 small designs (LOC ≤778), both agents achieve
equivalent coverage. The enhanced agent uses (median) 13.4×
fewer tokens and solves designs in 1–2 iterations, while
the base agent incurs overhead from environment setup, file
discovery, and coverage parsing.
Token advantage reduces on complex designs but en-
hanced agent achieves higher coverage. On 5 large designs,
reduction drops to 2–9×. The enhanced agent achieves equal
or higher coverage on: SD-card: +4.6pp, trng: +2.6pp, eth-
mac: +0.5pp, AXI crossbar: +1.2pp and CAN: +7.5pp. The
increase is due to effective feedback that directs the agent
toward uncovered coverage points.
The token gap is driven by infrastructure overhead. The
base tier consistently utilizes 400K–1.2M total billed tokens,
while the enhanced domain-specialized agent spends 30K–
100K. This design-independent baseline confirms the overhead
Fig. 7: Coverage achieved vs. Tokens consumed for GPT-5-mini
Fig. 8: Average token allocation across designs with GPT-5-mini.
is dominated by general-purpose environment interaction, not
design-specific reasoning. The enhanced agent shifts allocation
toward reasoning while structured coverage feedback reduces
wasted tokens. Thus, domain specialization reduces unproduc-
tive exploration and accelerates convergence. This paves way
for designing custom domain-specialized pipelines for various
hardware design and verification tasks, with potentially using
smaller LLMs to reduce costs and improve efficiency.
E. LLM Size Sensitivity
We evaluate our enhanced framework on a smaller model -
GPT-5-mini. Fig. 7 shows the coverage achieved across all de-
signs. On small designs, GPT-5-mini achieves 99.6% coverage
on average, requiring ∼1.7× more tokens and more iterations
than GPT-5.2, but reliably closes coverage on most runs. On
large designs, however, the coverage degrades monotonically
with complexity. SD-card: −2.9pp. trng: −5.4pp. ethmac:
−11.3pp, AXI crossbar: −14.9pp and CAN: −29.9pp.
To understand further, we plot the token allocation break-
down. Fig 5 shows that GPT-5.2 allocates 28.1% of large-
design tokens to Stimulus Generation reasoning, whereas
Fig 8 shows GPT-5-mini allocates only 7.9% (3.5× less).
Conversely, GPT-5-mini’s code output is 15.3% vs. GPT-5.2’s
17.3%, implying that mini writes the same proportion of code
with less reasoning, producing lower-quality stimulus that fails
to close R1/R2 holes.
F. Cost-Coverage Tradeoff
Smaller models are cheaper but struggle to achieve cover-
age, while generic frameworks use more tokens and domain-
Fig. 9: Cost vs. coverage tradeoff for various execution configurations
specialized agents reduce them. We evaluate whether domain
specialization compensates for coverage loss from smaller
models. We evaluate the cost vs. coverage tradeoff in Fig.
9. It plots the coverage achieved and the dollar cost of tokens
consumed across the three experiment configurations - Base
agent with GPT 5.2, Enhanced agent with GPT 5.2, and
Enhanced agent with GPT 5-mini.
Across nearly all designs, the enhanced agent (both GPT-5.2
and GPT-5-mini) dominates the base configuration, achieving
comparable or higher coverage at significantly lower cost. The
base agent exhibits a consistently high-cost regime regardless
of design complexity, due to infrastructure overhead identified
(Section III). In contrast, the enhanced agent reduces cost by
an order of magnitude, indicating domain-specialized work-
flows eliminate non-productive token expenditure.
The
enhanced
GPT-5-mini
configuration
occupies
the
lowest-cost region of the plot while achieving near 95–100%
coverage on majority of designs. This demonstrates that
domain specialization can partially compensate for reduced
model capability. However, this trend breaks down for more
complex designs, where GPT-5-mini exhibits coverage degra-
dation (e.g., in the 70–90% range), despite comparable or
slightly higher cost. This aligns with the reasoning-frontier
limitations identified earlier—in protocol sequencing and
multi-module coordination—where insufficient reasoning ca-
pacity cannot be offset by better tooling alone.
The enhanced GPT-5.2 configuration consistently achieves
the highest coverage (97–100%) across all designs while main-
taining moderate cost. Importantly, it avoids the low-coverage
outliers observed with GPT-5-mini, indicating that higher-
capacity models are necessary to reliably close “last-mile”
coverage holes. These results suggest a hybrid deployment
strategy: For small-to-moderate designs, domain-specialized
pipelines paired with smaller models offer the best cost-
efficiency. For complex designs, smaller models can be used
for initial coverage ramp-up, but escalation to larger models
is required once coverage plateaus.
VI. CONCLUSION
We presented CovAgent, a two-tiered open-source agentic
framework for hardware verification coverage closure, en-
abling systematic study of inference-time token allocation
and coverage hole classification. Across 19 designs spanning
100-8500 LOC, our analysis yields three key findings. First,
our coverage hole taxonomy reveals that residual coverage
holes are split between methodology-bound ceilings (tied-off
hardware, infeasible boundaries, dead code) and reasoning
frontiers (protocol sequencing, pipeline warm-up, timing pre-
cision) — with protocol model construction as the dominant
remaining challenge. Second, our token allocation analysis
shows that only 12–18% of inference-time tokens produce
actual testbench code, while design comprehension reasoning
scales superlinearly with design size (30–40x from small to
large designs). Third, domain-specific workflow (annotated
source feedback, structured tools, cumulative coverage merg-
ing) yields 4–13x token reduction at coverage parity compared
to a general-purpose baseline. By exposing both the limits
and opportunities of agentic verification, CovAgent provides
a foundation for building more efficient and practically de-
ployable verification systems.",True,31682
