1. Introduction
Precise knowledge of exchange interactions is clearly a prerequisite for
understanding the magnetic properties of quantum spin systems.
Strong
exchange interactions usually exist in short M–M pairs, where M denotes a
magnetic ion such as a Cu2+ ion. According to the Kanamori–Goodenough
rule [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], an antiferromagnetic (AFM) superexchange interaction
is stronger when its M–O–M angle is closer to 180◦.
For a given crystal
structure, we infer which M–M pairs can have strong exchange interactions.
In some materials, however, a long M–M pair has an interaction on the order
of several tens of kelvin. The interaction is possible in an M–O–O–M or M–
O–A–O–M (A = nonmagnetic ion) path. Examples of such materials include
CaCuGe2O6 [7, 8], CuWO4 [9], VODPO4·1/2D2O [10], (VO)2P2O7 [11], and
β-AgCuPO4 [12]. Some theoretical results have shown that a long M–M pair
can have a large interaction [13, 14].
We have been focusing on CuSb2O6. The strongest exchange interaction
is 96 K (8.3 meV), whereas the shortest Cu–Cu bond length is 4.635 Å.
Figures 1(a) and 1(b) depict the crystal structure of CuSb2O6. The space
group is monoclinic P21/n, and the lattice constants are a = 4.6349(1) Å, b =
4.6370(1) Å, c = 9.2931(10) Å, and β = 91.124(2)◦at room temperature [15,
16]. Figures 1(c)–(e) show a CuO6 octahedron projected in three directions.
The Cu–O lengths and O–Cu–O angles are described in the caption. The
distortion of the octahedron from cubic symmetry is small [15, 16, 17]. The
small Jahn–Teller distortion indicates that the energies of the 3d3z2−r2 and
3dx2−y2 orbitals are similar to each other [18].
The AFM transition was observed at TN = 8.7 K in the temperature
T dependence of the magnetic susceptibility and speciﬁc heat [15, 21, 22].
The magnetic susceptibility at T > TN agrees well with that of the AFM
uniform spin- 1
2 chain with the exchange interaction value J = 96 K (8.3
meV) [15, 20, 22]. Here, the exchange interaction parameter J is deﬁned
in the Hamiltonian J ∑
i Si · Si+1. The magnetic susceptibility at T > TN
is nearly isotropic. The g values parallel to the a, b, and c directions were
evaluated to be ga = 2.20(1), gb = 2.20(1), and gc = 2.24(2), respectively
[22]. These g values are close to those obtained in electron-spin resonance
(ESR) measurements at 20 K (ga = 2.21, gb = 2.21, and gc = 2.23) [23].
2
Figure 1: (a)–(b) The crystal structure of CuSb2O6 [15, 16], as drawn using VESTA
[19]. The space group is monoclinic P21/n (No. 14), and the lattice constants are a =
4.6349(1) Å, b = 4.6370(1) Å, c = 9.2931(10) Å, and β = 91.124(2)◦at room temperature.
We use P21/n in this paper because it is the most commonly used space group for CuSb2O6
in related papers. In the standard notation, space group No. 14 is expressed as P21/c
with a = 4.6349(1) Å, b = 4.6370(1) Å, c = 10.3031(10) Å, and β = 115.605(2)◦. There
are one Cu2+, one Sb5+, and three O2−crystallographic sites. The gray box represents the
crystallographic unit cell. The arrows show the ordered magnetic moments [20]. (c)–(e) A
CuO6 octahedron projected in three directions. Cu–O lengths and O–Cu–O angles are also
described. The Oi–Cu–Oi (1 ≤i ≤3) angles are 180◦. (f) Candidates for the exchange
interaction forming the AFM uniform chain. The Cu–Cu length is 4.6349, 6.5562, and
6.5562 Å for the Ja, Jab, and J′
