3
As T is lowered below TN, the magnetic susceptibility parallel to the a, b,
and c directions slightly decreases, rapidly decreases, and is nearly constant,
respectively [21, 22, 24]. The spin-ﬂop ﬁeld is 1.25 T [22, 23].
The magnetic structure is shown in Figs. 1(a) and 1(b). The propagation
vector is k = (1/2, 0, 1/2) [20, 21, 25]. Kato et al. reported that the ordered
magnetic moments are parallel to the b direction [20], whereas Gibson et al.
reported that the main component of the moments is b and that a small a
component exists [21]. The direction of the ordered moments is consistent
with the anisotropic magnetic susceptibility at T < TN [21, 22, 24]. Given
the magnetic structure, candidates of the exchange interaction forming the
AFM uniform chain are the Ja, Jab, and J′
ab interactions shown in Fig. 1(f).
According to the theoretical results of Kasinathan et al. [18] and Koo and
Whangbo [26], the AFM Jab interaction is strongest.
The magnetic speciﬁc heat at T < TN is reproduced by the formula
A exp(−∆/T) with ∆= 17.5 K (1.51 meV) at zero magnetic ﬁeld, sug-
gesting the existence of an energy gap in the magnetic excitations [22]. The
corresponding nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) results show that the spin-
lattice relaxation rate (1/T1) at T < TN also exhibits gap-like behavior [20].
Both the speciﬁc heat and the linear thermal expansion coeﬃcients exhibit
distinct jumps at TN [22]. The unusual character of this phase transition sug-
gests that it is a crossover between one-dimensional antiferromagnetism and
three-dimensional antiferromagnetic order possibly associated with a spin-
Peierls transition [27, 28, 29].
It is important to determine which exchange interaction forms the AFM
uniform chain and to conﬁrm whether the gap exists in the magnetic exci-
tations. If the gap exists, it is necessary to investigate its origin. We should
conﬁrm whether or not the gap can be explained by some anisotropy or we
should consider another origin such as the spin-Peierls transition. Conse-
quently, we carried out inelastic neutron scattering (INS) experiments on
CuSb2O6 powder.
2. Experimental and Calculation Methods
We synthesized crystalline powder of CuSb2O6 via a solid-state reaction.
The starting materials were CuO and Sb2O5 powders with 99.99% purity.
A stoichiometric mixture of the powders was sintered at 1273 K in air with
intermediate grinding. We recorded the X-ray powder diﬀraction patterns for
4
the product at room temperature using an X-ray diﬀractometer (RINT-TTR
III; Rigaku) and conﬁrmed the formation of the target compound.
We carried out INS measurements using the High-Resolution Chopper
(HRC) spectrometer at beamline BL 12 in the Japan Proton Accelerator
Research Complex (J-PARC) [30, 31, 32]. We calculated dispersion relations
of spin-wave excitations using linear spin-wave theory realized in the SpinW
program package [33].
3. Results and Discussion
Figure 2 shows the INS intensity I(Q, ω) map for CuSb2O6 powder, where
Q and ω are the magnitude of the scattering vector and the energy transfer,
respectively. The energy of the incident neutrons (Ei) is 11.4 meV in Figs.
2(a) and 2(c) and 51.0 meV in Figs. 2(b) and 2(d). Excitations in the energy
range 1.8 ≤ω ≤13 meV are observed at 2.5 K [Figs. 2(a) and 2(b)]. The
excitations at ω ≈13 meV are magnetic ones at the zone boundary of the
AFM uniform chain because the value of π
2J is 13 meV [34]. No excitation is
observed below 1.8 meV, indicating the existence of an excitation gap. This
gap value (1.8 meV) is close to that evaluated from the speciﬁc heat (1.51
meV) [22]. As shown in Fig. 2(c), the excitations at 12.5 K (> TN = 8.7 K)
appear to be gapless.
We infer that the 1.8 meV gap is caused by some
anisotropy in spin-wave excitations. The gap excitations are strongest at
Qzc ≈0.48 Å−1, corresponding to a Cu–Cu length of 6.6 Å. This result is
consistent with the theoretical one indicating that Jab, for which the Cu–Cu
length is 6.5562 Å, is strongest [18, 26]. The excitations at 1.8 meV exist at
Q > Qzc because signals at Q > Qzc can include the excitation at Qzc in the
powder sample.
We calculated spin-wave excitations for the following AFM uniform XXZ
chain using the SpinW program package [33]:
