anisotropy is generated by spin–orbit coupling even for S = 1/2 spins. Spin–
orbit coupling has been suggested to exist in CuSb2O6 [22]. Thus, single-
ion anisotropy in CuSb2O6 may be possible, However, as described in the
Introduction, the anisotropy in real space is small.
4. Summary
We performed INS experiments on a powdered sample of the AFM uni-
form spin- 1
2 chain compound CuSb2O6. We observed the magnetic excita-
tions in the energy range 1.8 ≤ω ≤13 meV at 2.5 K (< TN = 8.7 K). This
gap value (1.8 meV) is close to that determined from the speciﬁc heat (1.51
meV). The excitations at 12.5 K (> TN) appear to be gapless. Therefore, the
1.8 meV gap is generated by some anisotropy in spin-wave excitations. The
gap excitations are strongest at Qzc ≈0.48 Å−1, corresponding to a Cu–Cu
length of 6.6 Å. This result is consistent with the theoretical one showing
that Jab, for which the Cu–Cu length is 6.5562 Å, is strongest. The excita-
