Hence, more oscillatory βj (increasing bo −be > 0) leads
to faster power-law decay. The exponent γ, thus, even-
tually crosses γc = 1 for large enough J⊥.
The oscillations in βj, therefore, are an essential fea-
ture of the localization of the magnetic domains (to-
gether with the vanishing of the diagonal elements αj),
akin to recent analyses of Anderson localization [61].
Note that the Lanczos coefficients βj are proportional
to ∼∥ˆH |Kj−1⟩∥2 such that βj relates to the mobility
of the state |Kj−1⟩. Hence, the localization can be seen
as an intriguing consequence of structured mobility, by
which every odd state |K2j−1⟩, associated with β2j, is
comparatively less mobile than the next even state |K2j⟩,
associated with β2j+1. Indeed, for the odd states, rung-
ferromagnetic configurations tend to appear. This has
suppressed mobility because the rungs contain identical
spins, rendering them inactive under ˆH. Conversely, if
the initial state has a single antiferromagnetic rung, the
behavior flips, bo < be, and the formed zero mode is de-
localized (see End Matter).
