When the rung coupling ∆⊥is turned on, the large zero
mode degeneracy is lifted, but many low-entanglement
states remain. As a result, the time evolution features ex-
tremely long lived revivals in the magnetization dynam-
ics [Fig. 4(d)] with a frequency that increases with the
length of the system. This phenomenology bears close
resemblance to the quantum many-body scars observed
in Rydberg arrays [26, 28], related to the PXP model
[66, 67]. Indeed, we identify a set of scar states [60] re-
lated to so-called cages [68–70] that persist even in the
presence of ZZ rung couplings, ∆⊥.
These scars bear
no significance, however, for the non-ergodic magnetic
domains at ∆⊥= 0.
Finally, in the full XXZ model
with nonzero ∆≡∆⊥= ∆∥, the scar states disappear,
and the magnetization thermalizes to mz(x) = 0 on a
timescale (∆J∥)−1 [Fig. 4(f)].
