1. INTRODUCTION
The Crab pulsar (PSR B0531+21) is one of the most
studied sources in the X-ray sky, with its bright, stable,
double-peaked pulse profile comprising the main pulse
(P1), inter-pulse (P2), bridge (B) and off-pulse (OP)
phases. While the details of these structures can dif-
fer slightly in different energy ranges, such as the rela-
tive heights of P1 and P2, they serve as a precise phase
reference across energy bands (R. B. Wilson & G. J.
Fishman 1983). Despite its status as a “standard can-
dle” in the soft and hard X-ray bands, many questions,
such as the origin of the high energy emission remain.
Current models have the X-ray emission originating in
the magnetosphere: a rapidly rotating, strongly mag-
netized plasma environment that accelerates particles
to ultra-relativistic speeds. These particles emit radi-
ation across the electromagnetic spectrum, modulated
for an observer by the pulsar’s rotation and shaped
by complex electromagnetic field structures.
The po-
larization degree (PD) and polarization angle (PA) of
the high energy emission, especially when resolved as
a function of rotational phase, can distinguish between
competing emission geometries, such as those predicted
by outer-gap, slot-gap, and striped wind models, and
test if the X-rays originate near the magnetic poles, in
caustic magnetospheric regions, or from the wind ter-
mination shock
(J. P´etri 2013; A. K. Harding & C.
Kalapotharakos 2017; A. N. Timokhin & A. K. Hard-
ing 2019; C. Kalapotharakos et al. 2012; D. Gonz´alez-
Caniulef et al. 2025).
In the hard X-ray regime, the
photon flux from the nebula originates primarily from
ultra-relativistic electrons in the inner torus and knot re-
gions, where magnetic fields are predominantly toroidal
and highly ordered (M. C. Weisskopf et al. 2000; C.-Y.
Ng & R. W. Romani 2004). Polarization measurements
in the hard X-ray energy range therefore serve as diag-
nostics of magnetic-field geometry and turbulence in the
post-shock flow.
Recent IXPE observations (N. Bucciantini et al. 2023;
J. Wong et al. 2024; W. Wei et al. 2025), in the 2 – 8 keV
energy range, have revealed spatial and energy resolved
polarization across the nebula, with angles aligning to
the pulsar spin axis at higher energies (W. Wei et al.
2025). Extending these measurements to higher ener-
gies with XL-Calibur allows us to test whether the mag-
netic ordering persists in the population of freshly ac-
celerated electrons responsible for the hard-X-ray emis-
sion (H. Awaki et al. 2025a). Comparison of PD and
PA with those measured by IXPE can constrain the en-
ergy dependence of the degree of field uniformity, and
the efficiency of synchrotron cooling close to the termi-
nation shock (T. Mizuno et al. 2023; W. Wei et al. 2025;
Q. Abarr et al. 2021).
In the soft to medium energy
gamma-ray regime (>100 keV), polarization measure-
ments of the Crab have been obtained with INTEGRAL
and AstroSat. INTEGRAL/IBIS observations report a
strong polarization in the 200keV to 1MeV band with
the polarization aligned with the pulsar spin axis (T.
Bouchet et al. 2026). Similarly, measurements with As-
troSat in the 100 – 380 keV range detect a high polariza-
tion fraction consistent with synchrotron emission from
the inner nebula (S. V. Vadawale et al. 2018). These
measurements are broadly consistent with results from
PoGO+, which reported a PD of 20.9 ± 5.0% from the
Crab in the 18 – 160 keV band, comparable to the energy
range probed by XL-Calibur (M. Chauvin et al. 2017,
2018). Polarimetry in the XL-Calibur, ∼19 – 64 keV,
energy range can further probe synchrotron emission to
offer crucial insight into the particle acceleration pro-
cesses and emissions closest to the pulsar.
The balloon-borne mission XL-Calibur offers a high
signal-to-background platform for hard X-ray polarime-
try in the ∼19 – 64 keV energy range, where the Crab
is one of the brightest astrophysical sources (H. Awaki
et al. 2025a). XL-Calibur is a Compton polarimeter that
achieves polarization sensitivity by measuring the az-
imuthal scattering angles of incoming photons (Q. Abarr
et al. 2021). During its 2024 flight, the instrument ob-
served the Crab nebula and pulsar for a total source
exposure of ∼50 ksec and an additional ∼17 ksec of in-
terspersed background pointing, to extract both phase-
integrated and phase-resolved polarization signatures.
Additional observations of Cyg X−1 occurred while the
Crab was below the horizon. Initial results of the Crab
and Cyg X−1 are presented in H. Awaki et al. (2025a,b)
respectively.
However, due to intermittent failures of
the GPS system on the payload, approximately 38 % of
the total on-source Crab events lacked valid GPS event
timestamps, where an internal oscillator is used for time-
tagging instead, which lacked sufficient stability for a
phase-resolved study. As a result, this data originally
had to be excluded from the phase-resolved analysis (H.
Awaki et al. 2025a).
The focus of this paper is to detail a method to reclaim
the “GPS-off” fraction of the dataset, using on-board
clocks and the Crab pulse profile derived directly from
