Then, for every ∆, we find a CFT at the RG fixed point (βg = 0), where the scaling
dimension of ϕ is forced by the nonlocal kinetic term to be exactly ∆. The price we pay
is the loss of locality. We get a continuous family of CFTs parametrised by ∆, but the
fractional derivative makes the theory no longer local, as it no longer has a local energy-
momentum tensor of dimension ∆T = d; we call them nonlocal CFTs or long-range CFTs.
