This long-range construction works for any number nf of fermions or bosons, giving
an nf-dimensional family of nonlocal CFTs parametrised by {∆i}, which at discrete points
become local CFTs. We therefore state our result for this maximally general case. We
freely consider nonunitary and complex CFTs, to avoid CFTs apparently popping in and
out of existence as we vary d and ∆ϕ, and assume conformal invariance of these LR CFTs.
The proof of F-extremisation is remarkably simple, and follows rapidly in section 4 once
we have set the problem up correctly: at a conformal fixed point, the derivative of ˜F with
respect to ∆receives contributions only from the nonlocal term in the action, essentially
because one-point functions of local operators vanish. Manifestly, this nonlocal term must
be absent in the local limit4 – so the derivative vanishes, and we are at an extremum.
The proof of maximisation is more laborious, and requires second-order conformal
perturbation theory about the local CFT. Despite this, for the unitary theories, we can
offer some fuzzy intuition for maximisation, using the fact that ˜F is a weak C-function: if
˜F measures the number of effective degrees of freedom, then the local theory is the one with
the largest ˜F, as the values of the quantum fields are less dependent on what is happening
at separated points, because the action only couples adjacent sites.
3We provide further commentary on fig. 1 here. The two CFTs cross (and are identical) at d/4, where
ϕ4 becomes marginally irrelevant – then ϕ4 becomes irrelevant in the GFF for ∆> d/4. The generalised
F-theorem states that it is only possible to flow from higher to lower on this plot. This makes it manifest
why the scaling limit of the lattice long-range Ising gives mean-field behaviour for s < d
2, i.e. ∆> d/4; it is
no longer possible to flow to the WF theory. As we take d →4, the two local CFTs slide to ∆ϕ = d/4 →1
and merge, due to the triviality of ϕ4 theory in d = 4. For numerical values in d = 3, see section 6.2.2.
4An important caveat: we make the consistent choice to always define ˜F without the contribution of
the auxiliary generalised free field χ that implements nonlocality (see section 4.3 for a justification).
– 2 –
The free energy of these nonlocal theories therefore provides a nice way to identify the
local CFTs contained in a given long-range (LR) CFT. It also simplifies the presentation
of the conformal data in the cases that have been computed so far; see, for example, the
large-N case presented in section 2 below. It is similar to supersymmetric ˜F-maximisation,
which is the general-d version of c, F, a-maximisation that arises in SCFTs with sufficient
supersymmetry (reviewed below).
However, unlike it, we stress that we still ultimately
require perturbative control to compute the conformal data of the local CFTs – e.g. a large
N or a small ϵ. The general lesson that we find throughout, though, is that it can be useful
to step back and consider broader nonlocal and nonunitary families of CFTs, even when
our ultimate goal is understanding the local and unitary CFTs.
The rest of this paper proceeds as follows:
2. In section 2, we give some inspiration for how one might notice this phenomenon,
as well as definitions and disambiguations of ˜F and F, Weyl covariance, conformal
families, locality, and supersymmetric ˜F-maximisation/extremisation.
3. In section 3, we discuss the two known ways of creating long-range CFTs, which we
term GFF+local and CFT+ˆϕχ.
4. In section 4, we prove that F is extremised if and only if the LR CFT becomes local,
by requiring locality of the action, as sketched above. We assume conformal invariance
of the LR fixed points, and deliberately remove the contribution of the GFF χ.
5. In section 5, we prove maximisation for unitary theories. We first extend the gener-
alised F-theorem in conformal perturbation theory (CPT) to second order. This is
used to show that if a given unitary local CFT can be deformed to a line of unitary
long-range CFTs, then ˜F is locally maximised by the local CFT on that line (and the
Hessian is negative definite assuming IR stability). To apply this to the GFF+local
construction, we must assume the duality of the two LR constructions. We check
with the free scalar, and also find a superior normalization for ˜F, which we call ˆF.
6. In section 6, we provide checks of our result. It can be shown in interacting theories
where we have perturbative control: this has been done for the large-N expansion of
the vector models to order 1/N 2 [2]. We therefore demonstrate it for the Wilson-Fisher
ϵ expansion in the long-range O(N) ϕ4 and (diagonal) σϕiϕi + σ3 cubic theories.
7. We close by discussing our results in section 7.
The manifold appendices contain supplementary material: section A contains our con-
ventions; section B contains an alternate proof of a construction used in our proof; sec-
tion C and section D contain a proof of the multi-operator generalisation of the generalised
F-theorem and gradient flow in CPT to second order; section E contains the free energy
diagrams for the long-range ϕ4 theory, including a simplified form for a complicated double
sum; section F shows the improvement of extrapolation between dimensions when using
our new normalization ˆF for the free energy; section G contains a review of the spectral
continuity of the long-range Ising in the local limit and of the flows between the various
long-range CFTs in the Ising model setting.
