(2.11)
is not a marginal local operator (it is definitely not local, for one thing). Our families are
then continuous parametrisations of conformal fixed points in the sense of the ϵ expansion
or the large-N limit, rather than being conformal manifolds10.
Weyl covariance. When we say “the CFT”, we refer to the CFT defined on arbitrary
manifolds, not just in flat space. We demand Weyl invariance of the CFT, meaning that we
can compute all correlators on the sphere just by Weyl mapping from flat space, and those
correlators transform covariantly (see [53–55] for more discussion of this). As discussed in
[35], this is possible for generic (real) d, except in even d where it is sometimes possible
for local CFTs and never possible for nonlocal CFTs due to Weyl obstructions (not to be
confused with Weyl anomalies, which do not prevent Weyl mapping CFTs [35, §2.3.2] [56,
§4.2] [57]). The consequence is that ˜F is a number that we can use to characterise the CFT
independently of the manifold.
The vector SCFT and ˜F-extremisation/maximisation. Our story is very similar
