since this holds in all initial states and 𝑎(·) remains unchanged. Formally, this can be verified by
checking that 𝜄⇒∃𝑥.¬𝑎(𝑥), and that ¬𝑎(𝑥), which is a formula with a free variable 𝑥, is forward-
preserved, i.e., ¬𝑎(𝑥) ∧𝜏⇒¬𝑎′(𝑥). Since these verification conditions are valid, on forward traces
from 𝜄it is sound to assume that there exists some witness 𝑤which satisfies ¬𝑎(𝑥). Next, we can
add 𝑤as an unchanging witness to the safety problem along with the axiom 𝜑1 = ¬𝑎(𝑤), resulting
in an equivalently safe safety problem.
Under this prophecy assumption, the transition 𝜏becomes disabled for 𝑥= 𝑤, and so the formula
𝜑2 = ∃𝑦.¬𝑑(𝑤,𝑦) is backward inductive. Taken together 𝜑1 ∧𝜑2 = ¬𝑎(𝑤) ∧∃𝑦.¬𝑑(𝑤,𝑦) implies
¬𝜄, which means that the problem is safe. Note that this proof used only existentially quantified
auxiliary invariants, with a single quantified variable and no Boolean connectives. However, we
proved a property whose safe inductive invariant contained quantifier nesting and alternations.
