complete, we also include an inference rule that allows strengthening the bad states (i.e., weakening
the proven invariant), which preserves safety.
Definition 4.1. The forward proof system F consists of the following proof rules:
(Ind)
𝜄⇒𝜑, 𝜑∧𝜏⇒𝜑′
(𝜄,𝜏, ¬𝜑)
(𝜄,𝜏, ¬𝜑)
(Cons)
𝜑⇒¬𝛽
(𝜄,𝜏, 𝛽)
For a class of predicates P, we denote by FP the system F where applications of (Ind) are restricted
to 𝜑∈P.
Theorem 4.2 (Soundness). Every safety problem that has an F-proof is safe.
Proof. All inference rules of F are sound.
□
Note that all F-proofs can be collapsed into the following form, by collapsing consecutive
consequence rules:
(Ind)
𝜄⇒𝜑, 𝜑∧𝜏⇒𝜑′
(𝜄,𝜏, ¬𝜑)
(Cons)
𝜑⇒¬𝛽
(𝜄,𝜏, 𝛽)
Thus the F proof system has no meaningful way to simplify proofs by dividing them into multiple
applications of the induction rule. To allow such simplifications we introduce incremental proofs.
Incremental proofs may use an additional proof rule that allows adding previously proved invariants
as axioms of the safety problem. That is, in order to prove a safety problem Π = (𝜄,𝜏, 𝛽) safe, the
incremental proof rule requires proving that (1) a predicate 𝜑is an invariant with respect to 𝜄and
𝜏, and (2) the problem Π, when restricted to states that satisfy 𝜑, is safe.
Definition 4.3. The incremental forward proof system FI consists of the proof rules of F (Defini-
tion 4.1), and the additional incremental proof rule:
(𝜄,𝜏, ¬𝜑)
(𝜄∧𝜑,𝜏∧𝜑∧𝜑′, 𝛽∧𝜑)
(Inc)
(𝜄,𝜏, 𝛽)
For a class of predicates P, we denote by FIP the system FI where applications of (Ind) are restricted
to 𝜑∈P, and by FIP
𝑛the system where proofs use at most 𝑛applications of (Ind).
Theorem 4.4 (Soundness). Every safety problem that has an FI-proof is safe.
Proof. All inference rules of FI are sound.
□
Like the rest of the proof systems presented in this paper, an incremental FI-proof of a safety
problem can always be converted into a safe inductive invariant for the same problem. This is
another way to prove soundness theorems like Theorems 4.2 and 4.4, but can also be used to reason
about how expressive inductive invariants need to be to match the proof power of a given proof
system. In the case of incremental forward proofs FIP, each proof can be converted into a safe
inductive invariant in the form of a conjunction over P, where the number of conjuncts precisely
corresponds to the number of (Ind) rules used in the proof. This conversion is given below.
Definition 4.5. For every proof 𝑃∈FI define Inv→(𝑃) by induction on proof structure:
• if the root of 𝑃is a conclusion of (Ind), denoted (𝜄,𝜏, ¬𝜑), then Inv→(𝑃) = 𝜑;
