Using this approach, different classes of prophecy can be described and their explicit verification
conditions written down and verified. These can be used in proofs as prophecy short-cuts via the
appropriate derived inference rules, e.g., (Proph-Fwd) for forward-preserved prophecy formulas.
When searching for a proof, these can be used to constrain the kinds of prophecy considered, and
to supply explicit verification conditions for each.
We present one more prophecy pattern which turns out to be central to our evaluation in
Section 6.2. This prophecy considers a predicate 𝜃that is forward-preserved, and thus partitions
any trace of the program into an initial part where all states satisfy ¬𝜃, and a tail where all elements
satisfy 𝜃. This must be the case because once 𝜃holds at some point in the trace it must hold
thereafter, since it is forward-preserved. The added prophecy 𝜑(𝑥) has the property that it holds
for all elements before 𝜃holds; that it is forward preserved for any individual element once 𝜃holds;
and that ∃𝑥.𝜑(𝑥) is itself an inductive invariant. Intuitively, we can think of 𝜑(𝑥) as a property
that initially holds for all elements of a state until 𝜃is activated, upon which (at least) one element
is “selected” and continues to satisfy 𝜑(𝑥) the entire length of the trace. The verification conditions
detailed above translate to the following inference rule:
