and is instead introduced as a fresh prophecy witness by (Proph-Select), which uses 𝜃= decision(𝑟1, 𝑣1).
That is, the invariant states that if 𝑣2 is proposed at any round greater than 𝑟1, then 𝑣1 is no longer
choosable at 𝑟1 (i.e., in every quorum there is a node that is at a round greater than 𝑟1 and did
not vote for 𝑣1 at 𝑟1). Of all of the invariants in the proof of Paxos, this one has the most complex
Boolean structure. Note that it is not a clause because of the alternation of ∨and ∧. Interestingly, a
forward-backward proof can replace this invariant with simpler predicates. The key point is that
in a forward-only proof, we must represent the disjunction between 𝑣2 being proposed (at any
round greater than 𝑟1) and 𝑣1 being choosable (at 𝑟1). However, in a forward-backward proof we
can eliminate the disjunction, since we know that in a bad state there must be a decision on 𝑣1 at
𝑟1, so it must be choosable at every point along the path from init to bad.
Formally, we first argue by a forward proof step that a decision is supported by a quorum of
votes (as is done in the forward-only proof), focusing on 𝑟1 and 𝑣1:
decision(𝑟1, 𝑣1) →∃𝑞: quorum.∀𝑛: node. member(𝑛,𝑞) →vote_msg(𝑛,𝑟1, 𝑣1).
(2)
This step corresponds to the first application of (Inc) in Figure 2a, starting from the root, where 𝜑1
is eq. (2) above. This proof rule results in a left-hand premise proved by forward induction, and a
right-hand premise where eq. (2) is assumed as an axiom (see Definition 4.3).
Next, to prove the right-hand premise, we argue with a backward proof step that on any path to
the bad states 𝑣1 must always be choosable at 𝑟1:
∃𝑞: quorum∀𝑛: node. member(𝑛,𝑞) ∧current_round(𝑛) > 𝑟1 →vote_msg(𝑛,𝑟1, 𝑣1).
(3)
This step corresponds to the application of (B-Inc) in Figure 2a, where 𝜑2 is eq. (3) above, and the
left-hand premise is proved via backward induction.
Finally, using a forward step we can show that in the transition system resulting from assuming
eqs. (2) and (3), 𝑣2 cannot be proposed at any round greater than 𝑟1:
∀𝑟: round. 𝑟1 < 𝑟→¬propose_msg(𝑟, 𝑣2).
(4)
This easily leads to the ultimate safety proof. Formally, this last step corresponds to proving the
forward inductive invariant 𝜑3 in Figure 2a, which consists of eq. (4) and a few supporting invariants.
