correct assembly for the toddler bed in Section 4.
4
Demo Scenario
Generating Dynamic CAD Models. For our demonstration, we
tested whether AADvark could generate a dynamic CAD model for
a pair of scissors. The input to AADvark was two images. The first
image showed a pair of scissors in a closed state and the second
image showed them in an open state (left column of Figure 2).
AADvark initialized an assembly with a simple base rectangle and
then began to iteratively modify the assembly.
After four iterations (the 20% progress column in Figure 2) AAD-
vark already had modified the scissors assembly to resemble the
input image(s), including by properly constructing the revolute
joint to enable a cutting motion. However, the handles did not have
holes for fingers. AADvark then spent the majority of its iterations
modifying the handles to contain holes for fingers, before finally
accomplishing this task in iteration 20. On average, each iteration
took 745 seconds, cost $0.79, and processed 914k input tokens and
111k output (and thinking) tokens across 23.4 LLM calls. In total, it
took 4.14 hours, cost $15.85, and processed 18.2M input tokens and
2.2M output tokens across 468 LLM calls.
Generating Static CAD Models. As a second test, we sought to
demonstrate that AADvark could also generate 3D assemblies for a
diverse set of static objects, some of which were generated by prior
work [53]. We provided the system with images of a toddler bed,
chair, end table, carport, and a standing dry erase board. For each
object we provided 1-3 input images. To probe AADvark’s ability
to handle natural language specifications, we additionally provided
