where |∇kh| = O(r−τ−k) as r →∞and the norm || and ∇is with respect to the asymptotic
flat metric dr2 + r2γ. It is worth noting that Ricci-flatness implies τ = 4 [7].
Definition 2.3. A Riemannian manifold (M, g) is ALF if [6]:
• It has an end diffeomorphic to R × S where S is either S1 × S2 or S3/Γ and Γ is a finite
subgroup of O(4).
• S admits a 1-form η, a vector field ξ such that η(ξ) = 1, ιξdη = 0, and a ξ-invariant metric
γ on ker η of constant curvature +1 extended to TS so γ(ξ, ·) = 0.
• On the end
(2.5)
g = dr2 + r2γ + η2 + h
where |∇kh| = O(r−1−k) as r →∞and the norm || and ∇is with respect to the asymptotic
metric dr2 + r2γ + η2. These conditions imply ξ is a Killing field of this asymptotic metric.
We must now define what it means for a toric instanton to be one of these asymptotic types, which
requires a compatibility condition of the asymptotic structures with the toric symmetry.
Definition 2.4. A Riemannian manifold (M, g) that is toric (i.e.
admits an effective torus T
isometric action) is:
• Toric ALE if the T-action is an action on S that preserves γ.
• Toric ALF if the T-action is an action on S that preserves (η, ξ, γ) and ξ generates a subgroup
of the T-action.
The torus action implies Γ must be a cyclic group so in the spherical case S is a lens space L(p, q).
In order to make use of the harmonic map formulation of the Einstein equations, we need to
establish that Weyl coordinates are global in our context. The proof follows an old argument of
Weinstein for stationary and axisymmetric black holes [32].
Proposition 2.5. Let (M, g) be a toric ALE or ALF gravitational instanton.
Then, the Weyl-
