2.2. Toric ALF instantons. It will be useful to introduce an explicit set of coordinates adapted
to the asymptotic types introduced. In the ALE case this is straightforward and will be postponed
to the next section. In the ALF case this requires unpacking its general definition. The following
result can also be found in [6] (see also [1]).
Proposition 2.6. Let (M, g) be an ALF instanton. There exist coordinates (r, θ, ϕ, τ) on the as-
ymptotic end such that the metric takes the form
g = dτ 2 + dr2 + r2(dθ2 + sin2 θdϕ2) + O(r−1),
as r →∞. If (M, g) is toric ALF then ξ = ∂τ is a Killing vector field and we may choose coordinates
on S so that K = ∂ϕ is also a Killing vector.
Proof. First note that the definition of ALF implies Lξη = 0. Now let (τ, θ, ϕ) be coordinates on S
adapted so ξ = ∂τ. It follows from η(ξ) = 1 that in these coordinates η = dτ + ω where ιξω = 0
and Lξω = 0, so we can write ω = ωθ(θ, ϕ)dθ + ωϕ(θ, ϕ)dϕ. Now consider the symmetric 2-tensor
γ on S which by definition satisfies γ(ξ, ·) = 0 and is also invariant under ξ. In these coordinates
γ = γθθdθ2 + 2γθϕdθdϕ + γϕϕdϕ2 where all components depend on (θ, ϕ), so locally is takes the form
of a metric on a 2d space with coordinates (θ, ϕ). It thus gives a metric on ker η and the requirements
it has unit curvature implies it is locally isometric to the unit round metric on S2. Thus we can
always choose the coordinates so γ = dθ2 + sin2 θdϕ2. The asymptotic form of the metric is thus
g = dr2 + r2(dθ2 + sin2 θdϕ2) + (dτ + ωθ(θ, ϕ)dθ + ωϕ(θ, ϕ)dϕ)2 + O(r−1)
Recall that here O(r−1) refers to the norm with respect to the asymptotic metric dr2 + r2γ + η2.
However, since ωθ, ωϕ are O(1) the 1-form ω = O(r−1) and hence the claim follows.
□
