Riemannian analogues of the mass and angular momentum.
Similarly, Proposition 2.7 shows that in the ALF case we have asymptotic invariants N, m, j which
are natural to identify as Riemannian analogues of the ‘NUT charge’, the mass and the angular
momentum. Minerbe [26] has defined a mass for ALF manifolds (not necessarily toric or Ricci flat),
although he assumed that the circle at infinity has bounded length (so in the AF case Ω= 0). In
contrast we have obtained a mass for toric ALF instantons where we do not assume the circle at
infinity is bounded. It would be interesting to define a more general mass for ALF manifolds that
unifies these two definitions.
2.3. Toric ALE instantons. We now introduce coordinates adapted to the ALE case. This is
simpler since the cross-section at infinity (S, γ) is given by S = L(p, q) with γ maximally symmetric
with unit curvature, so it must be locally isometric to the unit round metric on S3. Therefore, the
metric at infinity dr2 + r2γ is a flat cone. There are of course many coordinate systems adapted to
the toric symmetry one could use, but perhaps one of the simplest are the Hopf coordinates which
ON THE EXISTENCE OF TORIC ALE AND ALF GRAVITATIONAL INSTANTONS
