3. Harmonic map formulation
3.1. Uniqueness and existence. We may reformulate toric gravitational instantons as harmonic
maps, following [19]. Define Φ = ρ−1g so that det Φ = 1. Then, by harmonicity of ρ,
(3.1)
d(ρˆ⋆Φ−1dΦ) = 0 .
In terms of Weyl-Papapetrou coordinates this can be written as
(3.2)
∇· (Φ−1∇Φ) = 0
where ∇is the gradient operator on R3 with Euclidean metric δ = dρ2 + ρ2dφ2 + dz2 and Φ is
an axisymmetric function on R3\Γ where Γ is the z-axis (note φ here is an auxiliary coordinate
and axisymmetry of Φ means ∂φΦ = 0). Thus Φ : R3\Γ →N is a harmonic map, where N =
SL(2, R)/SO(2) is a symmetric space. The tension of such a map is defined by
τ(Φ) = Φ∇· (Φ−1∇Φ)
and hence it is harmonic iff the tension vanishes. Its norm squared with respect to the target space
metric is
(3.3)
|τ(Φ)|2 = Tr(Φ−1τΦ−1τ) = Tr[(∇· (Φ−1∇Φ))2]
Therefore, we wish to address uniqueness and existence of such harmonic maps with a prescribed
(singular) boundary conditions on the axis Γ.
Fortunately, there is a well developed theory for
harmonic maps with target spaces that include N, which we may exploit.
First, we establish the uniqueness part of Theorem 1.1. This is much simpler and is essentially
the same argument used to prove the black hole uniqueness theorem, see e.g. [15].
