3.3
Ablation Studies
3.3.1
Component Ablation (RQ2)
We systematically remove one component at a time from IG-Search framework. All ablations use
Qwen2.5-3B-Base. Results are presented in Table 2.
Removing the IG reward entirely reduces the framework to a trajectory-level baseline, decreasing
the average EM from 0.430 to 0.403. 4 The degradation is most severe on multi-hop benchmarks
(Bamboogle: −4.9, HotpotQA: −3.4), confirming that step-level reward is especially effective when
multiple precise search steps are needed.
Replacing asymmetric scaling with symmetric treatment (λ = 1.0) causes the largest single-
component degradation, reducing the average EM to 0.391. This may seem surprising, but under
λ=1.0, the surviving negative values retain their full magnitude (approximately [−3, −0.5] nats after
dead-zone filtering). As a result, occasional large negative outliers from early poorly formed queries
can dominate individual minibatches. This overwhelming penalty drives the policy toward search
avoidance, as confirmed by the monotonic drop in search frequency shown in Appendix D.
Removing the dead zone (δ = 0) reduces performance to 0.416. On questions the model can already
answer, IG does not faithfully reflect retrieval quality (yielding small positive, near-zero, or even
misleading negative penalties; see Case 3 in §3.4.3) and feeding these into the advantage interferes
with learning on genuinely informative search steps.
4This single-seed ablation is functionally equivalent to AutoRefine-Base; the 0.2-point gap from Table 1 is within seed
variance (Appendix C).
