These ideas lead to a greedy block placement algorithm referred to as Greedy Block Placement with Cache
Reservation (GBP-CR), shown in Alg. 1, which sequentially places blocks onto servers in descending speeds
to form server chains (lines 3–4), until all the servers are processed or the scaled total service rate (denoted
by 𝜈) satisfies the given requirement (line 9).
Complexity: Alg. 1 has a near-linear time complexity of 𝑂(𝐽log 𝐽), dominated by the sorting of servers
in line 3.
Analysis: As an efficient solution, GBP-CR is expected to be generally suboptimal for (10) due to the
NP-hardness of the problem. Nevertheless, GBP-CR is optimal in a special case of practical relevance.
Specifically, consider the case where all the servers have an identical memory size 𝑀𝑗≡𝑀(∀𝑗∈J). This
can be caused by servers having a standard memory configuration (e.g., 8 GB for consumer GPUs or 80 GB
for datacenter GPUs) or owners limiting the amount of memory made available to the model serving system
even if the servers may have more physical memory (e.g., when the GPUs are contributed by volunteers as
in the case of PETALS [6]). In this special case, GBP-CR is optimal in the following sense.
