Algorithm: The main challenge is complexity. In addition to the NP-hardness of optimal cache allocation
as stated in Theorem 3.1, the dimension of the solution space is also large.
Specifically, let G𝒂,𝒎=
(J+, E𝒂,𝒎) denote a logical routing topology under a given block placement (𝒂, 𝒎), formed by the extended
server set J+ and the set E𝒂,𝒎of server pairs that can be traversed consecutively. Then each path in G𝒂,𝒎
from the dummy server 𝑗0 to the dummy server 𝑗𝐽+1 represents a valid server chain that traverses all the
blocks in order, as illustrated by Fig. 2b. Generally, the number of possible server chains under a given
block placement is exponential in the number of servers, which causes severe challenges for cache allocation.
Fortunately, a given load balancing policy may only assign jobs to a subset of all the possible server chains,
which allows efficient cache allocation by only focusing on this subset.
In particular, for load balancing among heterogeneous job servers with a central queue as considered in
this work, a state-of-the-art load balancing policy is Join-the-Fastest-Free-Server (JFFS) [5]. Under JFFS,
each incoming job will either be assigned to the fastest free job server if there is any, or entered into a central
queue if none of the job servers is free. We find that this policy only utilizes a polynomial-sized subset of
possible server chains, identified by Greedy Cache Allocation (GCA) as shown in Alg. 2. Let 𝑀(𝑙)
