3.2.3
Improved Parameter Tuning
The closed-form bounds in Theorem 3.7 enable another method of tuning parameter 𝑐in GBP-CR (Alg. 1):
instead of minimizing the surrogate objective in (14), we can choose 𝑐to minimize the upper/lower bound in
Theorem 3.7 for the chains constructed by GBP-CR (Alg. 1) and GCA (Alg. 2). Compared to the surrogate
objective, these bounds better represent the actual mean response time under the proposed solution, which
leads to better parameter tuning (see Fig. 6), at the cost of slightly more computation at the orchestrator
(because instead of only running GBP-CR for each candidate value of 𝑐, the orchestrator now needs to run
both GBP-CR and GCA).
4
Performance Evaluation
We will test our proposed solutions against benchmarks in serving LLM inference requests by distributed GPU
nodes via two complementary evaluation methods: (i) model-driven simulations that validate our solution
under its original assumptions, and (ii) experiments that evaluate the actual performance when applying our
solution to a leading open-source Internet-distributed LLM inference system called PETALS [6].
