In most cases, regularization is implemented by penalizing the model’s loss function and adding a nonnegative term.
One advantage of such treatment is that it reduces the solution space of the model, thereby preventing model overfitting
and potentially leading to more accurate solutions. To some extent, minimizing the loss function and maximizing the
likelihood probability are equivalent. Therefore, in this article, we considered penalizing the log-likelihood function
of the Kriging model. In the previous section, we explored the effect of the parameter 𝜽on the Kriging model. To
account for this, we subtracted a nonnegative term related to the hyperparameter 𝜽from the log-likelihood function of
