3.2. Parameter sensitivity analysis of the TRK model
Due to the addition of regularization penalties in the TRK model, it is necessary to evaluate the sensitivity of
the TRK model to different penalty parameters. When considering three different penalty TRK models, the Theta-
regularized Lasso Kriging (TR-LK) and Theta-regularized Ridge Kriging (TR-RK) models each have only one
regularization parameter. Therefore, various penalty parameters are selected, and the Forrester function from Section
2.2 is used to analyze the effect of 𝜆and 𝜇on the accuracy of the TR-LK and TR-RK models. RMSE and MAE (i.e.
mean absolute error, See equation (31) in Section 4) were used to reflect the accuracy of the model. The analysis
results are shown in Figure 5(a) and 5(b). The penalty parameters of different magnitudes have different impacts on
the accuracy of TR-LK and TR-RK models. It can be observed that for this test case, the TR-LK model and TR-RK
model seem to have high accuracy when the penalty parameter is set to 10. When the penalty parameter is set to other
Xuelin Xie et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier
Page 10 of 26
Theta-regularized Kriging: Modelling and Algorithms
magnitudes, the accuracy of the model will vary by different magnitudes. Therefore, when adjusting the regularization
parameters in practice, it is recommended to consider a geometric sequence such as 10−5, 10−4, ..., 105.
In the construction of the Theta-regularized Elastic-net Kriging (TR-EK) model, as indicated by Equation (26), the
new parameters introduced are 𝛼and 𝛾. Due to the involvement of two parameters, a grid search method is considered
for parameter selection, with the results shown in Figure 5(c). It is evident that the impact of parameters 𝛼and 𝛾
on model accuracy varies significantly. In this case, when the 𝛾value is fixed, changes in the 𝛼parameter seem
to have a minor effect on model accuracy, whereas changes in the 𝛾parameter, when 𝛼is fixed, have a substantial
impact on model accuracy. Therefore, for the TR-EK model, the regularization parameter 𝛾is more sensitive than
the weight parameter 𝛼. Additionally, the effect of different magnitudes of 𝛾on model accuracy varies greatly. Thus,
when adjusting the parameters of the TR-EK model, priority should be given to exploring different magnitudes of the
𝛾parameter, followed by selecting a suitable range for the weight parameter 𝛼.
