2604.14942v1,What if we have 90 minutes only to teach programming?,"The advancement of automated coding tools may reduce in the future the number of people willing to learn computer programming. We assume that the skill of computational problem solving is not only for the immediate economic benefit, but an important part of our knowledge about the world. As the incentives to learn are weaker, we aim to lower the entry barrier. We ask: Can programming ideas be taught in a very short time?
  We describe a session plan that introduces programming and computing fundamentals for novices, assuming only basic mathematical background. It requires using a non-mainstream, functional and concatenative language reducing accidental complexity. This language, a by-product of research in category theory, allows direct access to fundamental ideas like recursion and advanced like Gödel-encoding in an entertaining puzzle-like manner.",['Attila Egri-Nagy'],2026-04-16,0,https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.14942v1,cs.PL,"What if we have 90 minutes only to teach
programming?
Attila Egri-Nagy
Human & AI Center
Akita International University
Akita, Japan
https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7861-7076
Abstract—The advancement of automated coding tools may
reduce in the future the number of people willing to learn com-
puter programming. We assume that the skill of computational
problem solving is not only for the immediate economic benefit,
but an important part of our knowledge about the world. As the
incentives to learn are weaker, we aim to lower the entry barrier.
We ask: Can programming ideas be taught in a very short time?
We describe a session plan that introduces programming
and computing fundamentals for novices, assuming only basic
mathematical background. It requires using a non-mainstream,
functional and concatenative language reducing accidental com-
plexity. This language, a by-product of research in category
theory, allows direct access to fundamental ideas like recursion
and advanced like G¨odel-encoding in an entertaining puzzle-like
manner.
Index Terms—programming education, computer science for
all, functional programming, concatenative programming, stack-
based computation
“. . . one of the most important aspects of any com-
puting tool is its influence on the thinking habits of
those that try to use it,”
The Humble Programmer by Edsger W. Dijkstra, 1972
Turing Award Lecture [1]
I. INTRODUCTION
Automation is a driving force of computer programming.
Recently, the domain of automation has reached programming
itself. It is predicted that the activity of writing code will go
extinct [2]. therefore, it is conceivable that in the future fewer
people will learn how to develop computer programs. Even
if they decide to do so, they may not be willing sustain the
required effort for long. To put it pessimistically, we might
only have a 90-minute session in the future, hence the title of
this paper. In turn, this may lead to a reduction in the number
of people understanding the technologies underpinning our
everyday life.
The situation in programming is similar to writing, which
arguably the same skill as clear thinking [3]. It is predicted,
that even the number of people who can write will dimin-
ish, splitting the population into ‘writes and write-nots’ [4].
This project was funded in part by the Kakenhi grant 22K00015 by the
Japan Society for the Promotion of Science (JSPS), titled ‘On progressing
human understanding in the shadow of superhuman deep learning artificial
intelligence entities’ (Grant-in-Aid for Scientific Research type C, https:
//kaken.nii.ac.jp/grant/KAKENHI-PROJECT-22K00015/).
Similarly, we may have an even more contrasted ‘codes and
code-nots’. This might turn out to be an overly pessimistic
prediction and the utility of coding skills for the society is
changing and debated. We indeed hope that we are incorrect
in forecasting a decline in engineering skills. Still, it is better
to be prepared. Our cornerstone assumption is that losing
knowledge is not a good thing. We plan to counter the
disappearance of economic incentives by making the barrier
to advanced ideas lower. This requires rethinking the basics,
identifying the essentials, and making the activity attractive.
Going back to the absolute basics has been advocated before
in the context of the service teaching of computer science
[5]. That paper describes a complete self-referential runtime
system for a C-like language. It also invites the reader to
think about what would be a bare minimum to teach other
parts of computing. This paper is another answer for that
call, describing a blend of high-level functional and low-level
machine code style of programming.
A. The origin story of CON-CAT: a happy accident
We were not set out to develop educational material. The
goal of designing the programming language CON-CAT was
to have a high-level expressive language that admits the
mathematical tools for doing hierarchical decompositions in
algebraic and category theoretical automata theory [6]. The
niche paradigm of concatenative languages can serve this role.
The design process and inspiring languages are described in
[7]. The closest ancestor is the JOY programming language
[8].
During the development it was intentional to do actual
programming in the language. Then, it was recognized that
the unusual syntax makes writing problems very puzzling, At
the same time, the solutions are intellectually satisfying and
very expressive. These are the hallmarks of challenging course
materials.
B. Essential and Accidental Complexity
The seminal software-engineering paper [9] distinguishes
between two types of complexity we have to deal with when
solving problems. Roughly speaking, the essential complexity
belongs to the problem we want to solve, and there is no way
to eliminate it. The accidental complexity comes from the tools
we use. For instance, using a computer requires knowing how
arXiv:2604.14942v1 [cs.PL] 16 Apr 2026
to program and manage running applications. The invention of
high-level languages is a huge step in reducing this accidental
complexity. Of course, there always remains a bit of friction
in using any tools. Complete elimination is not possible, but
we aim to minimize.
C. Happiness as being in the zone
It is a commonplace that we learn better when we enjoy
the process. In [10], this observation was made precise and
confirmed by long-term psychological studies. The optimal
experience is defined as “a sense that one’s skills are adequate
to cope with the challenges at hand, in a goal-directed, rule-
bound action system that provides clear clues as to how
well one is performing”. For a programming language, the
complexity of the challenge can be fine-tuned by giving
subsets of the language (e.g., [11]). We see this balancing at
the edge of manageable complexity as the main driving force
for learning: once a concept is familiar, it becomes boring, thus
there is a need for proceeding to more challenging problems.
We design pathways in the course material to support this
succession.
It is possible to have a culture where mathematical and
problem solving is not directly tied to practical applications.
During the Edo period (1603–1867) in Japan, wasan was
developed a form of recreational but serious mathematics [12].
D. The axiomatic method
Ever since Euclid’s Elements, the ideal mathematical knowl-
edge representation is a formal system: axioms and rules of
inference. This is a form of compression that fits programming
languages as well. What are the language primitives and what
library functions can be built on top of those is an important
design decision. Since, we want to have a programming
language that can be used even without a computer, just
pen and paper computation, we want to have a core as
small as possible. The theory of concatenative operators has
mathematical interest as well [13].
E. The target level: Project Euler
PROJECT EULER1 is a website hosting a collection of math-
ematical problems that require both insight and computational
power. Typically the problems cannot be solved by using a
brute force approach, and at the same time a good idea alone
is not sufficient, as several cases need to be computed. It is
well recognized that the challenge problems are useful for both
learning programming and mathematics [14].
II. THE COURSE MATERIAL
Keeping up with the promise of creating a very short course
material, we present the key ideas of the tutorial here. The
reader can judge whether this material is sufficient enough
for understanding the basic mechanisms of computation. Nat-
urally, the time for reading the paper could be shorter than
the planned 90 minutes, but it lacks all the interactions and
communications of the live session.
1https://projecteuler.net/
Significant part of developing the material was about re-
fining the notation for execution traces. These are used for
questions too. Part of the stack or the code is replaced by a
question mark.
A. Stack, Input Buffer and the Execution Model
The stack data structure appears in multiple ways in life:
stacking coins, plates, or trays in a cafeteria. The underlying
mechanism is an intuitive idea: last thing in is the first go out.
There is no need for a formal definition. The empty stack is
denoted by a little box. In the notation used below the stack’s
top is on the right its depth grows towards the left. Here is a
simple execution trace. Time proceeds from top to bottom.
stack
input buffer
1 2 3
1
2 3
1 2
3
1 2 3
The input buffer is on the right. It is a sequence of tokens.
A token is just a sequence symbols not including whitespace
characters The stack is on the left. At one step, we pick the
left-most token and evaluate it. Basic data items just push
themselves to the stack. We can identify data literals with
programs that push values to the stack. This way everything
is a program.
Operators do something with the items on the stack. For
instance, dup pushes a new copy of, while drop discards the
top value.
1
dup drop drop
1 1
drop drop
1
drop
We can point out some common sense cases of errors. We
cannot duplicate or throw away the top of the stack if the
stack is empty.
At this stage these stack manipulation operators seem to
of little use. They become crucial in more complex computa-
tions. However, students are very quick to recognize that any
stack state can be constructed clearing the stack by dropping
everything and then filling it up with the desired data items.
Eventually, there is a need to postulate that simply dropping
and pushing is not a valid solution for a problem.
B. Arithmetic
Arithmetic is a solid base for learning about computation,
since everyone is familiar with it. One plus one equals two.
1 2 +
1
2 +
1 2
+
3
Since we use a stack, the arithmetic is postfix (Reverse Polish
notation, RPN). The operators follow the operands, and the
operators have fixed arity, therefore there is no need for
parentheses. Here are the first puzzles. In the first, we need to
find the result of the computation. The vertical dots indicate
omitted steps.
2 3 4 5 * + *
⋮
?
The problem simply requires playing out the evaluation steps.
The solution corresponds to the expression (2 ∗(3 + (4 ∗5))),
which equals 46.
It is more interesting to turn the question around: finding
not the result of the execution of a program, but constructing
a program that yields the result from the input.
2 3 4
?
⋮
14
We have three data items, thus by using two arithmetic
operators we can get down to a single number on the top
of the stack. Restricted to addition and multiplication we
have four possible such programs: + + yields 9, while + *
computes 2 ∗7 = 14, the desired result. By checking the
possibilities systematically we find that * + is also a solution
by 2 + 12 = 14, and * * produces 24. This is an excellent
opportunity to reflect on the possibility that a problem may
have several solutions. Later, the diversity may be desirable
for performance or readability reasons.
C. Lists
The fundamental collection data type of the language is the
list. It is inhomogeneous and we can join elements on both
ends, unlike in LISP. The square brackets [ and ] are separate
tokens. When processing an opening bracket, we push an open
list to the stack. It will swallow all the tokens until the closing
bracket arrives.
7 [ 8 9 ] ljoin
7
[ 8 9 ] ljoin
7 [⇩]
8 9 ] ljoin
7 [8,⇩]
9 ] ljoin
7 [8,9,⇩]
] ljoin
7 [8,9]
ljoin
[7,8,9]
The arrow symbol shows where the next data item will be
inserted. This notation is more important when we have nested
lists. As we close the inner open list, the indicator arrow moves
outwards.
[ [ ] ]
[⇩]
[ ] ]
[[⇩]]
] ]
[[],⇩]
]
[[]]
We have the analogous operation rjoin and the inverses (on
non-empty lists) lcut and rcut.
D. Quoting and Dequoting
The list is not just a collection, it is also used for quoting.
Quoting works just like in natural language. Compare the two
sentences:
1) Cambridge is a nice university town in England.
2) “Cambridge” starts with the letter C.
In the first sentence, our attention turns to the town itself,
imagining the medieval buildings, or punting on the river.
We move from the word to the meaning. In computing
terminology, we evaluate the token. In the second case, the
evaluation is withheld. We look at the token itself. Similarly,
putting code into a list makes it into inert data.
[ 1 2 + ]
[⇩]
1 2 + ]
[1,⇩]
2 + ]
[1,2,⇩]
+ ]
[1,2,+,⇩]
]
[1,2,+]
Nothing happens to the code, no arithmetic calculation is done
- that is the point. We pushed the code itself to the stack as
data for later execution or manipulation.
How to quote something already on the stack?
42
?
⋮
[42]
The program [ ] ljoin pushes an empty list on top, and by
left-joining it swallows the previous top element.
The inverse operation of quoting, dequoting, can be done
by the i operator. It prepends the content of a program (a list)
on the top of the stack to the input buffer. Thus, the program
gets executed.
[1,2,+]
i
1 2 +
⋮
3
The name i comes from combinatory logic [15], [16], where
I stands for the identity combinator. Perhaps the name is not
most fitting, as dequoting renders inactive code active.
How to act deeper on the stack? In the following puzzle we
want to add 2 and 3, but these operands are deeper down in
the stack.
2 3 4 [+]
?
⋮
5 4
We have the quoted program for addition and the number
4 is also in the way. We can swap the code closer to the
operands, then join the number with addition program on the
right. Finally, we execute the prepared program. Number 4
will itself back to the top. Therefore, the solution is creating
a program that executes the given program and restores the
stack: swap rjoin i.
E. Homoiconicity and G¨odel
We saw that programs are represented as lists, sequences of
tokens. The input buffer itself is a list, although we omit the
brackets in our notation. Similarly for the stack, we represent
it graphically. This is an example of the code as data principle.
In other words, CON-CAT is a homoiconic language.
This property allows metaprogramming, which is standard
way of programming in CON-CAT. This is also a simplified
version of G¨odel-numbering [17]. We can easily express the
idea of a program processing itself. If P is any program that
takes a list, then the following program is valid.
[ [ P ] P ]
F. Metalanguage
The standard use of programming languages is to extend
them with new function definitions. For the original mathemat-
ical purpose of CON-CAT, function definitions are redundant.
They do not increase the computational capabilities of the
language, just make programs shorter. However, for problem
solving this feature is needed. As a middle-way solution, we
define a metalanguage layer by tokens that start with a dot,
like hidden files in a UNIX filesystem.
""inc"" [1,+]
.def
The effect is that a new token inc will be available for use.
The stack is empty and the real change in the language is
invisible.
1 inc
1
inc
1
1 +
1 1
+
2
Processing a defined token is prepending the associated code
to the input buffer. This is the same mechanism as the one
used for the i operator. Note that the definition also requires
to have string literals.
G. Conditionals
Branching the execution based on a value is a necessary
ingredient for universal computation. It is standard to use the
logical values true and false to choose between alternatives.
true 1 2
choice
1
false 1 2
choice
2
The order of condition, consequent and alternative can be
arbitrary. This convention follows the usual if-then-else format.
Based on choice and quoting we can define more sophisti-
cated conditional statements. For instance,
I T E ifte
where I, T, E are programs. First, the program I for condition
is evaluated on the remaining stack. It leaves a logical value on
stack. Based on that we evaluate either T or E. Other variations
restore the stack after evaluating the condition.2
H. Recursion
Once we can define new tokens, recursion is straightfor-
ward.
""factorial""
[ [ dup zero? ]
[ drop 1 ]
[ dup 1 - factorial * ]
ifte ]
.def
This checks whether the top of the stack is zero or not. The
condition preventively duplicates the top number. If true, we
replace that with one. Otherwise, we push a decremented
number on the stack. This will also accumulate the required
number of multiplication tokens.
Can we do recursion without names? We can use the Y -
combinator [15]. It is even easier to express in a concatenative
language [18]. The idea is that in the moment of recursive
call, we need to have access to source code of the recursive
function. In our case, any program is a list. Therefore, it is
only a matter of keeping a copy of that list on the stack until
the base line case of recursion is called.
III. IMPLEMENTATION AND TRIALS
A. The CON-CAT-runtime
CON-CAT is written in CLOJURE [19], which is a modern
dialect of LISP. The source code and documentation for this
project is available at [20].
B. Typesetting the Execution Traces
All trace diagrams in this paper were generated by the
CON-CAT
runtime environment [20]. Using the same code
that executes the programs guarantees the correctness of the
examples. The typographic layout was done by TYPST, a
modern, markup-based document preparation system [21].
TYPST has an easy to use scripting language, convenient for
typesetting the execution traces.
C. Trials
The content of the course material was tested by participants
of diverse backgrounds. They include schoolchildren, univer-
sity students and professors, and software engineers. Their
feedback was instructive for designing the language and the
typographic presentations.
IV. CONCLUSION
Free from the constraints of practicality and compatibility
for programming education we could choose a programming
style for simplicity and depth. Concatenative functional pro-
gramming offers a unified view of machine code level pro-
cessing and higher order functions. We developed a language,
CON-CAT, according to these guidelines.
2See the standard library in CON-CAT source code, in the resources folder.
A. Future Work
The project is still in the preparatory phase. The next
tasks will be extending the content and start measuring the
efficiency.
We will adapt relevant content from the legendary SICP
textbook [22]. Another way to improve attractiveness is to
implement CON-CAT on a retrocomputing device. The best-
selling and recently reissued Commodore 64 microcomputer
is a challenging but rewarding implementation target.
For measuring the efficiency of this teaching material, we
will integrate the puzzles into extra-curricular mathematical
activities.",True,18574
