Introduction
An irrelevant composite operator in two-dimensional quantum field theories, denoted by T ¯T,
was introduced by Zamolodchikov in [1]. In Euclidean signature, it is defined by
OT ¯T := −det(T µν) .
(1.1)
Under general assumptions, including translational and rotational invariance, the existence
of a conserved stress tensor, factorization at large separation, and a short-distance conformal
fixed point, its expectation value satisfies the universal relation
⟨OT ¯T ⟩= −π2
4 ⟨T µµ⟩2 .
(1.2)
This factorization property underlies the solvable character of the associated deformation and
motivates the study of observables away from the undeformed conformal point. Correlation
functions can be used to examine how locality, operator mixing, and short-distance singulari-
ties are modified by the deformation. For reviews, see [2–4].
At the classical level, starting from an undeformed action S0, the T ¯T flow is defined by
