This deformation preserves integrability in many
examples [5–10], and it also relates a number of otherwise distinct models [6, 11, 12]. In
addition, the close relation between T ¯T deformations and two-dimensional gravity provides a
non-perturbative perspective on the deformation [13–15]; see also [16] for applications. More
specifically, on curved backgrounds, the classical T ¯T deformation is equivalent to coupling the
undeformed theory to a two-dimensional ghost-free massive gravity theory [15, 17, 18]. In this
formulation,
ST ¯T-deformed QFT(f, ϕ) = SQFT(e∗(f), ϕ) + Sgrav(e∗(f), f),
