Meanwhile, many global properties of the deformed theory can be treated analytically.
At the quantum level, analyzing local correlators involves additional difficulties, since the
deformation is irrelevant and the resulting theory is non-local at short distances. A result
in this direction was obtained in the large-c analysis of [19], where stress-tensor correlators
were shown to be computable in an appropriate ’t Hooft-like limit, and the deformed three-
point function of the stress tensor was derived explicitly. The diffusion equations satisfied by
deformed partition functions were realized in a JT-gravity path-integral framework in [14],
and were subsequently studied from the viewpoint of random geometry on different topologies
in [20]. On the torus, [21] showed that the partition function of a T ¯T-deformed CFT on T2
exhibits SL(2, Z) modular invariance, while related residual modular properties were further
analyzed in [22].
From a complementary modular-invariance perspective, [23] studied the
partition function of T ¯T-deformed CFTs on T2 and derived universal large-c formulas for the
density of states in both double- and single-trace deformations.
A treatment of local correlators was developed in [24], where flow equations for correlation
functions of local operators were derived, the derivation property of the deformation on the
operator algebra was established, ultraviolet (UV) divergences were analyzed, and renormal-
ized correlators together with a deformed operator product expansion and a Callan–Symanzik
equation were obtained. In particular, for deformed CFTs on the plane, the momentum-space
two-point function exhibits the characteristic k-dependent anomalous scaling implied by the
T ¯T flow. A complementary non-perturbative analysis based on JT gravity was carried out
in [25], where the same two-point function was computed and shown to display a distinct
large-momentum behavior, reflecting the non-locality of the deformed theory at short dis-
tances. This analysis was later extended to T2 in [26]. In [27, 28], the flow equation and
perturbative QFT methods were used to study the deformation of KdV charges and the cor-
responding modifications of the partition function. Later, [29] obtained the deformed CFT
partition function on a genus-two Riemann surface Σ(2).
– 2 –
Subsequent work extended these results in several complementary directions. Perturba-
tive analyses extended the study of deformed correlators to supersymmetric theories and to
compact backgrounds: first-order correlation functions were derived for 2d N = (1, 1) and
N = (2, 2) SCFTs [30], and the perturbative framework was generalized from R2 to T2 [31].
From the random-geometry perspective [20], this framework was further developed to repro-
duce deformed correlators and to clarify its relation to the gravitational description [32, 33].
More recently, the analysis has been studied by a systematic study of momentum-space two-
point functions in unitary T ¯T-deformed CFTs [25], as well as by higher-order treatments in
general CFTs [34]. These developments suggest that the existence of deformed correlators
is no longer the main focus; instead, their organization and kinematic characterization have
become relevant.
More recently, a closely related deformation, known as root-T ¯T, has attracted considerable
attention [35, 36]. The operator can be defined as follows
O√
