as NumPy and SciPy. matplotlib, as well as PyTorch for neural network train-
ing. This environment can be run locally on a laptop or workstation equipped
with Google Colab2. The goal of this library is to allow the building of complex
formulations easily.
We will explain now how to use this library on the simple example of Laplace
problem. We start from defining grid of 31 × 31 collocation points, representing
the discrete domain Ωh.
grid = Grid(30, 30)
assert grid.shape == (31, 31)
assert grid.size = 31 ** 2
X, Y = grid.points # array of shape (2, 31, 31)
assert grid.cell_volume == 1 / 30**2
The Grid class allows simple iteration over points, indices, boundary points,
etc. It also allows for the management of boundary normal vectors and the
computation of cell/face volumes.
The next step is to define a functionality of a GridFunction on a Grid of points.
It supports scalar, vector, and tensor values of discrete functions. Our library
supports arithmetic operations of discrete functions f + g, f * g, f / g, etc.; it
allows concatenating these discrete functions with other Python functions such
as sin(f), exp(f), sqrt(f), etc. It also supports differential operators including
gradient grad(f, ""+""), divergence div(F, ""-""), etc. It is also possible to convert
the discrete function to or from numpy arrays.
f = GridFunction.from_function(lambda x, y: x + y, grid)
assert f(i, j) == grid.points[0, i, j] + grid.points[1, i, j]
vals = f.tabulate() # vals.shape == (N, N)
g = GridFunction.from_array(vals, grid)
assert f == g
