Using the SymPy library enables the creation of manufactured solution prob-
lems. We illustrate this using the example of uexact = sin(πx) sin(3πy) defined
over the discrete domain Ωh = {(i/30, j/30) : 0 ≤i, j ≤30} ⊂Ω= [0, 1]2.
from sympy import diff, sin, lambdify, pi
def laplacian(expr, *vars):
return sum(diff(expr, v, v) for v in vars)
x, y = sympy.symbols(""x y"")
u_exact = sin(pi * x) * sympy.sin(3 * pi * y)
rhs = -laplacian(u_exact, x, y)
u_grid = GridFunction.from_function(lambdify([x, y], u_exact), grid)
rhs_grid = GridFunction.from_function(lambdify([x, y], rhs), grid)
Discrete weak formulations requires tools for computing discrete integrals,
defined as appropriate sums over the values defined at the grid points, or the sums
of the discrete finite-difference derivatives. We provide tools to define discrete
∥·∥h, ∥·∥∇,h norms, tools for defining discrete (·, ·)h, (·, ·)∇,h scalar products, as
well as tools to compute discrete integrals of arbitrary GridFunctions over Grid
points, and discrete boundary integrals computed over Grid points and boundary
points,
fg = integrate(f * g)
grad_prod = product(grad(f, ""+""), grad(g, ""+""), ""h"")
grad_prod2 = product(f, g, ""grad_h"")
bd_term = integrate_bd(f)
n_x = GridFunction(lambda i, j: grid.facet_normal(i, j)[0], grid)
a = integrate(Dx(f, ""+"") * g)
b = -integrate(f * Dx(g, ""-"")) + integrate_bd(f * g * n_x)
assert a == b
The library supports scalar, vector, and arbitrary tensor function spaces,
P = FunctionSpace(grid) # scalars
U = VectorFunctionSpace(grid, 2) # 2 element vectors
S = TensorFunctionSpace(grid, (2, 2)) # 2 x 2 tensors
It also supports products of spaces,
W = CompositeFunctionSpace(S, U, P)
assert U.dim == 2 * P.dim
assert S.shape == (2, 2)
assert U.zero == np.array([0, 0])
# indices of basis functions on the x = 0 boundary
select_dofs(P, lambda i, j: i == 0)
