The robust discrete variational physics-informed neural network will be trained
based on the residual loss multiplied by the inverse of the Gram matrix computed
in the proper discrete product, namely
L(θ) = ∥Auθ −f∥2
V ′
= RT
θ GRθ = RES (θ)T G−1 RES(θ)
(62)
where Rθ = G−1 RES(θ) is the vector representing v 7→(Au −f, v)h, RES(θ)i =
(Au −f, vi)h and vi is the i-th basis function of V . We compute the LU factor-
ization of the Gram matrix of our (·, ·)∇,h scalar product, which coincides with
the matrix of our bilinear form.
G_ = B_
G_LU = scipy.linalg.lu_factor(G_)
Since the loss function is evaluated many times, each loss computation reuses
the factorization
residuum_vec = B_ @ vals_vec - M_ @ rhs_vec
residuum_rep = scipy.linalg.lu_solve(G_LU, residuum_vec)
loss = np.dot(residuum_vec, residuum_rep)
For efficient training of the neural network using the robust loss, to enable back-
propagation when using scipy.linalg.lu_solve, we can wrap the loss computa-
tion in a torch.autograd.Function:
class ResiduumNormSq(torch.autograd.Function):
@staticmethod
def forward(vals):
vals_vec = remove_dofs(vals.cpu().numpy(), W_bc)
residuum_vec = B_ @ vals_vec - M_ @ rhs_vec
residuum_rep = scipy.linalg.lu_solve(G_LU, residuum_vec)
return torch.tensor(np.dot(residuum_vec, residuum_rep))
...
We also need to compute the derivative with respect to the input vector
L(v) = (Av −f)T G−1(Av −f) =⇒∂L
∂v = 2AT G−1(Av −f)
(63)
This functionality is implemented as follows :
Python library supporting discrete weak formulations with CRVPINN
17
@staticmethod
def setup_context(ctx, args, output):
(vals,) = args
ctx.save_for_backward(vals)
@staticmethod
def backward(ctx, grad_output):
(vals,) = ctx.saved_tensors
vals_vec = remove_dofs(vals.cpu().numpy(), W_bc)
residuum_vec = B_ @ vals_vec - M_ @ rhs_vec
residuum_rep = scipy.linalg.lu_solve(G2_LU, residuum_vec)
full_residuum_rep = reinsert_dofs(B_.T @ residuum_rep, W_bc)
grad_vals = grad_output * 2 * torch.tensor(full_residuum_rep)
return grad_vals.to(device)
With these tools in hand, we are ready to define the loss function used for
training:
def loss_function(pinn):
points = np.concat(grid.points).reshape(2, -1)
args = torch.from_numpy(points.T.astype(np.float32)).to(device)
values = torch.ravel(pinn(args).t())
return ResiduumNormSq.apply(values)
which can then be used with standard PyTorch optimizers.
4.2
Formulation of the discrete weak Stokes problem
In this section, we will show, step by step, how to implement the Stokes problem
formulation from section section 3. The grid object remains the same as in the
