(2.19a)
with Kk defined as in (2.12b). Solutions to this family of equations are given by
ϕk(τ) = sk(τ) +
Z τ
0
Rk(τ, ˜τ) sk(˜τ) d˜τ
for some continuous resolvent function Rk : R × R −→C. The main goal of this section is Propo-
sition 3.1, which establishes the pointwise bound (3.1b) on Rk, bounding its growth polynomially
along with exponential decay in (τ −˜τ). This specific bound, when applied to (2.11a), then allows
us to estimate ˜F[ρ] pointwise in terms of ˜F[S] by straightforward computations, see Corollary 3.3.
In absence of the scale factor a(T(˜τ)), the integration kernel in (2.19) would be of convolution
type. Then, one could explicitly solve these equations using the Fourier-Laplace transform, which
turns (2.19) into an algebraic relation in the Fourier-Laplace domain, as is usually done when
studying classical Landau damping, see for example [MV11; BMM16; GNR21].
The presence
of expansion causes significant issues in this standard approach since it breaks the convolution
structure. Instead, we use that Rk(τ, ˜τ) satisfies an integral equation itself that, for the Poisson
background, can be reduced to a second order ordinary differential equation whose solutions we
can estimate via the Liouville-Green approximation.
This crucially uses that, for the Poisson
equilibrium, Kk can be written as a product of functions of τ and ˜τ, which is the main simplification
it provides. While this method does not extend to general equilibria, it is possible that there are
different ways of establishing resolvent bounds as in (3.1b) for general, radially symmetric equilibria
in light of the classic Penrose condition.
2.5.2. The nonlinear analysis. In Section 4, our goal is to use the linear estimate in Corollary 3.3
to obtain Theorem 2.8 by estimating the nonlinearities in the source S. To this end, we make the
following bootstrap assumption.
