Thus, if (2.21) is satisfied, then this is a strict improvement of the bootstrap assumption, and
by Lemma 2.5, we know that there is some τ ′ > τBoot such that the bootstrap assumption (2.20)
extends to [0, τ ′). This closes the bootstrap, and Theorem 2.8 then follows directly. We provide
more details on this at the end of Section 4.
While adaptations are needed throughout the argument to account for the presence of ex-
pansion, the two main points causing qualitatively different behaviour compared to, in particular,
[GNR21] are due to changes in the relationship between ˜F[ρ] and ˜F[S] compared to the classical
setting. First, the linear estimate in Corollary 3.3 implies that there is a loss of decay by ⟨τ⟩β′
when estimating the density by its source. Conversely, the nonlinearity in (2.11b) behaves the
most poorly for |k| ≈|l|, while the integral is concentrated at kτ ≈l˜τ. Thus, for the terms that
impose sharp Gevrey requirements on the initial data in the classical setting, the scale factor acts
approximately as an amplification by ⟨τ⟩β when trying to relate ˜F[S] back to ˜F[ρ]. Neverthe-
less, by imposing (2.15), we can account for these amplifications in the proof of Lemma 4.4 along
with that arising from the resolvent bound, and thus to control the source on the bootstrap interval.
In particular, we note that this section is largely independent of the specific shape of the
background equilibrium, or even whether the particle interaction is attractive or repulsive, once an
estimate as in Corollary 3.3 is obtained. While there are certainly limitations on obtaining such
an estimate for attractive particle interactions in d = 3 even for the Poisson equilibrium, as we
discuss in Appendix B, it is possible that this section can be applied to other equilibria in the
repulsive setting if a similar linear estimate is obtained by other means. In this spirit, the nonlinear
estimates in Section 4 are performed without using the specific form of the background aside from
when the linear damping results need to be invoked, instead only relying on the weaker regularity
assumption (4.1).
