Figure 2: PASS@(k, Tmax) vs. sampling budget k on a log axis. Tmax = 0 for Category A and
Tmax = 5 for B and C. On Category C, πbase and πRL cross near k = 4: at k = 1 πbase is slightly
ahead, but as k grows πRL pulls above and the gap widens (+4 pp at k = 64), the opposite of the
convergence reported by Yue et al. [2025]. πSFT sits below both.
(i) Category A is flat in T; pass-curves cluster within ≤5 pp with no systematic ordering.
Tool-use RL is orthogonal to parametric MATH-500 reasoning, the expected negative control.
(ii) Category B saturates at T = 2. Every model jumps from near-zero at T = 0 to its plateau
at T ∈{1, 2} and stays flat. At k = 64, πRL is highest (0.86), πSFT follows (0.85), and πbase trails
by ∼4 pp; the two-retrieval strategy is simple enough that both training signals find it, and the
RL-over-SFT gap is just 1 pp.
(iii) Category C pass-curves diverge as k grows, with πRL pulling ahead. At k = 1, πbase is
actually slightly ahead of πRL on Cat C (0.363 vs. 0.335): single-shot, RL is less reliable than base.
As k grows the ordering flips near k = 4 and the gap widens, reaching PASS@(64, 5) = 0.81 vs.
0.77 for πbase and 0.73 for πSFT, a +4 pp gap that is largest at the right tail. This is exactly the
decomposition PASS@(k, T) is designed to expose: if RL’s effect were efficiency improvement, the
RL-over-base gap would be largest at small k and converge at large k, the behavior Yue et al. [2025]
report for RLVR on static reasoning. We see the opposite: on Cat C, RL’s contribution is primarily
capability expansion, not efficiency improvement. RL has effectively traded efficiency for capability:
giving up a small amount of single-shot reliability in exchange for the ability to solve additional
problems that lay outside the base agent’s capability set at any sampling budget.
4.3
Per-problem capability analysis and interaction saturation
Averaged pass-curves hide how many individual problems changed hands. Figure 3a (left) plots
per-problem PASS@(64, 5) on the 100 bridge questions, with rows sorted by πRL’s value; the top
half has a clear band of rows that are bright for πRL but dark for πbase. Figure 3a (right) collapses the
scatter into four-way stacked counts against πbase: {both solve, only base, only model, neither}. The
πRL-vs-πbase decomposition yields |only πRL| = 5 and |only πbase| = 1, a 5:1 asymmetry, with 76
problems in the intersection. For πSFT the corresponding decomposition is 3:7, the opposite direction:
matched-data SFT loses more bridge questions than it gains. The exploration-bonus variant πRL+explore
sits in between at 4:3. In the πRL-vs-πSFT comparison, |B(C)
RL \ B(C)
SFT| = 9 against |B(C)
