5
Mechanism Analysis: How Does RL Reshape the Agent?
Section 4 established what changes in agent capability; we now ask how. Three diagnostic analyses
(perplexity decomposition, strategy-diversity measurement, and cross-policy swapping) each isolate
a different hypothesis about where RL’s improvement lives (queries, reasoning, or both). Figure 4
combines the three.
(a) Perplexity decomposition: novelty sits on reasoning, not queries.
We isolate each side of
an πRL trajectory by computing πbase’s per-token negative log-likelihood restricted to tokens inside
Search: lines (query formulation) versus tokens inside Thought:/Answer: lines (reasoning), with
environment-generated observation tokens excluded (formulas and protocol in Appendix D). On 200
successful πRL trajectories from Category C at T = 5 we obtain PPLsearch = 2.07 (median 1.94) vs.
PPLreason = 3.08 (median 2.79), a 50% gap. Per-token surprisals are 0.73 nats for queries and 1.12
nats for reasoning. If RL had primarily learned exotic search strings, PPLsearch should dominate; we
see the opposite. The novelty that πbase detects in πRL’s successful trajectories is concentrated on how
the agent reasons over the returned paragraphs, not on what it searched for.
(b) Strategy diversity: SFT collapses, RL preserves.
For each Category C test problem we
sample 64 trajectories at T = 5 and count distinct ordered tuples of (normalized) search queries. πbase
produces a mean of 40.1 unique sequences per problem (median 44), πRL produces 45.5 (median 51),
and πSFT collapses to 14.7 (median 10), a 3× reduction. A complementary “novelty fraction” (the
share of a model’s sequences that do not appear in any πbase trajectory for the same problem) is 97.7%
for πSFT but only 83.9% for πRL: SFT has almost completely replaced the base query distribution,
