A. Related Work
End-to-end evaluation of next-generation wireless systems
is supported by network simulators, channel simulators,
and link-level frameworks, each with different abstraction
levels. For example, the ns-3 mmWave module enables
full-stack simulation of cellular mmWave networks with
modular physical (PHY)/Medium Access Control (MAC)
components and statistical channels, with optional support
for measurement- or ray-tracing-based traces [4]. NYUSIM
provides an open-source, measurement-driven mmWave and
sub-THz channel simulator [5], and has been integrated
into ns-3 to enable drop-based sub-THz end-to-end simula-
tions (e.g., at 140 GHz) for throughput/latency studies using
an existing protocol stack [6]. Complementary PHY-layer
frameworks such as HermesPy support link-level evaluation
and can include hardware-in-the-loop experiments [7], while
impairment-focused studies investigate how sub-THz phase
noise shapes waveform and receiver design [8]. Outside
communications, modular end-to-end simulators in submil-
limeter domains (e.g., astronomical instrumentation) also
demonstrate the value of system models that span multiple
components [9].
These approaches are well suited for their respective goals,
but they do not capture the specific coupling introduced
by the radio-stripe architecture, where distributed RUs are
connected by a fiber-based analog transport chain and where
algorithmic choices (e.g., RU selection and beamforming) are
directly shaped by hardware effects (e.g., frequency-selective
fiber transport, insertion-loss accumulation, nonlinear ampli-
fication, and phase noise). In contrast, this simulator com-
bines (i) a configurable waveform layer, (ii) measurement-
aligned component models along the fiber/stripe chain, and
(iii) either stochastic channel models or site-specific ray-
tracing channel state information (CSI) datasets, enabling
consistent studies of the interplay between algorithms and
hardware-limited signal propagation. Furthermore, the simu-
lator allows to include sub-10 GHz access points to investi-
gate trade-offs and complementary dual-band operation for
communication and positioning.
