Fig. 1: A deployment of distributed low-complexity sub-Thz RUs and sub-10GHz with dual-frequency operation. Block diagram of the
sub-Thz fiber-based infrastructure (the stripe), consisting of a central unit (CU), and multiple RUs connected with polymer microwave
fibers (PMFs). A RU can be configured to receive signals transmitted by a user equipment (UE) (green), transmit over the air towards a
UE (orange) or amplify the signal (blue) to be carried further over the PMF to account for the losses.
II. SIMULATOR OVERVIEW
A. Scope and architecture
The simulator implements an end-to-end signal chain and
is organized around four layers:
1) Scenario definition: environment geometry, stripe and
user equipment (UE) locations, and carrier/bandwidth
settings are described in YAML configuration files.
2) Waveform layer: the default waveform is cyclic pre-
fix (CP)-orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing
(OFDM), including pilot insertion and oversampling.
3) Hardware chain: baseband in-phase and quadrature
(IQ) signals propagate from the central unit (CU), over
the fibers and through RUs/booster units (BUs) using
configurable component models.
4) Wireless
propagation: sub-THz channels can be
loaded from precomputed datasets (e.g., ray-tracing)
or generated using stochastic or deterministic models
(e.g., Rayleigh, line-of-sight (LoS)).
B. Simulation Setup Flow
The overall workflow consists of configuring the simulator
through YAML files (Section II-B1), generating the channel
response and performing the waveform/hardware simulation
(Section II-B3). Either a ray-tracer (RT) (Sections II-B2
and IV-B) computes the channels upfront to be used later
in the hardware simulation or deterministic/stochastic models
(Section IV-A) compute the channel on the fly while simulat-
ing the hardware. Sionna RT generates the channel response
upfront and exports it as a dataset; the Python simulator then
applies the channels by lookup while running the waveform
and hardware models (Sections III and IV).
1) Configuration File: Simulation scenarios are config-
ured through human-readable YAML files shipped with the
simulator repository. The configuration is split into (i) an
environment file defining geometry and node locations, (ii)
waveform configuration file, and (iii) component configura-
tion file, allowing re-use of waveform and hardware models
across multiple rooms/stripe layouts. Currently, parameters
related to the antennas, carrier frequency, bandwidth and
number of subcarriers are tied to the RT configuration.
Coupling the RT more tightly to the simulator, allowing it to
run in real time, would overcome this limitation. For the time
being the same parameters have to be used in the simulator as
in the specific environment simulation in the ‘RT module‘.
a) Environment configuration: Each environment folder
contains a configuration file with the following main sections:
• room: room dimensions in meters (x, y, z).
• stripe_config: high-level stripe topology (number
of stripes N_stripes, number of RUs N_RUs, inter-
RU and inter-stripe spacing, start/end positions and
orientation metadata).
• radio_stripes: a list of stripes, where each stripe is
a list of nodes starting with one CU (central_unit)
followed by the RUs (radio_unit), each specified by
(x, y, z) coordinates.
• ue_positions: a list of UE locations, each specified
by (x, y, z) coordinates.
• sub_thz: optional frequency-band metadata for the
sub-THz mode used by the RT. This includes carrier
frequency fc (fc), bandwidth bw (B), and number of
subcarriers num_subcarriers (Q).
• sub10GHz: optional configuration for a sub-10 GHz
baseline (e.g., access points (APs) positions and an-
tenna pattern/polarization) and the number of antenna
branches per AP (N_sub10ghz_antennas). Carrier
frequency fc (fc), bandwidth bw (B), and number of
subcarriers num_subcarriers (Q) are only added
when a RT generated the channel.
• antenna:
number
of
antenna
branches
per
RU
(N_antennas),
antenna
polarization
(polarization) and pattern selection (pattern).
Only present when RT is used.
• central_unit_fiber_length: fiber length be-
tween the CU and the first RU.
b) Waveform
configuration:
The
waveform
file
defines the baseband numerology, e.g., waveform_type
(e.g.,
CP-OFDM),
n_ofdm_symbols,
qam_order,
oversampling_factor,
cp_length,
pilot
settings
(pilot_spacing, pilot_mode), and transmit power
(tx_power).
c) Component configuration: Hardware blocks are pa-
rameterized through a component YAML file that selects
models and parameters for the stripe chain, e.g., booster
and antenna-side amplifier settings (boost_amplifier,
antenna_amplifier),
fiber
(fiber)
and
coupler
(coupler) models, and their corresponding measurement
files (e.g., .s2p responses).
2) 3D Models and Ray Tracer: Geometry-based propaga-
tion is handled by a companion module (“RT module”) that
runs offline. The ray tracer computes multipath components
and exports a per-subcarrier MIMO channel to a NetCDF
dataset. A 3D scene description specifies the simulation envi-
ronment in an XML format and assigns the correct material
properties. The environment YAML defines the stripe and
UE locations for which to compute the channels. During
simulation, the Python layer reuses the same configuration to
query H[q] for a given stripe/RU and UE index, keeping the
computationally heavy ray tracing step out of the main loop.
Implementation details of the Sionna RT setup and dataset
structure are given in Section IV-B.
3) System Simulator: Figure 1 shows the modeled signal
flow along a stripe. Only one RU is assumed to be active at
any given time during transmission, while preceding units act
as BUs to compensate insertion losses and fiber attenuation.
The simulator supports gain calibration routines that set
booster gains to maintain a desired power level along the
stripe. The Python simulator integrates the scenario con-
figuration, waveform generation, hardware-aware component
models, and wireless propagation into a single end-to-end
execution flow:
1) Waveform generation: generate baseband symbols
and the time-domain CP-OFDM waveform (including
pilots and oversampling) from the waveform configu-
ration.
2) Stripe and hardware chain: instantiate the stripe
from the configured node locations and apply the
selected component models (fiber/couplers, booster
and antenna-side amplifiers, splitter/combiner, phase
shifters) as the signal propagates from the CU via the
BUs to the active RU.
3) Wireless channel integration: apply the configured
wireless channel model (stochastic/deterministic) or
load precomputed RT channels; for OFDM, the channel
is applied per subcarrier via y[q] = H[q]x[q] (see
Section IV-B).
The detailed model options for the hardware chain and
the wireless channel are described in Table I and Sec-
tion IV-B, respectively. A complete overview of how to
implement a simulation can be found in the examples
folder of the simulator repository. For example, the exam-
ples/stripe ul/example.ipynb notebook shows how to con-
figure and run an uplink simulation and plots intermediate
results as the signal travels along the stripe.
III. HARDWARE-AWARE COMPONENT MODELS
A. Model selection and parameterization
All hardware blocks inherit from a common Component
abstraction that supports multiple modeling modes (e.g.,
ideal, odd-order polynomial nonlinearity, soft limiting,
etc.). Scenario-level YAML files select component models
and parameters; for example, S-parameter files (.s2p) to
define frequency-selective couplers and PMF links, while am-
plifier modes and noise figures are configured independently.
This allows for easy integration of new measurements or
model updates into the simulator framework.
B. Central Unit
The CU generates and processes complex baseband sam-
ples prior to injection into the first fiber segment. In the
current implementation, the main impairments are: (i) DAC
clipping/quantization, (ii) IQ imbalance and DC offset, (iii)
oscillator phase noise and carrier frequency offset (CFO), and
(iv) amplifier nonlinearity and noise. In compact form, the
modeled CU chain can be written as
y = GfPA
␀MIQ
␀Q(x) , ejϕ
,
(1)
where x is a vector of baseband samples, Q(·) models
DAC effects, MIQ(·) applies IQ impairment and mixing by
the oscillator phasor ejϕ, and fPA(·) is the transmit power
amplifier model and G represents the linear gain.
C. Fiber and couplers
Fiber segments and couplers are modeled as linear filters
with frequency selectivity, gain, and delay [1], [2]. When
S-parameters are available, the simulator interpolates the
measured/simulated S21(f) to the OFDM subcarrier grid and
