B. Ray-Tracing Channels using Sionna
To complement stochastic fading, the simulator can load
deterministic, site-specific MIMO channel state information
generated with the NVIDIA Sionna RT engine [11], [12]. In
the simulator, this functionality is provided as a companion
module (“RT module”) that precomputes channels for a
given 3D scene and exports them in a dataset format that the
simulator can query efficiently at runtime. The overall goal
is to make the wireless propagation model consistent with
(i) the room geometry, (ii) the radio-stripe topology, and (iii)
the OFDM numerology used by the waveform layer, while
keeping the computationally heavy ray-tracing step out of the
main simulation loop.
a) Scene import and material modeling:
The RT
pipeline loads an indoor environment from a scene descrip-
tion (XML) and assigns radio materials to the scene objects.
To capture strong frequency dependence at sub-THz, the
material parameters (conductivity and relative permittivity)
are updated as a function of carrier frequency using ITU-
inspired callbacks for common building materials (e.g., glass,
concrete, metal, polystyrene, and MDF) as measured in [13].
This enables the same geometry to be reused for sub-
10 GHz and sub-THz operation with frequency-appropriate
electromagnetic parameters.
b) Transmitter/receiver placement and antenna arrays:
The radio-stripe layout is taken from a stripe configuration
(number of stripes, number of RUs per stripe, inter-RU
and inter-stripe spacing, and a stripe start position). As the
component placement determines the channel and stripe char-
acteristics this configuration is shared between the simulator
and the RT setup. For each RU position, the RT pipeline
instantiates a Sionna Transmitter with a planar antenna
array; the UE is instantiated as a Receiver at a config-
urable set of locations. In the default dataset generation, both
ends use a 1×4 uniform linear array and a single polarization,
which yields a per-subcarrier channel matrix with dimension
(Nrx) × (Ntx), where Nrx = Ntx = 4. A 1 × 4 array
matches the hardware designed in the 6GTandem project
D4.3 [14]. However, this can be adapted to any desired array
configuration.
c) Ray-tracing and frequency response generation: For
each UE location and each RU, the RT pipeline runs Sionna’s
PathSolver with line-of-sight enabled and a finite inter-
action depth (default maximum depth 5), including specular
reflections and refraction while disabling diffuse scattering.
The resulting set of multipath components is converted to a
channel frequency response (CFR) on the subcarrier grid.
The subcarrier frequencies are aligned with the simulator
waveform settings; for the current sub-THz configuration this
corresponds to fc = 157.75 GHz, B = 3.0 GHz and 4096
subcarriers, i.e., the RT output can be applied directly as
y[q] = H[q]x[q] without additional interpolation.
d) Dataset structure and runtime integration: To sup-
port reproducible experiments and fast random access during
simulation, the RT output is stored as NetCDF files. UE
metadata is stored in a separate NetCDF file, allowing for
lightweight querying of users based on their characteristics.
This metadata contains the location coordinates, a user id
and optional metadata indices for locations directly under
a stripe grid. For each UE index, a channel file stores the
complex CFR tensor together with coordinates that map each
transmitter instance to its stripe and RU id. At runtime, the
simulator matches the requested UE coordinates to the UE
index, loads the corresponding channel file, and extracts H[q]
for a given stripe/RU pair on demand.
V. CONFIGURATION AND SIMULATION OUTPUT
The simulation is driven by a set of YAML configuration
files (Section II-B1). Separating scenario, waveform, and
component parameters enables consistent experiments across
environments and straightforward parameter sweeps without
modifying the simulator code.
This supports studying the impact of hardware and wave-
form choice in a specific scenario. Only certain parts of
a stripe can be enabled or bypassed to analyze specific
effects. Instantiating components with an ideal/linear model
forms a reference to compare the hardware-accurate models
against. These realistic models are parametrized by measure-
ments e.g., S-parameters, gain/noise figures, and nonlinear
transfer characteristics. The wireless side supports switching
between deterministic/stochastic channel models and ray-
tracing datasets; pilot density and mode (e.g., scattered
pilots within each OFDM symbol versus dedicated pilot-
only OFDM symbols spanning all subcarriers) determine the
quality of channel estimates, and enable controlled studies
of channel estimation error. Finally, the same workflow can
be repeated for different scenarios changing the environment,
stripe layout, beamforming strategy, and RU selection policy
to quantify geometry- and activation-dependent performance.
To support debugging and quantitative evaluation, the
simulator produces both intermediate and end-to-end out-
puts. It can export the complex baseband waveform at the
input and output of each modeled component, from which
constellation diagrams, AM/AM, and AM/PM characteristics
can be derived. Additionally it reports performance metrics
at the CU such as normalised mean square error (NMSE)
and signal-to-noise-and-distortion ratio (SNDR) as well as bit
error rate (BER) after demodulation. The frequency responses
used by the component models (e.g., S-parameter traces)
are stored alongside the results to facilitate easily adding
new measurements as well as validation and documentation.
Simulation outputs are exported in lightweight formats (e.g.,
CSV) for post-processing and plotting. As an example, Fig. 7
illustrates how nonlinear distortion and noise accumulate
along the stripe via stage-wise AM/AM curves. Figure 8
shows a heatmap of NMSE at the CU when activating each
individual RU, which can directly support beamforming and
RU selection studies.
