Policy and baselines. The classifier is a gradient-boosted machine (GBM, XGBoost [51]) trained
on 16 lightweight text features: lexical statistics (length, word count, sentence count), structural
indicators (question marks, numbers, fractions, multi-step keywords), and a normalised entropy
estimate from a single-pass LLM call. Training uses an 80/20 split with 3 random seeds; all
results report mean ± std. Full feature and hyperparameter details are in Appendix E. We compare
against: (1) Oracle (Lagrangian-optimal allocation; upper bound requiring knowledge of Acc(x, b)),
(2) Fixed-b (uniform budget: largest b ∈B with b ≤B), (3) Random (random budget matching
E[b] = B), and (4) Heuristic (rank by prompt length as difficulty proxy, assign high budgets to
longest prompts).
5.2
Main Results
Table 1 presents task accuracy and realized cost for all methods across four model–dataset pairs and
three budget constraints B ∈{1.5, 2.0, 3.0}.
Our method (AdaCompute-GBM) consistently outperforms all non-oracle baselines across every
model, dataset, and budget level. On MATH, improvements over Fixed-b reach +5.8pp (DeepSeek-
V3), +5.2pp (GPT-4o-mini), and +6.4pp (Qwen2.5-7B) at B = 3.0. Even on GSM8K with 94.6%
base accuracy, adaptive allocation yields +1.9pp. The learned policy often uses less than the full
budget (e.g., cost 2.73 at B = 3.0 for DeepSeek × MATH), confirming efficient identification of
questions that benefit from additional samples. The Heuristic baseline provides only modest gains
(+0.3–1.2pp), confirming that allocation requires a learned non-linear mapping.
5.3
Compute–Accuracy Pareto Frontier
Figure 4 shows the full Pareto frontier across a dense grid of budget constraints B ∈[1, 16]. Several
patterns emerge: (i) our method dominates all baselines at every budget level, (ii) the largest relative
gains occur at intermediate budgets (B = 2–6) where the flexibility to differentially route questions
matters most, (iii) beyond B ≈3, all methods plateau because additional budget cannot help easy or
intractable questions, and (iv) Fixed-b forms a staircase because it can only operate at budget levels
in B.
5.4
Oracle Gap and Allocation Analysis
Across all model–dataset–budget combinations, the learned GBM policy achieves 91–99% imitation
accuracy with task-accuracy gaps of only 0.4–1.4pp (full breakdowns in Appendix F, Table 5). The
gap is small even when imitation is imperfect because misclassifications often assign a “nearby”
budget with similar accuracy, consistent with the Mλ-weighted bound of Theorem 2.
